d849b', 'word_count': 762, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b125e787-47b7-5a98-82c4-0ee177ad849b'}, {'abstract': 'The architect and MacArthur “genius” grantee discusses her collaboration with Swarovski, “Thinning Ice.”', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/02/jeanne-gang-climate-change-crystals-design-miami/', 'snippet': 'The architect and MacArthur “genius” grantee discusses her collaboration with Swarovski, “Thinning Ice.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Design installations are often steeped in commentary about materiality, politics and the environment, but rarely do they tackle these issues head-on. For its ninth annual collaboration at Design Miami, which is opening this\xa0week, Swarovski commissioned the celebrated Chicago-based architect Jeanne Gang to put her own eco-conscious spin on Swarovski crystals. The work, “Thinning Ice,” is shaping up to be the series’s most stirring creation yet — and perhaps its most poignant. Gang teamed with the noted photographer and environmentalist James Balog, who for three years documented the deterioration of the Stubai glacier in the Austrian Alps, and turned that work into the subject of the installation, combining photography, video and a table and chairs created by the architect to evoke glacier-like forms.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/t-magazine/03gang-rubinstein-3/03gang-rubinstein-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/03/t-magazine/03gang-rubinstein-3/03gang-rubinstein-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/t-magazine/03gang-rubinstein-3/03gang-rubinstein-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank':