'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-02T23:28:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/829e56f5-6d27-51ba-9668-30182427a9fd', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/829e56f5-6d27-51ba-9668-30182427a9fd'}, {'abstract': 'The Mediterranean diet is associated with longer telomeres, the protective structures at the end of chromosomes that are tied to longevity and age-related illness.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/02/mediterranean-diet-is-good-for-your-dna/', 'snippet': 'The Mediterranean diet is associated with longer telomeres, the protective structures at the end of chromosomes that are tied to longevity and age-related illness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Mediterranean diet — higher in vegetables, fruits, whole grains and olive oil, and lower in dairy products and meat — has long been cited for its health-promoting benefits. Researchers have new clues as to why.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/07/health/well-nutrition/well-nutrition-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 232, 'width': 229, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/07/health/well-nutrition/well-nutrition-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 232, 'width': 229, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/07/health/well-nutrition/well-nutrition-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width':