 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e35f3684-950f-5c52-b17d-da72ebbc9dc7', 'word_count': 792, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e35f3684-950f-5c52-b17d-da72ebbc9dc7'}, {'abstract': 'Supporters of Mayor Bill de Blasio’s plan to expand supervised release and treatment programs said one key was to identify troubled offenders early.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/03/nyregion/new-york-citys-effort-to-divert-the-mentally-ill-from-jails-revolving-door.html', 'snippet': 'Supporters of Mayor Bill de Blasio’s plan to expand supervised release and treatment programs said one key was to identify troubled offenders early.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is hard to walk the halls of the Criminal Courts Building in Manhattan without encountering stories of crimes committed by people with mental illnesses. On Monday, two separate murder cases were being tried against men who had presented evidence they were psychotic when they stabbed their victims to death.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Plan Aims to Divert Mentally Ill People From Jail’s Revolving Door', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mayoral Plan Aims to Divert Mentally Ill People From Jail’s Revolving Door', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T03:14:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5df875fa-ebd6-5154-abeb-b871153bda8f', 'word_count': 874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5df875fa-ebd6-5154-abeb-b871153bda8f'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Haruf’s breakthrough novel was “Plainsong,” which was nominated for the National Book Award.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/03/books/kent-haruf-sublime-novelist-of-small-town-life-dies-at-71.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Haruf’s breakthrough novel was “Plainsong,” which was nominated for the National Book Award.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kent Haruf pulled a wool cap over his eyes when he sat down at his manual typewriter each morning so he could “write blind,” fully immersing himself in the fictitious small town in eastern Colorado where he set a series of quiet, acclaimed novels, including “Plainsong,” a 1999 best seller. Mr. Haruf often wrote a chapter a day, most recently in a prefabricated shed in the backyard of his home in Salida, Colo., where he died on Sunday at 71. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/arts/HARUF-1-obit/HARUF-1-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 469, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/03/arts/HARUF-1-obit/HARUF-1-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 469}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': 