tps://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/03/will-wearable-technology-ever-really-catch-on/', 'snippet': 'Would you want a pair of Google’s computer glasses? Would you want an Apple Watch? Is wearable technology something you’re ready to embrace? Why?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Google rolled out a test edition of its much hyped Google Glass in spring 2013, and this September Apple announced the coming of Apple Watch. But while the public waits for a general consumer release of these products, other companies, like Samsung and Sony, already have smartwatches on the market. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/26/opinion/rfdgoogleglass-LN/rfdgoogleglass-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 412, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/26/opinion/rfdgoogleglass-LN/rfdgoogleglass-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 412}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/26/opinion/rfdgoogleglass-LN/rfdgoogleglass-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 703, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/26/opinion/rfdgoogleglass-LN/rfdgoogleglass-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1406, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/26/opinion/rfdgoogleglass-LN/rfdgoogleglass-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/26/opinion/rfdgoogleglass-LN/rfdgoogleglass-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/26/opinion/rfdgoogleglass-LN/rfdgoogleglass-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Wearable Technology Ever Really Catch On?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T10:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ead6a461-0d03-5b12-ab05-f67e45776b9e', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ead6a461-0d03-5b12-ab05-f67e45776b9e'}, {'abstract': 'A major exhibit of the New York Public Library’s vast photo collection is a reminder that photography has always been a social medium.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/03/sharing-and-liking-photos-from-day-one/', 'snippet': 'A major exhibit of the New York Public Library’s vast photo collection is a reminder that photography has always been a social medium.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With more than almost 5 million photos, the New York Public Library boasts a wide-ranging collection that would be the envy of any museum: from 19th century cyanotypes of algae, handcrafted scrapbooks and cartes-de-visite, to classic Farm Security Administration photos, movie location pictures and contemporary digital art.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sharing and Liking Photos From Day 1', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T10:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_nam