on their tenure. The top bonuses being doled out by law firms are roughly $40,000 more than most big New York firms handed out to their most senior associates last year. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Big Law Bolsters Bonuses', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T12:40:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0588788c-be8c-5beb-9431-c5299ae104b9', 'word_count': 2141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0588788c-be8c-5beb-9431-c5299ae104b9'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: 1939: Princess Louise Dies in London, Oldest Surviving Child of Queen Victoria.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/03/1939-princess-louise-dies-in-london/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: 1939: Princess Louise Dies in London, Oldest Surviving Child of Queen Victoria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Louise, oldest surviving child of Queen Victoria and oldest member of the British royal family, died at Kensington Palace at the age of ninety-one years today. At the outbreak of war last September, Princess Louise refused to leave London, declaring that she would stay there even if bombs rained on the city. At Kensington Palace, where she lived alone except for her old servants, there were no sandbags and no gummed paper on the windows. The princess merely had dark blue blinds that had seen service in the World War put up for the black-outs, while an underground tunnel near the kitchens served as an air-raid shelter. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Dec. 4, 1939', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/04/blogs/04iht-retrospective-Louise/04iht-retrospective-Louise-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 877, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/04/blogs/04iht-retrospective-Louise/04iht-retrospective-Louise-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 877}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/04/blogs/04iht-retrospective-Louise/04iht-retrospective-Louise-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 701, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/04/blogs/04iht-retrospective-Louise/04iht-retrospective-Louise-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1026, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/04/blogs/04iht-retrospective-Louise/04iht-retrospective-Louise-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/04/blogs/04iht-retrospective-Louise/04iht-retrospective-Louise-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/04/blogs/04iht-retrospective-Louise/04iht-retrospective-Louise-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '1939: Princess Louise Dies in London', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'na