oments in “Wild,” featuring Reese Witherspoon and Mo McRae. The film is based on the memoir by Cheryl Strayed, about her over 1,000 hike along the Pacific Crest National Scenic Trail. Here, she takes a detour from the trail because of snowfall and tries to hitch a ride, with comic results.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jean-Marc Vallée Narrates a Scene From ‘Wild’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strayed, Cheryl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vallee, Jean-Marc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Witherspoon, Reese', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T15:30:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/365c7b53-f6a1-5677-b99c-08ce0363df2f', 'word_count': 63, 'uri': 'nyt://article/365c7b53-f6a1-5677-b99c-08ce0363df2f'}, {'abstract': 'Bruce Jay Friedman reviews an exhaustively researched biography of the comedian Bob Hope.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/hope-entertainer-of-the-century-by-richard-zoglin.html', 'snippet': 'Bruce Jay Friedman reviews an exhaustively researched biography of the comedian Bob Hope.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For all his brilliance, it’s hard to imagine a Steve Martin Airport. Yet there is a Bob Hope Airport in Burbank, Calif., a postage stamp with his likeness and streets all across America named after the comedian. The Navy called one of its ships the Bob Hope. (A Gilbert Gottfried naval destroyer? Sorry, babe, I don’t think so.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07FRIEDMAN/07FRIEDMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 503, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07FRIEDMAN/07FRIEDMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 503}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07FRIEDMAN/07FRIEDMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 859, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07FRIEDMAN/07FRIEDMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1718, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07FRIEDMAN/07FRIEDMAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07FRIEDMAN/07FRIEDMAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07FRIEDMAN/07FRIEDMAN-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Hope: Entertainer of the Century,’ by Richard Zoglin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Thanks for the Memory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zoglin, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hope, Bob', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Hope: Entertainer of the Century (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T15:31:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Jay Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': 'Jay', 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/10d73304-26b4-513f-b8ef-0df771d67c5f', 'word_count': 1163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10d73304-26b4-513f-b8ef-0df771d67c5f'}, {'abstract': 'Girls’ social aggression gets a lot of attention, but new research suggests the problem may be even bigger in boys.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/03/what-grown-ups-are-missing-about-boys/', 'snippet': 'Girls’ social aggression gets a lot of attention, but new research suggests the problem may be even bigger in boys.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it comes to mean or destructive social behavior, Pamela Orpinas says “so much of the research has focused on girls.” “We’ve had papers and books and qualitative analyses and focus groups” about the ways girls mistreat one another socially, the professor of health promotion and behavior told Op-Talk, “but we don’t know that much about boys.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/02/opinion/nyto-aggression/nyto-aggression-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/02/opinion/nyto-aggression/nyto-aggression-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 588}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/02/opinion/nyto-aggression/nyto-aggression-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1003, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/02/opinion/nyto-aggression/nyto-aggression-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 2041, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/02/opinion/nyto-aggression/nyto-aggression-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/02/opinion/nyto-aggression/nyto-aggression-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/02/opinion/nyto-aggression/nyto-aggression-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Grown-Ups Are Missing About Boys', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T15:31:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna North', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'North', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfce8641-5d7a-522a-ab46-8cdf1a585988', 'word_count': 585, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfce8641-5d7a-522a-ab46-8cdf1a585988'}, {'abstract': 'Stephen King reviews Rick Bragg’s new book about the rock ‘n’ roll pioneer Jerry Lee Lewis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/jerry-lee-lewis-his-own-story-by-rick-bragg.html', 'snippet': 'Stephen King reviews Rick Bragg’s new book about the rock ‘n’ roll pioneer Jerry Lee Lewis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite the title of Jerry Lee Lewis’s 2006 album “Last Man Standing,” a few other survivors from rock’s early days still remain. Chuck Berry, an old nemesis, is still around. So are Little Richard, Fats Domino and Wanda Jackson, the let’s-have-a-party girl who briefly dated Elvis Presley and shouted her own version of the Lewis anthem “Whole Lotta Shakin’ Goin’ On.” Still, Lewis is the summation of that early period, before cleaner-cut teen idols like Frankie Avalon and Fabian came on the scene. In Tolkien parlance, he’s the one ring that binds them, and as such, he deserves a rich and textured biography. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07king-sub/07king-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07king-sub/07king-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 734}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07king-sub/07king-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 960, 'width': 785, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07king-sub/07king-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 960, 'width': 785, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07king-sub/07king-sub-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07king-sub/07king-sub-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07king-sub/07king-sub-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Jerry Lee Lewis: His Own Story,’ by Rick Bragg', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Whole Lotta Shakin'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Stephen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bragg, Rick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lewis, Jerry Lee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T15:32:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen King', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'King', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/88f88476-ba2a-5aa1-8f04-7f4fd2adb585', 'word_count': 1397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88f88476-ba2a-5aa1-8f04-7f4fd2adb585'}, {'abstract': 'In a book review of “Magic Words,” Jonathan Knee examines the work of a professional magician-mentalist turned corporate motivational speaker who provides tools to help lawyers, bankers, consultants and others close deals.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/12/03/and-for-his-next-trick-getting-people-to-do-what-you-want/', 'snippet': 'In a book review of “Magic Words,” Jonathan Knee examines the work of a professional magician-mentalist turned corporate motivational speaker who provides tools to help lawyers, bankers, consultants and others close deals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nothing is more likely to annoy investment bankers, corporate lawyers, management consultants and other service professionals than to suggest that they are salespeople. They did not survive graduate school and toil late nights of apprenticeship honing their craft to be considered mere peddlers. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/business/dbpix-magic-words2/dbpix-magic-words2-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/03/business/dbpix-magic-words2/dbpix-magic-words2-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/business/dbpix-magic-words2/dbpix-magic-words2-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/business/dbpix-magic-words2/dbpix-magic-words2-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 981, 'width': 1472, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/business/dbpix-magic-words2/dbpix-magic-words-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/03/business/dbpix-magic-words2/dbpix-magic-words-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/business/dbpix-magic-words2/dbpix-magic-words-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Tim David’s Next Trick, ‘Magic Words’ That Get People to Do What You Want', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magic and Magicians', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'David, Tim', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T15:37:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan A. Knee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Knee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68d67130-4d59-5f9e-8d8a-5d3d9ff00707', 'word_count': 970, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68d67130-4d59-5f9e-8d8a-5d3d9ff00707'}, {'abstract': 'Touré reviews Greil Marcus’s new book about songs that have been interpreted multiple times by artists in different generations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/greil-marcuss-history-of-rock-n-roll-in-ten-songs.html', 'snippet': 'Touré reviews Greil Marcus’s new book about songs that have been interpreted multiple times by artists in different generations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every great, enduring rock song is like a cell in our cultural memory. A molecule of our shared experience containing not just an incredible performance but also a shared desire for something — love, money, sex, peace, rebellion, power, freedom — some intensely held desire. But is a great, enduring rock song powered most by the song or the performance? Are we swooning because the song is so perfectly written that we can’t help singing along? Or is it the rendition that etches it into our minds? Or is it both — and more — since every song is a Proustian madeleine that evokes all our memories of the song and of the people we celebrated it with? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '51', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TOURE/07TOURE-1416527241893-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TOURE/07TOURE-1416527241893-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TOURE/07TOURE-1416527241893-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TOURE/07TOURE-1416527241893-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1377, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TOURE/07TOURE-1416527241893-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TOURE/07TOURE-1416527241893-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TOURE/07TOURE-1416527241893-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Greil Marcus’s ‘History of Rock ‘n’ Roll in Ten Songs’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Singer and the Song', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Toure', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marcus, Greil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T15:38:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Touré', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Touré', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e53fe73-3980-5765-95c9-9c975a26d5d9', 'word_count': 1372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e53fe73-3980-5765-95c9-9c975a26d5d9'}, {'abstract': 'Some Times readers took issue with calling the 18-year-old what he was.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/03/was-michael-brown-a-teenager/', 'snippet': 'Some Times readers took issue with calling the 18-year-old what he was.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I wasn’t surprised by the—shall we say—vibrant comment section on a blog post I wrote highlighting Officer Darren Wilson’s description of shooting Michael Brown. After all, the events surrounding and following the shooting have been contentious.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Was Michael Brown a ‘Teenager’?', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Darren (1986- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T15:43:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39f1001a-b6e8-5332-955a-42c88f0339b8', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39f1001a-b6e8-5332-955a-42c88f0339b8'}, {'abstract': 'P. J. O’Rourke reviews an expanded edition of H. L. Mencken’s memoirs, a full picture of the man and his contradictions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/h-l-menckens-days-trilogy-expanded-edition.html', 'snippet': 'P. J. O’Rourke reviews an expanded edition of H. L. Mencken’s memoirs, a full picture of the man and his contradictions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If we exclude Mark Twain, whose reminiscences suffered the rapine of fiction and whose attempt at autobiography is a mess, the three best memoir writers in American literature are H. L. Mencken.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '49', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07OROURKE/07OROURKE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 867, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07OROURKE/07OROURKE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 867}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07OROURKE/07OROURKE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07OROURKE/07OROURKE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1418, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07OROURKE/07OROURKE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07OROURKE/07OROURKE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07OROURKE/07OROURKE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'H. L. Mencken’s ‘Days Trilogy: Expanded Edition’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Skeptic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mencken, H L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-03T15:48:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': "By P. J. O'Rourke", 'person': [{'firstname': 'P.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': "O'Rourke", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9641327a-2651-55d8-9cc2-b22e5c5875e6', 'word_count': 1332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9641327a-2651-55d8-9cc2-b22e5c5875e6'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Ian Black reviews a memoir by John Cleese, one of comedy’s most original minds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/so-anyway-a-memoir-by-john-cleese.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Ian Black reviews a memoir by John Cleese, one of comedy’s most original minds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Full disclosure: My reverence for Monty Python is nearly boundless. After I was force-fed a diet of mediocrity and cringe-worthy catchphrases from “Saturday Night Live” during my youth, discovering Monty Python’s freewheeling, surreal style felt like a revelation. You mean, sketches could just end? You mean, things didn’t need to make sense? You mean, you didn’t have to make fun of whatever celebrities were in the news right that very second? Why hadn’t anybody told me this before? I began watching them only years after they disbanded, but their comedy still felt fresher than anything I’d been exposed to. Even the name of the show, “Monty Python’s Flying Circus,” felt wild and anarchic, a dipsy collision of unrelated words more akin to Lewis Carroll than Lorne Michaels. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '59', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BLACK/07BLACK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 826, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BLACK/07BLACK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 826}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BLACK/07BLACK-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 744, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BLACK/07BLACK-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1595, 'width': 1158, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BLACK/07BLACK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BLACK/07BLACK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75