Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Rubinstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubinstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b976dd39-c84c-5257-ae74-70f5e01b581b', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b976dd39-c84c-5257-ae74-70f5e01b581b'}, {'abstract': 'The patch of dirt may have been covered in weeds and candy wrappers, but it was mine.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/03/a-gentrification-story/', 'snippet': 'The patch of dirt may have been covered in weeds and candy wrappers, but it was mine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before purchasing a house in Brooklyn, I did not know the term “tree pit.” All I knew was that our house came with a small square plot of dirt out front, with a skinny, scraggly tree, and that it was up to me to maintain or ignore as I saw fit. At first I attended to it. I dug shovelfuls of dirt out of the pit. I weeded and plucked out the city detritus — rubber bands, candy bar wrappers and always more cigarette butts. Then I filled the hole with freshly purchased dirt and planted around the tree six low vinelike plants that the woman at the gardening store had said were good for ground cover. When I was done I admired my handiwork and immediately went inside to sterilize my hands.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/04/opinion/1204PRIVATELIVES/1204PRIVATELIVES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/04/opinion/1204PRIVATELIVES/1204PRIVATELIVES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 737}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/1