ipriani Wall Street', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-04T00:02:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Koblin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koblin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/985d65bf-0082-503c-ba85-3275a069c605', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/985d65bf-0082-503c-ba85-3275a069c605'}, {'abstract': 'Just as breast-feeding is the best nutrition for my son, being a college-educated mom is one of the best things I can do for my son’s future. These facts go together in my mind, and in social science, yet they fail to come together on my campus.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/03/a-young-mother-feeling-unwelcome-on-campus/', 'snippet': 'Just as breast-feeding is the best nutrition for my son, being a college-educated mom is one of the best things I can do for my son’s future. These facts go together in my mind, and in social science, yet they fail to come together on my campus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week at my university I counted 10 students and one professor who had the opportunity to see my breasts. No, I was not drunk on campus, pulling a “Girls Gone Wild,” nor am I an anatomy major. I am simply a student and a mother and I pump milk for my 10-month-old son, Louie, five days a week in a public restroom on campus.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/us/motherlode-college-mother/motherlode-college-mother-articleLarge.png', 'height': 282, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/03/us/motherlode-college-mother/motherlode-college-mother-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeh