ts', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Davis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a632c6b5-dc13-566e-9c4b-775f8deacb5f', 'word_count': 1054, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a632c6b5-dc13-566e-9c4b-775f8deacb5f'}, {'abstract': 'New research has uncovered the remarkable sophistication with which electric eels deploy their shocks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/04/science/the-surprising-power-of-an-electric-eels-shock.html', 'snippet': 'New research has uncovered the remarkable sophistication with which electric eels deploy their shocks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For thousands of years, fishermen knew that certain fish could deliver a painful shock, even though they had no idea how it happened. Only in the late 1700s did naturalists contemplate a bizarre possibility: These fish might release jolts of electricity — the same mysterious substance as in lightning.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/04/science/04zimmer/04zimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/04/science/04zimmer/04zimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 642}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/04/science/04zimmer/04zimmer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 957, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/04/science/04zimmer/04zimmer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1914, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 