erial': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5e108f1-fe8e-51dd-9b85-c2457503c9a9', 'word_count': 40, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5e108f1-fe8e-51dd-9b85-c2457503c9a9'}, {'abstract': 'The shooting death of an unarmed black man by a white police officer here on Tuesday has added new fuel to the already fiery debate over the role of race in the police use of lethal force.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/06/us/phoenix-officer-who-shot-black-suspect-mistook-pill-bottle-for-gun-authorities-say.html', 'snippet': 'The shooting death of an unarmed black man by a white police officer here on Tuesday has added new fuel to the already fiery debate over the role of race in the police use of lethal force.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHOENIX — The shooting death of an unarmed black man by a white police officer here on Tuesday has added new fuel to the already fiery debate over the role of race in the police use of lethal force.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Police Shooting in Phoenix Fuels Protesters’ Outrage ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Police Shooting in Phoenix Fuels Protesters’ Outrage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brisbon, Rumain (1980-2014)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Phoenix (Ariz)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maricopa County (Ariz)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn for the Advancement of Colored People', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T16:03:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebekah Zemansky and Tamar Lewin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebekah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zemansky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Tamar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40189ad9-05ca-51fa-9fae-ac3b357106a8', 'word_count': 907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40189ad9-05ca-51fa-9fae-ac3b357106a8'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FLYOVER LIVES: A Memoir,  by Diane Johnson. (Plume, $16.) The widely traveled author of the popular novel “Le Divorce,” among other books about Americans abroad, probes her Midwestern roots in this elegiac memoir. From the Friday night football of her youth in Illinois to the adventures illuminated in the letters and diaries of her stalwart pioneer ancestors, Johnson seeks to understand the people and geography that shaped her character.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '82', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T16:10:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/00bc9bfb-8f35-5032-b9ca-3f4c1ae7855e', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00bc9bfb-8f35-5032-b9ca-3f4c1ae7855e'}, {'abstract': 'A selected, critical guide to dance performances in New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/05/arts/dance/dance-listings-for-dec-5-11.html', 'snippet': 'A selected, critical guide to dance performances in New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater (through Jan. 4) If any holiday dance tradition can rival “The Nutcracker,” it’s the Ailey company’s winter season at City Center. This year’s five-week run continues with two world premieres: First up, on Sunday, is “The Pleasure of the Lesson,” Robert Moses’s seductive dance for 10; it’s followed on Wednesday by “Odetta,” Matthew Rushing’s tribute to the singer and civil rights activist. Tuesdays through Thursdays at 7:30 p.m., Fridays at 8 p.m., Saturdays at 2 and 8 p.m., Sundays at 3 and 7:30 p.m., 131 West 55th Street, Manhattan, 212-581-1212, nycitycenter.org, alvinailey.org; $25 to $150. (Siobhan Burke)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dance Listings for Dec. 5-11', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T16:13:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/b21234d2-a641-508b-b9ee-9ee4ef289339', 'word_count': 1067, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b21234d2-a641-508b-b9ee-9ee4ef289339'}, {'abstract': 'A new biography of Isabella of Castile, the driving force of 15th-century Spanish — and therefore European — politics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/kirstin-downeys-isabella-the-warrior-queen.html', 'snippet': 'A new biography of Isabella of Castile, the driving force of 15th-century Spanish — and therefore European — politics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While Ferdinand of Aragon has always gotten first billing, Isabella of Castile was the driving force of 15th-century Spanish — and therefore European — politics. “Isabella: The Warrior Queen” follows Kirstin Downey’s 2009 “The Woman Behind the New Deal: The Life and Legacy of Frances Perkins — Social Security, Unemployment Insurance, and the Minimum Wage,” and if so immensely provocative a figure as Isabella seems an unlikely successor to Franklin Roosevelt’s secretary of labor, who quietly worked out of the public eye, the two women successfully maneuvered in an almost exclusively male world of politics. In fact, Downey credits Isabella with instituting a “shift from medieval to modern management principles.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HARRISON/07HARRISON-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 705, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HARRISON/07HARRISON-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 705}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HARRISON/07HARRISON-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 872, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HARRISON/07HARRISON-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1738, 'width': 1479, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HARRISON/07HARRISON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HARRISON/07HARRISON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HARRISON/07HARRISON-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kirstin Downey’s ‘Isabella: The Warrior Queen’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Power Play', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Downey, Kirstin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T16:18:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Harrison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harrison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/44554f2b-8ec6-51f6-9a61-ba5efe4cbb24', 'word_count': 873, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44554f2b-8ec6-51f6-9a61-ba5efe4cbb24'}, {'abstract': 'Geoff Dyer reviews the second volume of Ian Bell’s biography of Bob Dylan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/time-out-of-mind-the-lives-of-bob-dylan-by-ian-bell.html', 'snippet': 'Geoff Dyer reviews the second volume of Ian Bell’s biography of Bob Dylan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People tend to get into Bob Dylan, often very seriously, in their late teens or early 20s. The particular entry point varies according to when the listener comes of Dylanological age. It might have been the Greenwich Village folk scene of the late 1950s, the post-Newport electric period or “Blood on the Tracks.” It could even be the resurgent late phase from “Time Out of Mind” onward. The flip side of this is that at some point people lose interest in Dylan. For every entry point, there’s an equally convenient exit. Loss of interest rarely proves terminal, however, especially since 1991, when his continuing recording career began to proceed in tandem with a slow drip of “official” bootleg releases (most recently: “The Basement Tapes Complete”).', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DYER1/07DYER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 476, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DYER1/07DYER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 476}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DYER1/07DYER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 812, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DYER1/07DYER1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1625, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DYER1/07DYER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DYER1/07DYER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DYER1/07DYER1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLa