05T17:27:40+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/8876683a-d502-5e8b-a4e8-3831e1115fa1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/8876683a-d502-5e8b-a4e8-3831e1115fa1'}, {'abstract': 'Starbucks plans a new chain of coffee shops for those who thought Starbucks coffee was too inexpensive. Four studies show that the Affordable Care Act is working “incredibly well.” And here is how to deal with a toxic employee.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/05/today-in-small-business-the-best-jobs-numbers-in-years/', 'snippet': 'Starbucks plans a new chain of coffee shops for those who thought Starbucks coffee was too inexpensive. Four studies show that the Affordable Care Act is working “incredibly well.” And here is how to deal with a toxic employee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s affecting me, my clients and other small-business owners today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Today in Small Business: The Best Jobs Numbers in Years?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T17:28:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gene Marks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/135fa488-a1bb-5450-8ad8-4b7c70d6d80e', 'word_count': 406, 'uri': 'nyt://article/135fa488-a1bb-5450-8ad8-4b7c70d6d80e'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Plagens offers the fullest survey yet of the protean artist Bruce Nauman’s work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/bruce-nauman-the-true-artist-by-peter-plagens.html', 'snippet': 'Peter Plagens offers the fullest survey yet of the protean artist Bruce Nauman’s work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As one of the contemporary art world’s pre-eminent jesters, Bruce Nauman is hardly a barrel of laughs; known as much for his deadpan wit as for his dire take on mortality, his art engages bleak themes (the failure of language; the body’s betrayals; the repetitive, claustrophobic nature of daily life) even as it sparks a knowing, gallows grimace. How else to react to, say, “Sex and Death/Double ‘69,’ ” one of his trademark neon sculptures, which arrays four figures of indeterminate gender in an arrangement (two hang down between two standing) felicitous for simultaneous oral and genital copulation by both pairs? The iconography may owe a debt to high school bathrooms, but the tension between the pulsing colors and the matter-of-fact postures of these doleful sybarites evokes the title’s universal and enduring linkage, as well as the more particular moment of its creation at the beginning of the AIDS crisis in 1985. The scary sense that the core of our human enterprise may be nothing more than a garish amusement park diversion feels inescapable — and as such we are invited to grin and bear it.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '63', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07MOBILIO/07MOBILIO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 552, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07MOBILIO/07MOBILIO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 552}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07MOBILIO/07MOBILIO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 942, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07MOBILIO/07MOBILIO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1884, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07MOBILIO/07MOBILIO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07MOBILIO/07MOBILIO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07MOBILIO/07MOBILIO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Bruce Nauman: The True Artist,’ by Peter Plagens', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Provocateur', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Plagens, Peter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nauman, Bruce', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T17:31:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Albert Mobilio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Albert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mobilio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/45dfa729-e803-523c-98d7-a760aa8cbcbe', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45dfa729-e803-523c-98d7-a760aa8cbcbe'}, {'abstract': 'A hot punch, set aflame if you’d like, warms up winter parties. (Article plus video.)', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/10/dining/raise-the-punch-bowl-thermostat.html', 'snippet': 'A hot punch, set aflame if you’d like, warms up winter parties. (Article plus video.)', 'lead_paragraph': 'Your typical party punch is all well and good when the weather is fine. But in winter, when there’s a damp chill no layering of sweaters can defeat, a hot rum punch is what you want at a holiday celebration. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Rum Punch That Raises the Thermostat', 'kicker': 'A Good Appetite', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Raise the Punch Bowl Thermostat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Melissa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citrus Fruits', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T17:32:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bae0864-f7a0-59ca-9b47-668f7b043a93', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bae0864-f7a0-59ca-9b47-668f7b043a93'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'STALIN: Volume I: Paradoxes of Power, 1878-1928, by Stephen Kotkin.  (Penguin Press, $40.) In this riveting account, written with pace and aplomb, a Princeton professor takes pains to debunk some of the myths.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '80', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T17:32:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/09b2bbe6-a003-5876-9adb-12ce8c941848', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09b2bbe6-a003-5876-9adb-12ce8c941848'}, {'abstract': 'One morning, my daughter found a scarf in my purse and declared, “Mama, let me do your scarf so you can look Muslim.” She said this in the same way you might say, “Let me do your hair so you can look beautiful.”', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/05/mama-let-me-do-your-scarf-so-you-can-look-muslim/', 'snippet': 'One morning, my daughter found a scarf in my purse and declared, “Mama, let me do your scarf so you can look Muslim.” She said this in the same way you might say, “Let me do your hair so you can look beautiful.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'My 6-year-old daughter loves headscarves. In preschool she went through a phase where she color-coordinated them with her outfits while getting dressed. This is my same daughter whose most frequent proclamation when we view her vast dress collection in the morning is, “Nope, not fancy enough!”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images