ction_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/82151dee-c8e9-577a-b5b2-2eb21e0458f1', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82151dee-c8e9-577a-b5b2-2eb21e0458f1'}, {'abstract': 'An artisan perfumer urges us to “regain an authentic connection to the sense of smell.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/fragrant-by-mandy-aftel.html', 'snippet': 'An artisan perfumer urges us to “regain an authentic connection to the sense of smell.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why do we like our chewing gum to taste of mint? The herb grows abundantly on nearly all continents and has long been used as a curative for a variety of conditions. The Japanese once considered mint an effective form of birth control. In the Trobriand Islands, it served as a love charm. The ancient Greeks thought it could end hiccups. And the Romans believed a crown of fresh mint placed atop one’s head would serve to stimulate the mind. In several cultures, mint was recommended as an aid to calm upset stomachs, a practice that explains the longstanding tradition of serving after-dinner mints. But perhaps the crisp, bright smell of mint and the cooling sensation of menthol in the mouth are what make it so refreshing to the palate. When, in the 1890s, William Wrigley Jr. added chewing gum to his line of soaps and baking powder, he tried the brilliant idea of mixing spearmint and peppermint oils with the chicle he was importing from Latin America. Soon his gum was a hit — and a minty-fresh flavor industry was born. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Fragrant,’ by Mandy Aftel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Perfumery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AFTEL, MANDY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smell (Olfaction)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thessaly La Force', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thessaly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Force', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/decc735d-1cc3-581c-81e8-8bac4740d2fb', 'word_count': 674, 'uri': 'nyt://article/decc735d-1cc3-581c-81e8-8bac4740d2fb'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of the singer Peggy Lee.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/is-that-all-there-is-a-peggy-lee-biography.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of the singer Peggy Lee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Peggy Lee had the quintessential unhappy childhood in small-town North Dakota: dead mother (when Lee, then Norma Deloris Egstrom, was 4), alcoholic father, wicked stepmother (who started out as the stern housekeeper) and two devastating house fires in six years. (Lee didn’t write the lyric “Is that all there is to a fire?” but every time she sang it, she certainly knew the answer.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '43', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB-07GATES/SUB-07GATES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 777, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB-07GATES/SUB-07GATES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 777}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB-07GATES/SUB-07GATES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 791, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB-07GATES/SUB-07GATES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1582, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB-07GATES/SUB-07GATES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB-07GATES/SUB-07GATES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB-07GATES/SUB-07GATES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Is That All There Is?,’ a Peggy Lee Biography', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Miss Peggy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Anita', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gavin, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Peggy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anita Gates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/01a6d611-e42e-54d6-b258-5ad695be5363', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01a6d611-e42e-54d6-b258-5ad695be5363'}, {'abstract': 'New books by Bob Odenkirk, Mallory Ortberg and others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/bob-odenkirks-a-load-of-hooey-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books by Bob Odenkirk, Mallory Ortberg and others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most art doesn’t command attention past a generation or two, because most art isn’t very good. Nonetheless, it sometimes feels as if comedy in any guise — written, visual, staged, filmed — is the most evanescent art form of all, especially given the binary response it provokes, either amusing you or not. A book or painting or drama that no longer speaks to us can be shrugged off, but an unfunny joke grates — comedy might be dismissed by some as a lower art form, but in a weird way we hold it to a higher standard. Thus comic strips or stand-up routines that made Grandma and Grandpa laugh can strike the present day as not just dated but downright embarrassing or even, depending on the butt of a joke, shameful; whatever wit an old laugh line possessed can curdle across decades to the point that its rank mirthlessness becomes a kind of KICK ME sign taped to the past’s back. Think of punning he-she jokes from the pre-Henry Luce Life magazine, or racist shtick out of a golden-age Hollywood movie, or the fact that “The Beverly Hillbillies” was once the top-rated show on television — and cringe for our ancestors.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '64', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-HANDY/1207-bks-HANDY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-HANDY/1207-bks-HANDY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-HANDY/1207-bks-HANDY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-HANDY/1207-bks-HANDY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 1199, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-HANDY/1207-bks-HANDY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-HANDY/1207-bks-HANDY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-HANDY/1207-bks-HANDY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Humor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Humor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Handy, Bruce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Handy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Handy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/29fa40d3-9883-50a3-88f4-20a69689453d', 'word_count': 2555, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29fa40d3-9883-50a3-88f4-20a69689453d'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of narratives about meaningful pieces of used clothing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/worn-stories-by-emily-spivack.html', 'snippet': 'A collection of narratives about meaningful pieces of used clothing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is one account in “Worn Stories,” a collection of almost 70 narratives about used clothing, that is so devastating and remarkable it makes all the others fade by comparison. It is offered by Dorothy \xadFinger, whose Jewish parents owned a small department store in what was then Poland and were killed during the Holocaust. Dorothy survived a gunshot and \xadtyphus; nearly froze and starved. After she was liberated by Russians and traveled (on a ship that almost capsized) to relatives in Wilmington, Del., she had a skirt suit custom-made with wool that had been salvaged from the department store — an incalculably precious memento of her early life — but wore it only a few times. “I didn’t love how it fit,” she tells the author, Emily Spivack.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '57', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07JACOBS/07JACOBS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1002, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07JACOBS/07JACOBS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1002}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07JACOBS/07JACOBS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 614, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07JACOBS/07JACOBS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1299, 'width': 778, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07JACOBS/07JACOBS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07JACOBS/07JACOBS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07JACOBS/07JACOBS-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Worn Stories,’ by Emily Spivack', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Threads', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spivack, Emily', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Worn Stories (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ccf15e4-d5ac-51a1-980f-49124c3369a8', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ccf15e4-d5ac-51a1-980f-49124c3369a8'}, {'abstract': 'Three holiday books offer different perspectives on seasonal adventures, from the German front lines to a lonely iceberg.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/eric-a-kimmels-simon-and-the-bear-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Three holiday books offer different perspectives on seasonal adventures, from the German front lines to a lonely iceberg.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The holidays are so unpredictable. Some years you’re stuck on an iceberg with your menorah; some years you’re cowering in a muddy border trench facing German soldiers; and some years you’re kidnapped by a vaguely demonic wooden horse named Trott-trott. You just never know. So it makes sense that three new holiday books offer very different perspectives on wintertime adventures.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07KIDS1/07KIDS1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 331, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07KIDS1/07KIDS1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 331}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07KIDS1/07KIDS1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 565, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07KIDS1/07KIDS1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1130, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07KIDS1/07KIDS1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07KIDS1/07KIDS1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07KIDS1/07KIDS1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Eric A. Kimmel’s ‘Simon and the Bear,’ and More', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wintry Mix', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ingall, Marjorie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kimmel, Eric A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trueman, Matthew', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daniel, Noel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hendrix, John (Illustrator)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marjorie Ingall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marjorie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ingall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f4e610b-01cb-55f3-902d-f7cdcc26883f', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f4e610b-01cb-55f3-902d-f7cdcc26883f'}, {'abstract': 'An outré reading list for the holiday season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/frances-larsons-severed-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'An outré reading list for the holiday season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s talk books. No, not the books on your gift list or the ones you hope to find under the tree. Let’s talk about guilty pleasures, the mysteries you don’t want to be caught reading because they seem too silly, sentimental or strange for someone of your refined tastes. But in the spirit of the holidays, I’ll be happy to share the books on my own outré reading list.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '83', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-CRIME/1207-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 499, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-CRIME/1207-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 499}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-CRIME/1207-bks-CRIME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 852, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-CRIME/1207-bks-CRIME-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1663, 'width': 1999, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-CRIME/1207-bks-CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-CRIME/1207-bks-CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-CRIME/1207-bks-CRIME-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Losing Our Heads', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Losing Our Heads', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Rita Mae', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, Rebecca (1970- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Larson, Frances', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hitchman, Beatrice', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fowler, Christopher (1953- )', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/db939efd-cd16-5d6a-9960-45bc0fe7e77c', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db939efd-cd16-5d6a-9960-45bc0fe7e77c'}, {'abstract': 'The stories in this collection for young adults are set during the winter holidays, but their real subject is relationships.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/my-true-love-gave-to-me-edited-by-stephanie-perkins.html', 'snippet': 'The stories in this collection for young adults are set during the winter holidays, but their real subject is relationships.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beyond “The Hunger Games” and “The Fault in Our Stars” lies a rich, varied landscape of young adult literature. In this new collection of holiday-themed tales of young love, 12 popular Y.A. authors skillfully demonstrate its breadth of styles and differing depths. Both genre and contemporary fiction are represented, making the slightly uneven terrain fascinating. Winter solstice, Christmas, Hanukkah and New Year’s Eve serve as backdrops, but relationships are central, reminding readers that while the season can be painful, it’s also a time of hope.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘My True Love Gave to Me,’ Edited by Stephanie Perkins', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beyond Reindeer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hopkins, Ellen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perkins, Stephanie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'My True Love Gave to Me: Twelve Holiday Stories (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ellen Hopkins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hopkins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e35225d-817d-5e57-864d-f9aa009489ab', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e35225d-817d-5e57-864d-f9aa009489ab'}, {'abstract': 'The actor Cary Elwes relates behind-the-scenes tales from the making of the 1987 classic “The Princess Bride.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/as-you-wish-about-the-making-of-the-princess.html', 'snippet': 'The actor Cary Elwes relates behind-the-scenes tales from the making of the 1987 classic “The Princess Bride.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his new memoir about the making of the film “The Princess Bride,” the actor Cary Elwes spends a fair amount of time on the preparation and execution of that movie’s rollicking sword-fighting scene. But it’s you who will feel as if you need a sword as you make your way through his book, to hack through the jungle of encomiums that hide the smattering of genuinely interesting nuggets.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '73', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GENZLINGER/07GENZLINGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 632, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GENZLINGER/07GENZLINGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 632}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GENZLINGER/07GENZLINGER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 972, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GENZLINGER/07GENZLINGER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1965, 'width': 1865, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GENZLINGER/07GENZLINGER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GENZLINGER/07GENZLINGER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GENZLINGER/07GENZLINGER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘As You Wish,’ About the Making of ‘The Princess Bride’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'True Love', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Elwes, Cary', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Layden, Joe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Princess Bride (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7451fb99-43e9-5973-9eaf-64bc3a39c95b', 'word_count': 668, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7451fb99-43e9-5973-9eaf-64bc3a39c95b'}, {'abstract': 'A candid autobiography from Herbie Hancock, and a less forthcoming one by George Benson.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/new-books-by-herbie-hancock-and-george-benson.html', 'snippet': 'A candid autobiography from Herbie Hancock, and a less forthcoming one by George Benson.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When he was 11 he performed a Mozart piano concerto with the Chicago Symphony Orchestra. In his 20s he was in a Miles Davis quintet widely regarded as one of the great small groups in jazz history. He’s an Oscar-winning film composer and the first jazz instrumentalist in four decades to win the Grammy Award for album of the year. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '60', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07KEEPNEWS1/07KEEPNEWS1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 873, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07KEEPNEWS1/07KEEPNEWS1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 873}}, {'r