name': 'Keepnews', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/737fa79e-bf9f-50d8-b474-e184c814f1d8', 'word_count': 1392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/737fa79e-bf9f-50d8-b474-e184c814f1d8'}, {'abstract': 'The fantasy writer Patrick Rothfuss is planning to kiss an animal chosen by his Twitter followers, for charity: “It looks overwhelmingly like it will be a llama.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'The fantasy writer Patrick Rothfuss is planning to kiss an animal chosen by his Twitter followers, for charity: “It looks overwhelmingly like it will be a llama.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Giver: The hirsute fantasy writer Patrick Rothfuss has three books to his name, all of them best sellers. (His latest, “The Slow Regard of Silent Things,” spent a month on the hardcover fiction list before falling, this week, to No. 20 on the extended list.) Rothfuss is also the driving force behind a charity called Worldbuilders, which uses auctions, giveaways and “good old-fashioned ballyhoo,” as he put it in a recent phone conversation, to benefit the anti\xadpoverty organization Heifer International. \xadBallyhoo? “Cool people doing weird things,” Rothfuss explained. For instance, when donations to the current fund drive reached $150,000, Worldbuilders posted a video of the author Myke Cole reviewing “Hedging His Bets,” a work of \xadwere-hedgehog erotica. (“It’s nice to know that, at least in the world of sub-sub-sub-sub-sub-genre literature, we’re forging into bold new territory,” Cole said.) When donations reached $325,000, the “Eragon” author Christopher Paolini recited a story that has entered the so-bad-it’s-good fantasy canon. And when the drive ends at midnight on Monday, if donations have exceeded last year’s total of $681,000, Rothfuss himself has pledged to kiss an animal chosen by his Twitter followers. “It looks overwhelmingly like it will be a llama,” he said. “I’m a little nervous about that, actually. Alpacas are warm and fuzzy, like kittens. Llamas are like Viking warriors. People use them to guard their livestock. I anticipate being trampled to death.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07list-rothfuss/07list-rothfuss-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07list-rothfuss/07list-rothfuss-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07list-rothfuss/07list-rothfuss-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07list-rothfuss/07list-rothfuss-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07list-rothfuss/07list-rothfuss-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13c9dff0-f278-57dc-b612-c599209f58a4', 'word_count': 472, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13c9dff0-f278-57dc-b612-c599209f58a4'}, {'abstract': 'Charles King traces the making of modern Istanbul.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/midnight-at-the-pera-palace-by-charles-king.html', 'snippet': 'Charles King traces the making of modern Istanbul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every now and then, there’s a story that needs to be told. It may come in a film or a novel, but it often arrives as a history. These days, we need a history of Ukraine and one of Syria, and we also need Charles King to trace the making of modern Istanbul. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '84', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GOODWIN/07GOODWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1105, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GOODWIN/07GOODWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1105}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GOODWIN/07GOODWIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 557, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GOODWIN/07GOODWIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1869, 'width': 1015, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GOODWIN/07GOODWIN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GOODWIN/07GOODWIN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GOODWIN/07GOODWIN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Midnight at the Pera Palace,’ by Charles King', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Imperial City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Charles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Goodwin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodwin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6ec5024-b7ed-5861-8b64-b0c725949f78', 'word_count': 1283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6ec5024-b7ed-5861-8b64-b0c725949f78'}, {'abstract': 'A group portrait of American photographers, including Edward Weston, Ansel Adams and Imogen Cunningham.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/group-f64-about-ansel-adams-and-others-by-mary-street-alinder.html', 'snippet': 'A group portrait of American photographers, including Edward Weston, Ansel Adams and Imogen Cunningham.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Oct. 15, 1932, several prominent and promising photographers gathered at a studio in Oakland, Calif., that belonged to two young photography enthusiasts, Willard Van Dyke and Mary Jeanette Edwards. Edward Weston, already known for his nudes and his works depicting nautilus shells and vegetables, had recently taken Van Dyke under his wing, and Van Dyke and Edwards wanted to impress him. They invited other photographers — a woman named Imogen Cunningham, the not-yet-famous Ansel Adams — to join them for a party. “Before evening’s end,” Mary Street Alinder writes of this loose fellowship that would become Group f.64, they were looking to “solve some of their common problems,” both aesthetic and financial.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07ALTMAN/07ALTMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 793, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07ALTMAN/07ALTMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 793}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07ALTMAN/07ALTMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 775, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07ALTMAN/07ALTMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1799, 'width': 1361, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07ALTMAN/07ALTMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07ALTMAN/07ALTMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07ALTMAN/07ALTMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Group f.64,’ about Ansel Adams and Others, by Mary Street Alinder', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sharp Focus', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Alinder, Mary Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ADAMS, ANSEL', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weston, Edward', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cunningham, Imogen', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Altman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Altman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6424671-5548-52fc-a4ed-53936f0948b2', 'word_count': 908, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6424671-5548-52fc-a4ed-53936f0948b2'}, {'abstract': 'A biographer says undiagnosed post-traumatic stress disorder explains \xadeverything that Jacqueline Kennedy did during the three decades after witnessing her husband’s assassination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/jacqueline-bouvier-kennedy-onassis-by-barbara-leaming.html', 'snippet': 'A biographer says undiagnosed post-traumatic stress disorder explains \xadeverything that Jacqueline Kennedy did during the three decades after witnessing her husband’s assassination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been 20 years since Jacqueline Kennedy Onassis died at age 64 of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma, and yet the Jackie Industry remains robust. Earlier this year, All Hallows College in Ireland made headlines by \xadannouncing plans to auction — for an estimated $1.6 million — the former first lady’s private letters to a now deceased priest, which included critical comments about her mother-in-law, Rose (“I don’t think Jack’s mother is too bright”), and anguish over her husband’s assassination (“I am so bitter against God”).', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '62', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GORDON/07GORDON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 527, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GORDON/07GORDON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 527}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GORDON/07GORDON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GORDON/07GORDON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1799, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GORDON/07GORDON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GORDON/07GORDON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07GORDON/07GORDON-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Jacqueline Bouvier Kennedy Onassis,’ by Barbara Leaming', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Oh, Jackie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leaming, Barbara', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Onassis, Jacqueline Kennedy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meryl Gordon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meryl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e453f6bd-9378-5d4b-80da-2e8f9a370b20', 'word_count': 753, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e453f6bd-9378-5d4b-80da-2e8f9a370b20'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Twigger’s book about the Nile is a pastiche of history and travelogue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/red-nile-by-robert-twigger.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Twigger’s book about the Nile is a pastiche of history and travelogue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1896, a 32-year-old adventurer named Jean-Baptiste Marchand embarked on an ill-conceived expedition to claim the Upper Nile for France. Nicknamed John the Baptist for his visionary intensity, Marchand sailed a steamer up a tributary of the Congo River into the African interior, grinding to a halt where the river ran out on a plateau 130 miles from the Nile. Marchand’s porters sawed the steamer into sections, laid wooden tracks through the jungle and carried the boat and their gear — including 90,000 rounds of ammunition and hundreds of kegs of fine French wine — for months across the divide. By the time they reached the Nile, the British Army had slaughtered thousands of Sudanese soldiers at the battle of Omdurman and solidified its control over the river’s upper reaches. All of Marchand’s efforts had been for naught.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HAMMER/07HAMMER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HAMMER/07HAMMER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HAMMER/07HAMMER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 634, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HAMMER/07HAMMER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1268, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HAMMER/07HAMMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HAMMER/07HAMMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07HAMMER/07HAMMER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Red Nile,’ by Robert Twigger', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fertile Waters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hammer, Joshua', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Twigger, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Hammer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hammer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef8e3496-0b84-50a7-8b29-edcd0fe4ad5a', 'word_count': 1334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef8e3496-0b84-50a7-8b29-edcd0fe4ad5a'}, {'abstract': 'Andrew McCarthy on the season’s travel books, including David Greene’s “Midnight in Siberia,” about a journey along the almost 6,000-mile Trans-Siberian Railway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/david-greenes-midnight-in-siberia-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Andrew McCarthy on the season’s travel books, including David Greene’s “Midnight in Siberia,” about a journey along the almost 6,000-mile Trans-Siberian Railway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Travel writing used to be easier. Back in the 19th century, all Richard Burton had to do was survive hostile natives throwing a javelin through his cheek, slay the wildest of animals and endure mysterious and grotesque illness to come back with a rousing tale of darkest Africa. And even as late as the dawn of the last century, Frank Worsley, Ernest Shackleton’s captain, merely had to carry on through a winter trapped on the Antarctic ice, sail 800 miles in an open lifeboat across the Southern Ocean, then traverse South Georgia Island under moonlight in order to reach help and write about his ordeal. But in our Google Maps world, even once sleepy places like poor Provence have become hackneyed and played out.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TRAVEL1/07TRAVEL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TRAVEL1/07TRAVEL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TRAVEL1/07TRAVEL1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TRAVEL1/07TRAVEL1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 1199, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TRAVEL1/07TRAVEL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TRAVEL1/07TRAVEL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TRAVEL1/07TRAVEL1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Travel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Travel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Andrew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greene, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Curtis, Wayne', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bonnett, Alastair', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Iyer, Pico', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew McCarthy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCarthy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1ce51f5-0749-5277-8a2d-3e09576e9aef', 'word_count': 2592, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1ce51f5-0749-5277-8a2d-3e09576e9aef'}, {'abstract': 'A comprehensive monograph of the work of the Italian architect Ettore Sottsass.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/sottsass-by-philippe-thom.html', 'snippet': 'A comprehensive monograph of the work of the Italian architect Ettore Sottsass.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What does a computer look like? We recognize them now as slim and metallic, but not so long ago they were bubbly and candy-colored. Before that they were black and boxy. Before that they filled rooms, attended by staff. (Before that, computers were staff.) Ettore Sottsass (1917-2007), the Italian architect, designer and provocateur, was one of the first to grapple with the character of these new objects in the office landscape. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '85', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07LANGE1/07LANGE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 417, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07LANGE1/07LANGE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 417}}, {'rank': 0, '