: None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/464cf006-bb3e-5d1b-a081-3e821fba7d0c', 'word_count': 1184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/464cf006-bb3e-5d1b-a081-3e821fba7d0c'}, {'abstract': 'New gardening books include Katrina Blair’s “Wild Wisdom of Weeds.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/katrina-blairs-wild-wisdom-of-weeds-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New gardening books include Katrina Blair’s “Wild Wisdom of Weeds.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m grateful to my father for many things, particularly for the way he taught me to love the natural world. One morning, when I was a child, I excitedly pointed out a mysterious circle of lush, vibrant grass in our lawn, a pattern that seemed to have sprung up overnight. Thankfully, he didn’t fill my head with facts about underground fungal mycelia. Instead, he told me about the fairies that came out to dance at midnight, carelessly leaving magical traces in the grass — fairy rings.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '68', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-Browning-sub1/1207-bks-Browning-sub1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-Browning-sub1/1207-bks-Browning-sub1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-Browning-sub1/1207-bks-Browning-sub1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-Browning-sub1/1207-bks-Browning-sub1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 1199, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-Browning-sub1/1207-bks-Browning-sub1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-Browning-sub1/1207-bks-Browning-sub1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/1207-bks-Browning-sub1/1207-bks-Browning-sub1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gardening', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gardening', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Browning, Dominique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dominique Browning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dominique', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bffb1a26-e784-50e8-9b9b-ee7e8ea993ec', 'word_count': 2473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bffb1a26-e784-50e8-9b9b-ee7e8ea993ec'}, {'abstract': 'An account of the career and personal life of Aretha Franklin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/respect-the-life-of-aretha-franklin-by-david-ritz.html', 'snippet': 'An account of the career and personal life of Aretha Franklin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How often do you remember the first time you heard a song? One evening in the late spring of 1967, I had dinner with friends at the old Shanghai Cafe under the elevated local tracks at Broadway and 125th Street. As we left the restaurant, we heard music coming from farther east on 125th. A record store had set up a turntable on the sidewalk, and was playing Aretha Franklin’s “Respect” over and over again. A small crowd had gathered to listen. My friends and I knew something about R & B, and we were even familiar with Otis Redding’s recording of the song. But we had never heard anything like this. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DIXLER/07DIXLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 922, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DIXLER/07DIXLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 922}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DIXLER/07DIXLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 667, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DIXLER/07DIXLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1333, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DIXLER/07DIXLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DIXLER/07DIXLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07DIXLER/07DIXLER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Respect: The Life of Aretha Franklin,’ by David Ritz', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Queen of Soul', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ritz, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franklin, Aretha', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Respect: The Life of Aretha Franklin (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elsa Dixler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elsa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dixler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c274c926-3ea3-55a4-b694-e5cfb6794f4b', 'word_count': 1100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c274c926-3ea3-55a4-b694-e5cfb6794f4b'}, {'abstract': 'The story of married advertising artists, and a collection of international product design from World War II until now.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/dorothy-and-otis-and-the-art-of-things.html', 'snippet': 'The story of married advertising artists, and a collection of international product design from World War II until now.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A man from the American hinterlands with a murky past and a talent for invention rises in the boom years of advertising. He marries a beauty. They live in style. Heavy drinking, infidelity and a predictable breakup follow. Age and dissipation ravage the man’s taut features, and his creative genius looks rusty at the end — by which I do not mean the concluding episodes of “Mad Men” that are scheduled to air in the spring, but the retirement and death of Otis Shepard.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '56', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07LASKY/07LASKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 238, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07LASKY/07LASKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 238}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07LASKY/07LASKY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07LASKY/07LASKY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1665, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07LASKY/07LASKY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07LASKY/07LASKY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07LASKY/07LASKY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Dorothy and Otis’ and ‘The Art of Things’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Designing the Dream', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hathaway, Norman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadel, Dan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Forest, Dominique', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Lasky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lasky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/62e9cbcf-2e75-5864-a585-874310ed77fd', 'word_count': 1071, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62e9cbcf-2e75-5864-a585-874310ed77fd'}, {'abstract': 'Two essential new books for lovers of American roots music.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/wayfaring-strangers-and-ralph-peer-and-the-making-of-popular-roots-music.html', 'snippet': 'Two essential new books for lovers of American roots music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For lovers of American roots music, these two books are essential. Fiona Ritchie, the host of NPR’s “The Thistle and Shamrock,” and Doug Orr, the founder of the Swannanoa Gathering music workshops, have put together a gorgeous holiday gift book, including a CD of various artists’ renditions of the songs whose origins the authors so beautifully recount. “Wayfaring Strangers” tells the story of how Scottish immigrants to Ulster in Northern Ireland merged their own musical traditions with those of the Irish before coming to America and adding their music to the American songbook. These Scotch-Irish immigrants moved principally to Appalachia, where their traditions took new forms in classics like “Barbara Allen” and “Shady Grove.” As Dolly Parton writes in her introduction, “I grew up in the Smoky Mountains listening to these ancient ballads that had crossed oceans and valleys,” songs that became the basis for folk, bluegrass and country music.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RADOSH-credit-fix/07RADOSH-credit-fix-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RADOSH-credit-fix/07RADOSH-credit-fix-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RADOSH-credit-fix/07RADOSH-credit-fix-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RADOSH-credit-fix/07RADOSH-credit-fix-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1359, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RADOSH-credit-fix/07RADOSH-credit-fix-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RADOSH-credit-fix/07RADOSH-credit-fix-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RADOSH-credit-fix/07RADOSH-credit-fix-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Wayfaring Strangers’ and ‘Ralph Peer and the Making of Popular Roots Music’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Roots Music', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Orr, Doug', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mazor, Barry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Folk Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ritchie, Fiona Karen (1960- )', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ronald Radosh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ronald', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Radosh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d65c6807-4f69-5c6c-a452-accc19946e37', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d65c6807-4f69-5c6c-a452-accc19946e37'}, {'abstract': 'Claudia Rankine reviews the latest and perhaps last volume in Dominique de Menil’s monumental archival project.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/the-image-of-the-black-in-western-art.html', 'snippet': 'Claudia Rankine reviews the latest and perhaps last volume in Dominique de Menil’s monumental archival project.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dominique de Menil’s monumental archival project of collecting and documenting the “image of the black in Western art” began in the 1960s as an aesthetic form of resistance to anti-black racism at the height of the civil rights movement. But with the publication of the fifth volume, concentrating on the 20th century, it has become a necessary cultural resource documenting the visual construction of blackness over the past 5,000 years. This latest and perhaps last volume — subdivided into two parts, “The Impact of Africa” and “The Rise of Black Artists” — redirects the underlying colonialist, Eurocentric framing of the previous four volumes. The co-editors, David Bindman and Henry Louis Gates Jr., bring focus to black artists globally as makers of their own art and imagery, rather than solely the subjects of others’ fantasies and fascination. Though some attention is paid to overtly racist imagery popular in the first half of the 20th century, the books as a pair stand in line with de Menil’s original, corrective intent to assert presence. Given the volumes’ title and the original terms of de Menil’s archival project, a reader could not be faulted for anticipating a salon installation of numerous images — but in fact, brilliant essays by the likes of Deborah Willis, Christian Weikop, Petrine Archer and many other noted scholars richly motivate this endeavor.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RANKINE1/07RANKINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RANKINE1/07RANKINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 715}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RANKINE1/07RANKINE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 859, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RANKINE1/07RANKINE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1574, 'width': 1320, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RANKINE1/07RANKINE1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RANKINE1/07RANKINE1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07RANKINE1/07RANKINE1-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Image of the Black in Western Art’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Race Represented', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Henry Louis Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bindman, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia Rankine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rankine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9abcb12b-9ac3-539b-bb33-05ee2b26af53', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9abcb12b-9ac3-539b-bb33-05ee2b26af53'}, {'abstract': 'Diane Schoemperlen quilts seven stories out of sources like an 1854 text on natural phenomena and a 1946 Ontario public-school geography textbook.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/by-the-book-by-diane-schoemperlen.html', 'snippet': 'Diane Schoemperlen quilts seven stories out of sources like an 1854 text on natural phenomena and a 1946 Ontario public-school geography textbook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is, perhaps, unsurprising that the literary collage is having its most potent revival since Walter Benjamin’s unfinished masterpiece, “The Arcades Project.” Our moment of texts and tweets, of cutting and pasting, of sampling and repurposing provides irresistible grist for collagists, their medium ideally suited for our smartphone-fragmented consciousness. Benjamin’s recent descendants include the likes of Jonathan \xadLethem (who assembled an entire Harper’s Magazine essay about plagiarism using other sources), David Shields (whose manifesto, “Reality Hunger,” is similarly Frankensteined together) and now the Canadian author Diane Schoemperlen, the author of several story collections and three novels. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SARVAS/07SARVAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 851, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SARVAS/07SARVAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 851}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SARVAS/07SARVAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 723, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SARVAS/07SARVAS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1279, 'width': 902, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SARVAS/07SARVAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SARVAS/07SARVAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SARVAS/07SARVAS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘By the Book,’ by Diane Schoemperlen', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paste-Ups', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schoemperlen, Diane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Sarvas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sarvas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1df21cbf-8aa9-5201-88f3-b7fbe744f598', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1df21cbf-8aa9-5201-88f3-b7fbe744f598'}, {'abstract': '“Vivian Maier: A Photographer Found,” “Henri Cartier-Bresson: Here and Now,” and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/vivian-maier-a-photographer-found-and-more.html', 'snippet': '“Vivian Maier: A Photographer Found,” “Henri Cartier-Bresson: Here and Now,” and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vivian Maier, who worked mostly in domestic service, mostly in Chicago, was a serious photographer who shot some 100,000 images over upward of 40 years. Although those who knew her — primarily the children in her care; she had no real intimates — were aware that she took pictures, she never published or exhibited them, and rarely showed them to others. Her work came to general attention only when, in 2007, two years before her death, five storage lockers on which she had failed to keep up payments were emptied and their contents auctioned off. By the time the buyers realized what they had on their hands, she had died. John Maloof, who bought most of it, put up a selection of scans on a website, which immediately went viral; Maier posthumously became a media sensation. Maloof’s VIVIAN MAIER: A Photographer Found (Harper \xadDesign, $80), with text by Marvin Heiferman, is already the fifth book of her photographs to have been published.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SANTE1/07SANTE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SANTE1/07SANTE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SANTE1/07SANTE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SANTE1/07SANTE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SANTE1/07SANTE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SANTE1/07SANTE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SANTE1/07SANTE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Photography', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Photography', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sante, Luc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Luc Sante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Luc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d99f445-ad5f-5802-9d53-b9e44c8824c0', 'word_count': 2038, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d99f445-ad5f-5802-9d53-b9e44c8824c0'}, {'abstract': 'A new memoir by the 86-year-old Gordie Howe, one of the most prolific scorers in hockey history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/mr-hockey-my-story-by-gordie-howe.html', 'snippet': 'A new memoir by the 86-year-old Gordie Howe, one of the most prolific scorers in hockey history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gordie Howe had three teeth knocked out in his first game with the Detroit Red Wings in 1946. By the time he retired from the National Hockey League in 1980, at age 52, he had sustained at least 14 broken noses and, by his estimation, required more than 300 stitches to repair his face. He was once kicked in the temple and, so woozy he couldn’t see straight, missed the entry to the team bench by three feet.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BRANCH/07BRANCH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 751, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BRANCH/07BRANCH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 751}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BRANCH/07BRANCH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 818, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BRANCH/07BRANCH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1636, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BRANCH/07BRANCH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BRANCH/07BRANCH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BRANCH/07BRANCH-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Mr. Hockey: My Story,’ by Gordie Howe', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hat Trick', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howe, Gordie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Branch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Branch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b61beb79-e0e6-5573-9721-2692148f86e0', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b61beb79-e0e6-5573-9721-2692148f86e0'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of the photographer Sam Wagstaff.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/wagstaff-before-and-after-mapplethorpe-by-philip-gefter.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of the photographer Sam Wagstaff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The mention of Sam Wagstaff’s name is likely to elicit a respectful nod from anyone who cares about photography. He made his mark in the ’70s, when he haunted print shops and auction houses and assembled a major collection. Eccentric and wide-ranging, his holdings mingled classic images from the 19th century — misty views of French forests, soulful portraits of poets — with contemporary images of brazen eroticism. Was photography art? Wagstaff’s lover, Robert Mapplethorpe, answered the question with a vengeance, yoking an austere, nearly arctic elegance to images of flowers, male nudes and the rituals of sadomasochism. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SOLOMON/07SOLOMON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 513, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SOLOMON/07SOLOMON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 513}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumb