any other TV series.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/norman-lears-even-this-i-get-to-experience.html', 'snippet': 'An autobiography by Norman Lear, the creator of “Maude,” “All in the Family” and many other TV series.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Maude, c’est moi.” Norman Lear could easily have adopted Flaubert’s famous phrase about Emma Bovary to explain who inspired one of his most beloved sitcoms, “Maude.” Because in his charming, candid and copious autobiography, “Even This I Get to Experience,” Lear holds back only when it comes to the real-life model for Maude.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '53', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07STANLEY/07STANLEY-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 457, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07STANLEY/07STANLEY-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 457}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07STANLEY/07STANLEY-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 780, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07STANLEY/07STANLEY-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1560, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07STANLEY/07STANLEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07STANLEY/07STANLEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07STANLEY/07STANLEY-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Norman Lear’s ‘Even This I Get to Experience’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Those Were the Days', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stanley, Alessandra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lear, Norman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alessandra Stanley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alessandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stanley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee4bd5cf-2df3-5f5f-aa00-12e15700dcc1', 'word_count': 1037, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee4bd5cf-2df3-5f5f-aa00-12e15700dcc1'}, {'abstract': 'New books about the lingo of food and finance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/john-lanchesters-how-to-speak-money-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books about the lingo of food and finance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s surprising that even in this data-driven era, we don’t know exactly how many words there are in English. Every specialty has its jargon, of course, its own subset of terms that separate the knowing from the newbies. When expertise is often measured in language, knowing the lingo shows that you’re ready to talk the talk, which is especially important in fields that touch our lives as frequently and intimately as food and finance.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '41', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SOLOKOWSKI/07SOLOKOWSKI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 514, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SOLOKOWSKI/07SOLOKOWSKI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 514}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SOLOKOWSKI/07SOLOKOWSKI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 830, 'width': 968, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SOLOKOWSKI/07SOLOKOWSKI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 830, 'width': 968, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SOLOKOWSKI/07SOLOKOWSKI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SOLOKOWSKI/07SOLOKOWSKI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SOLOKOWSKI/07SOLOKOWSKI-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'John Lanchester’s ‘How to Speak Money,’ and More', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Just Sayin'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lanchester, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jurafsky, Daniel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Christopher J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanders, Ella Frances', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Language of Food: A Linguist Reads the Menu (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Sokolowski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sokolowski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e6ea5aa-a30f-52ba-8c67-4d115b10db1f', 'word_count': 1197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e6ea5aa-a30f-52ba-8c67-4d115b10db1f'}, {'abstract': 'New books by Joe Perry, George Clinton, Viv Albertine, Billy Idol and other music stars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/jope.html', 'snippet': 'New books by Joe Perry, George Clinton, Viv Albertine, Billy Idol and other music stars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tone, it’s all about tone. If you’re writing a book “with” a rock star — if you’re crouched in a darkened room, that is, with your quill quivering, waiting for the legend slumped opposite you to ingest an amount of caffeine/nicotine sufficient to propel him through the charred drug-holes of his own memory — you’ve got to get the tone just right. Outside, the inevitable California afternoon blazes with cruel banality; inside, it’s 2 a.m. forever. An ear rings, a synapse misfires. Your rock star \xadbegins to ramble. How true will you be to the tone of his talk, to his fractured anecdotal style with the woozy \xadsilences and the skeletal chuckles? Will you fix his grammar, trim his profanities, check his facts? He’s just told a story that has no ending, no point — almost no beginning. Are his eyes even open? Impossible to tell through the dark glasses. You might have to make half of this up.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07PARKER/07PARKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07PARKER/07PARKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07PARKER/07PARKER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07PARKER/07PARKER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 1199, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07PARKER/07PARKER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07PARKER/07PARKER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07PARKER/07PARKER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pop Music', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop Music', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 