rk Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WATMAN/07WATMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WATMAN/07WATMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WATMAN/07WATMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WATMAN/07WATMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 1199, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WATMAN/07WATMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WATMAN/07WATMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WATMAN/07WATMAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cooking', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cooking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Max Watman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Max', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Watman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/477f7809-e7f5-597a-9f8e-3f1431fd99e8', 'word_count': 2654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/477f7809-e7f5-597a-9f8e-3f1431fd99e8'}, {'abstract': 'A Freudian take on classic depictions of sexual imagery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/the-sexual-night-by-pascal-quignard.html', 'snippet': 'A Freudian take on classic depictions of sexual imagery.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Sexual Night” is a damp little relic, the sort of psycho\xadsexual mumbo jumbo one thought — one hoped — wouldn’t survive Norman Mailer. Alas, the French writer Pascal Quignard, best known for his 1991 novel “Tous les Matins du Monde,” has resurrected the genre in these Freudian disquisitions on classic depictions of sexual imagery.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB2-07SEHGAL/SUB2-07SEHGAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB2-07SEHGAL/SUB2-07SEHGAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB2-07SEHGAL/SUB2-07SEHGAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB2-07SEHGAL/SUB2-07SEHGAL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1081, 'width': 1706, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB2-07SEHGAL/SUB2-07SEHGAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB2-07SEHGAL/SUB2-07SEHGAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/SUB2-07SEHGAL/SUB2-07SEHGAL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Sexual Night,’ by Pascal Quignard', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Primal Scenes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sehgal, Parul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quignard, Pascal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Parul Sehgal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Parul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sehgal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b57c0e8-df2d-542a-ba18-ecfe4556ff16', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b57c0e8-df2d-542a-ba18-ecfe4556ff16'}, {'abstract': 'Kate Williams’s “Ambition and Desire” is the latest in a growing corpus of work about Josephine Bonaparte, Napoleon’s empress.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/ambition-and-desire-the-dangerous-life-of-josephine-bonaparte-by-kate-williams.html', 'snippet': 'Kate Williams’s “Ambition and Desire” is the latest in a growing corpus of work about Josephine Bonaparte, Napoleon’s empress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This year marks the bicentennial of the death of Josephine Bonaparte, but Napoleon’s empress has been having a moment for some time now. In the past two decades, she has starred in at least 20 new biographies, six museum exhibitions and six novels. Three editions of her correspondence have also appeared during this time, as have many more studies (of Napoleon and other Bonapartes) in which she features. The latest addition to this corpus is “Ambition and Desire: The Dangerous Life of Josephine Bonaparte,” by Kate Williams, a biographer of Queen Victoria and Emma Hamilton. Beyond her appreciation for “flawed, vulnerable, engaging, powerful” women, Williams does not seem to have a compelling reason to tell this story. In the absence of new material or a new approach, she offers a breathless paean to the woman who, while “no great beauty,” could with “one twitch of her skirt . . . enthrall the man who terrorized Europe.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '72', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WEBER/07WEBER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WEBER/07WEBER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 407}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WEBER/07WEBER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WEBER/07WEBER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1389, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WEBER/07WEBER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WEBER/07WEBER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WEBER/07WEBER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Ambition and Desire: The Dangerous Life of Josephine Bonaparte,’ by Kate Williams', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Empress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weber, Caroline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bonaparte, Josephine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Kate (Historian)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Weber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9d8f7a7-7ff0-5409-b3d8-aee35a2447eb', 'word_count': 951, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9d8f7a7-7ff0-5409-b3d8-aee35a2447eb'}, {'abstract': 'Lavish reproductions of work by Willem de Kooning, accompanied by research and historical anecdotes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/a-way-of-living-the-art-of-willem-de-kooning-by-judith-zilczer.html', 'snippet': 'Lavish reproductions of work by Willem de Kooning, accompanied by research and historical anecdotes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mark Stevens and Annalyn Swan’s “De Kooning: An American Master,” which won a \xadPulitzer Prize in 2005, was billed as the first major biography of the artist. It covered most of the salient points: Willem de Kooning’s hardscrabble upbringing in Rotterdam; the sea voyage to America in 1926; his struggles with paint; his sexual affairs and the beloved child born out of wedlock (with a woman other than his wife of 45 years, Elaine de \xadKooning); the alcoholism that, remarkably, didn’t kill him. In his later years, de Kooning admitted he shouldn’t even be alive. After suffering from Alzheimer’s disease for more than a decade, he died in 1997 at the age of 92. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWENDENER/07schwendener-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 794, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWENDENER/07schwendener-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 794}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWENDENER/07schwendener-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 774, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWENDENER/07schwendener-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1548, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWENDENER/07SCHWENDENER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWENDENER/07SCHWENDENER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWENDENER/07SCHWENDENER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Way of Living: The Art of Willem de Kooning,’ by Judith Zilczer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art Star', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zilczer, Judith', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Kooning, Willem', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Schwendener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwendener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/82c8e1e5-12d4-59d9-897d-7545a8b99536', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82c8e1e5-12d4-59d9-897d-7545a8b99536'}, {'abstract': 'New books about propaganda posters and other items from the relatively short-lived German Democratic Republic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/ddr-posters-and-beyond-the-wall.html', 'snippet': 'New books about propaganda posters and other items from the relatively short-lived German Democratic Republic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For 40 years, the Cold War dominated the world stage. East and West Germany stood at the front lines of the global confrontation, symbolized by the Berlin Wall, which separated friends, lovers and families. To mark the 25th anniversary of the fall of the wall, a number of publications have recently appeared. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '75', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WELCH1/07WELCH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 897, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WELCH1/07WELCH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 897}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WELCH1/07WELCH1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 685, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WELCH1/07WELCH1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1370, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WELCH1/07WELCH1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WELCH1/07WELCH1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07WELCH1/07WELCH1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘DDR Posters’ and ‘Beyond the Wall’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Back in the G.DR.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heather, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jampol, Justinian', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Propaganda', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Welch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Welch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/19e8df39-fea9-5ce6-905e-c9f1b7fe9f9f', 'word_count': 1142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19e8df39-fea9-5ce6-905e-c9f1b7fe9f9f'}, {'abstract': 'A memoir by the comedian and sitcom star Amy Poehler.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/yes-please-by-amy-poehler.html', 'snippet': 'A memoir by the comedian and sitcom star Amy Poehler.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amy Poehler admits she wrote her new memoir while sleep-deprived. And it shows. But not in an entirely bad way. It helps that Poehler is upfront about her exhaustion, given that she is a 43-year-old sitcom star who shoots 12 hours of television a day while mothering two children under 7. Even when Poehler does sleep, she wakes herself up 20 to 30 times a night (according to a Beverly Hills sleep clinic) and, apparently, snores “like a dragon.” She freely allows that “Yes Please” is a “spontaneous overflow in the middle of chaos, not tranquillity,” a thing written on subways and planes, “ugly and in pieces.” And in the end, no apologies. She finished the job. Perfection is a luxury. As improv’s godfather Del Close used to say: “Don’t think.” “Get out of your head,” Poehler writes. “Stop planning and just go.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '74', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TSING-LOH/07TSING-LOH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TSING-LOH/07TSING-LOH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 409}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TSING-LOH/07TSING-LOH-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TSING-LOH/07TSING-LOH-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1126, 'width': 1650, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TSING-LOH/07TSING-LOH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TSING-LOH/07TSING-LOH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07TSING-LOH/07TSING-LOH-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Yes Please,’ by Amy Poehler', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Upright Citizen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Poehler, Amy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Yes Please (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sandra Tsing Loh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sandra', 'middlename': 'Tsing', 'lastname': 'Loh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/22e31bb2-e1ae-5986-b73b-2f82fef26330', 'word_count': 862, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22e31bb2-e1ae-5986-b73b-2f82fef26330'}, {'abstract': 'Memoirs by Roger Moore, Sophia Loren and Anjelica Huston are among the season’s books about movies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/sophia-lorens-yesterday-today-tomorrow-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Memoirs by Roger Moore, Sophia Loren and Anjelica Huston are among the season’s books about movies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When did self-proclaimed movie geekdom supplant fandom as a badge of superior commitment to cinema love? Being more fan than geek and therefore not beholden to quantifiable research, I blithely estimate that the mutation began about the time Darth Vader first started wearing his CPAP machine around the house and frightening the children. Geekdom was born under the moon of the modern Hollywood blockbuster era and nurtured by the simultaneous development of home computers and cable television — which in turn begat the Internet, TCM, Fandango and trivia-enhanced moviegoing life as we know it. Fans may be happy with publicity stills, but geeks want content, and context too. Fans may enjoy autographs, but geeks demand Reddit Ask Me Anything threads.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '76', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWARZBAUM/07SCHWARZBAUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWARZBAUM/07SCHWARZBAUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWARZBAUM/07SCHWARZBAUM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 770, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWARZBAUM/07SCHWARZBAUM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1498, 'width': 1992, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWARZBAUM/07SCHWARZBAUM-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWARZBAUM/07SCHWARZBAUM-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07SCHWARZBAUM/07SCHWARZBAUM-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hollywood', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hollywood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwarzbaum, Lisa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T18:28:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Schwarzbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwarzbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff857779-ce7b-574c-8675-1c5a00513d7d', 'word_count': 2458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff857779-ce7b-574c-8675-1c5a00513d7d'}, {'abstract': 'A cultural historian argues that Oscar Wilde was among the first to realize that celebrity could come before accomplishment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/books/review/wilde-in-america-by-david-m-friedman.html', 'snippet': 'A cultural historian argues that Oscar Wilde was among the first to realize that celebrity could come before accomplishment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chances are you’ll never have a conversation as scintillating as the one Oscar Wilde was overheard conducting at a gathering in San Francisco in 1882. At the time, the 27-year-old Irish upstart had yet to write any of the works that would earn his fame. Undercredentialed as Wilde then was, his verbal verve and outlandish dress (satin breeches, velvet jackets, black cape) had made him a sought-after dinner guest in London and prompted a 10-month American tour, where his brio met with an ecstatic reception. “It was a superb performance, a masterpiece of sparkling wit and gaiety,” wrote one audience member in an account of the event. “Never before, or since, have I heard anything that compared to it.” Who was Wilde’s lucky interlocutor? It was a dressmaker’s dummy: The man was, in essence, talking with himself. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '52', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': 