ank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T21:43:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/50c0ee32-e001-543c-ac34-3a6a86b2b74e', 'word_count': 982, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50c0ee32-e001-543c-ac34-3a6a86b2b74e'}, {'abstract': 'Bodo Ramelow of the Left Party, which is made up of ex-Communists and former Social Democrats, was installed as governor of the state of Thuringia in the east.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/06/world/europe/germanys-left-party-takes-its-first-governors-seat.html', 'snippet': 'Bodo Ramelow of the Left Party, which is made up of ex-Communists and former Social Democrats, was installed as governor of the state of Thuringia in the east.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — A quarter-century after the Berlin Wall fell, the first politician from the Left Party, made up of ex-Communists and former Social Democrats, was installed on Friday as the governor of a German state, leading Thuringia in the east.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Germany’s Left Party Takes Its First Governor’s Seat', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-05T21:44:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Smale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d0b6f6a-e293-5caf-816a-a9f46edf570b', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d0b6f6a-e293-5caf-816a-a9f46edf570b'}, {'abstract': 'As long ago as 1991, there were calls to pursue “soft,” not binding, steps toward a global climate treaty. Talks are progressing now because this shift has occurred.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/05/the-soft-path-to-a-climate-agreement-from-lima-to-paris/', 'snippet': 'As long ago as 1991, there were calls to pursue “soft,” not binding, steps toward a global climate treaty. Talks are progressing now because this shift has occurred.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Needless to say, there’s been a big and promising shift in tone and some substance in global warming\xa0diplomacy\xa0of late — led by the paired pledges of China and the United States to intensify efforts to curtail heat-trapping carbon dioxide emissions. Other countries, including gas-rich Malaysia, have promised to act on climate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/05/blogs/dotlima1/dotlima1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 580, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/05/blogs/dotlima1/dotlima1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 580, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/05/blogs/dotlima1/dotlima1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/05/blogs/dotlima1/dotlima1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'u