 an indie spirit avant la lettre, and the subject of a meaty retrospective at the Museum of Modern Art, through Jan. 17. Altman’s heyday was the 1970s, when movies like “Nashville,” “The Long Goodbye” and “California Split,” full of overlapping talk and intersecting lives, helped define an era and a certain sensibility: revisionist, romantic, downbeat. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 898, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 898}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 685, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1369, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What This Town Needs Is a Brothel', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kic