, '_id': 'nyt://article/595e1552-8e1c-5c81-903d-afe4ef627d66', 'word_count': 1463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/595e1552-8e1c-5c81-903d-afe4ef627d66'}, {'abstract': 'The sports editor Jason Stallman explained how coming up with the article “Jets? Giants? There Are Better Ways to Spend Your Sunday” was a team effort.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2014/12/08/better-than-football/', 'snippet': 'The sports editor Jason Stallman explained how coming up with the article “Jets? Giants? There Are Better Ways to Spend Your Sunday” was a team effort.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday, The Times’s home page featured a splashy article called “Jets? Giants? There Are Better Ways to Spend Your Sunday.”Blasphemy from the sports desk?The piece included reflections and suggestions from various Times reporters on good ways, as the introduction said, to “take a break from the ritual masochism.”In an entry under the rubric “Improve Yourself,” the sports staff reporter Sarah Lyall suggested emptying your email inbox. Another sports reporter, John Branch, suggested a visit to the New Jersey swamps. The senior editor Sam Dolnick put together an intriguing day of activity — movies, songs and web surfing — designed to conjure visions of love gone wrong. The staff editor Benjamin Hoffman thought doing nothing was a good alternative to watching football.Whose idea was this madcap guide to football-less wellness?The sports editor Jason Stallman explains:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Better Than Football?', 'kicker': 'Times Insider', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-08T19:27:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', '