iginal': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/137af939-3f1c-505f-812f-0ad3d305a100', 'word_count': 812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/137af939-3f1c-505f-812f-0ad3d305a100'}, {'abstract': 'In a video, a military strategist spoke out strongly against the practice of beheading, asserting that the organization founded by Osama bin Laden was more pragmatic and less extreme than the Islamic State.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/09/world/middleeast/qaeda-commander-denounces-decapitations-as-used-by-islamic-state.html', 'snippet': 'In a video, a military strategist spoke out strongly against the practice of beheading, asserting that the organization founded by Osama bin Laden was more pragmatic and less extreme than the Islamic State.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISTANBUL — Nearly a decade ago, Ayman al-Zawahiri — the man who would go on to become the head of Al Qaeda — wrote a letter to his deputy in Iraq, scolding him for beheading hostages and posting videos of their execution online. He explained that although he was in favor of killing the enemy and agreed with the principle of sowing terror, the scenes of slaughter risked turning public opinion against their organization.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Qaeda Commander Denounces Decapitations as Used by Islamic State', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Qaeda Commander Denounces Decapitations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zarqawi, Abu Musab al-', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-09T02:10:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rukmini Callimachi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rukmini', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Callimachi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbf7fade-fa99-538d-a4c0-17321122474b', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbf7fade-fa99-538d-a4c0-17321122474b'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Brace, a C.I.A. contract flier, was held in a Hanoi prison cell next to that of a future senator, John McCain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/09/us/ernest-brace-civilian-pilot-held-as-pow-in-vietnam-dies-at-83.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Brace, a C.I.A. contract flier, was held in a Hanoi prison cell next to that of a future senator, John McCain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ernest Brace, who as a pilot working for the C.I.A. endured nearly eight years of torture during the Vietnam War, first in bamboo cages, then at the prison camp known as the Hanoi Hilton in a cell next to John McCain’s, died on Friday in Klamath Falls, Ore. He was 83 and said to have been the longest-serving American civilian prisoner of war in that conflict.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/09/us/BRACE-obit/BRACE-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/09/us/BRACE-obit/BRACE-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 453}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/09/us/BRACE-obit/BRACE-obit-jumbo.jpg', '