zation': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06f35811-40e3-5682-bbe5-c7d104f0c1c0', 'word_count': 43, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06f35811-40e3-5682-bbe5-c7d104f0c1c0'}, {'abstract': 'After Citizens United, it turns out that surprisingly few wealthy people and corporations are using campaign donations to influence elections.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/07/magazine/who-wants-to-buy-a-politician.html', 'snippet': 'After Citizens United, it turns out that surprisingly few wealthy people and corporations are using campaign donations to influence elections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Don Blankenship, the recently indicted former chief executive of Massey Energy, has a history of donating a lot of money to West Virginia politicians. In 2004, for instance, Blankenship spent $3 million to support the election of a lawyer named Brent Benjamin to the state’s Supreme Court of Appeals. It was, notably, more than three times the amount spent by Benjamin’s own campaign. But for Blankenship, it appeared to be a beneficial investment. Once seated, Benjamin cast the deciding vote to overturn a $50million jury verdict against Massey.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/14econ/07mag-14economy.t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 460, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/14econ/07mag-14economy.t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 460}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/14econ/07mag-14economy.t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 785, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/14econ/07mag-14economy.t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1425, 'width': 1860, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/14econ/07mag-14economy.t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/14econ/07mag-14economy.t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/14econ/07mag-14economy.t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Wants to Buy a Politician?', 'kicker': 'It’s the Economy', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Wants to Buy a Politician?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appelbaum, Binyamin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citizens United v Federal Election Commission (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-09T09:58:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c41a0f1-d9cd-5f11-83ea-d81a8ca5151c', 'word_count': 1191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c41a0f1-d9cd-5f11-83ea-d81a8ca5151c'}, {'abstract': 'When Shelley Calton heard a friend’s tale about a gun going off by accident at a hair salon, she decided to explore the world of women who carry firearms.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/09/janies-got-a-gun/', 'snippet': 'When Shelley Calton heard a friend’s tale about a gun going off by accident at a hair salon, she decided to explore the world of women who carry firearms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One Saturday morning in Houston, Shelley Calton ran into a frazzled friend who had just come out of the hair salon. While she was getting a new cut, another patron’s handgun accidentally went off in her purse. The bullet ricocheted in the room, narrowly missing her. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Janie’s Got a Gun', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Houston (Tex)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-09T10:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurence Butet-Roch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Butet-Roch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de073c64-5ff8-5089-931c-09c2a39da12b', 'word_count': 831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de073c64-5ff8-5089-931c-09c2a39da12b'}, {'abstract': 'Does paying ransom to terrorists and kidnappers only lead to more kidnapping? Or, is an inflexible policy unfair to the families of hostages?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/09/should-countries-pay-ransom-to-free-hostages-held-by-terrorists/', 'snippet': 'Does paying ransom to terrorists and kidnappers only lead to more kidnapping? Or, is an inflexible policy unfair to the families of hostages?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This past weekend the United States tried to rescue an American photojournalist held hostage by Al Qaeda in Yemen, but the raid ended in tragedy. Like most countries, the American government forbids negotiating with terrorists and kidnappers, saying that ransom payments only encourage more hostage taking. But other countries have secretly paid ransoms to save their citizens, and loved ones sometimes argue that an inflexible policy leaves no room for hope. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/world/07hostage-1-LN/07hostage-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/world/07hostage-1-LN/07hostage-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/world/07hostage-1-LN/07hostage-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/world/07hostage-1-LN/07hostage-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/world/07hostage-1-LN/07hostage-1-thu