ntments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boards of Directors', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Milberg, Joachim', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-09T12:58:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Ewing', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b611643-b3bf-5197-a789-5f9180f1a536', 'word_count': 758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b611643-b3bf-5197-a789-5f9180f1a536'}, {'abstract': 'On Tuesday, Dec. 16, in lieu of a weekly quiz, look for our annual news quiz that rounds up the biggest stories of the year. What do you think should be included?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/09/weekly-news-quiz-dec-2-8-2014/', 'snippet': 'On Tuesday, Dec. 16, in lieu of a weekly quiz, look for our annual news quiz that rounds up the biggest stories of the year. What do you think should be included?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Have you been following the news? Take our quiz to see what you know and to learn more.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/nyregion/20141204_NQ_LN/20141204_GARNER_HP-slide-2WIT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/03/nyregion/20141204_NQ_LN/20141204_GARNER_HP-slide-2WIT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/nyregion/20141204_NQ_LN/20141204_GARNER_HP-slide-2WIT-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/nyregion/20141204_NQ_LN/20141204_GARNER_HP-slide-2WIT-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/nyregion/20141204_NQ_LN/20141204_GARNER_HP-slide-2WIT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/03/nyregion/20141204_NQ_LN/20141204_GARNER_HP-slide-2WIT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/03/nyregion/20141204_NQ_LN/20141204_GARNER_HP-slide-2WIT-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekly News Quiz | Dec. 2-8, 2014', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-09T12:59:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffcadf95-ac44-51f3-accc-993c9c672343', 'word_count': 103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffcadf95-ac44-51f3-accc-993c9c672343'}, {'abstract': 'Sometimes an anecdotal lead is the perfect way to humanize a story. But overused, it can seem mechanical and can annoy busy readers who just want us to get to the point.', 'web_url': 'https://afterdeadline.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/09/the-stranger-in-the-lede-3/', 'snippet': 'Sometimes an anecdotal lead is the perfect way to humanize a story. But overused, it can seem mechanical and can annoy busy readers who just want us to get to the point.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s the classic form of anecdotal lead: a first paragraph introducing, by name, a character unfamiliar to the readers, who are expected to continue patiently until this character’s larger significance becomes clear.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Stranger in the Story', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-09T13:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38fb2d60-c2cc-516c-9e31-ec76436ea1cd', 'word_count': 1647, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38fb2d60-c2cc-516c-9e31-ec76436ea1cd'}, {'abstract': 'A performance of “Go Away,” from Ms. Soper’s 2011 piece “Only the Words Themselves Mean What They Say.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/09/in-performance-kate-soper-and-erin-lesser/', 'snippet': 'A performance of “Go Away,” from Ms. Soper’s 2011 piece “Only the Words Themselves Mean What They Say.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a rich tradition of music for soprano and flute, a combination that conjures up thoughts of pieces like the delicate “Caro nome” from Verdi’s “Rigoletto” or the unhinged mad scene in Donizetti’s “Lucia di Lammermoor.” But the composer and performer Kate Soper does something utterly different with the pairing in “Go Away,” a virtuosic piece she wrote for herself and the flutist Erin Lesser, to a text by Lydia Davis. Ms. Soper and Ms. Lesser are members of the Wet Ink Ensemble, a new music group that will perform Dec. 15 at St. Peter’s Church, at 346 West 20th Street. Here they are performing “Go Away,” from Ms. Soper’s 2011 limit\xad-stretching piece “Only the Words Themselves Mean What They Say.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Performance: Kate Soper and Erin Lesser', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Lydia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lesser, Erin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soper, Kate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wet Ink Ensemble', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-09T13:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35412c0a-a5b7-5c40-aeda-7348afddc106', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35412c0a-a5b7-5c40-aeda-7348afddc106'}, {'abstract': 'One fund, Starboard Value, issued a scathing 294-page critique of Darden Restaurants, which led to the replacement of the company’s entire board.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/12/09/taking-recipes-from-the-activist-cookbook/', 'snippet': 'One fund, Starboard Value, issued a scathing 294-page critique of Darden Restaurants, which led to the replacement of the company’s entire board.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a general rule, hedge funds are not shy about letting companies know what they can be doing better.', 'p