/10/today-in-politics-54/', 'snippet': 'The sharp partisan split over the validity of the Senate’s new report on torture raises questions about how closely the two parties will cooperate on intelligence issues when Republicans take control of Congress in January.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Torture Revelations Raise Partisan Heat', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Today in Politics', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T12:28:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8249d9ea-3308-5d69-bde2-c37575a47ca0', 'word_count': 1137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8249d9ea-3308-5d69-bde2-c37575a47ca0'}, {'abstract': 'Kabam, a mobile games maker, is letting employees and funders sell more than $40 million in shares to institutional investors to help the company put off an initial public offering for as long as it can.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/10/kabam-puts-off-public-offering-with-share-sale/', 'snippet': 'Kabam, a mobile games maker, is letting employees and funders sell more than $40 million in shares to institutional investors to help the company put off an initial public offering for as long as it can.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many of the most successful new games companies have rushed to sell shares to the public as soon as they possibly could. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/09/technology/09bits_kabam/09bits_kabam-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/09/technology/09bits_kabam/0