me': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/26ecfab9-1a3a-5c35-9478-8532d5cb2c1a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/26ecfab9-1a3a-5c35-9478-8532d5cb2c1a'}, {'abstract': 'Meghan Daum’s essays grapple with death, illness and ambivalence about motherhood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/the-unspeakable-by-meghan-daum.html', 'snippet': 'Meghan Daum’s essays grapple with death, illness and ambivalence about motherhood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the song “Poetic Justice,” Kendrick Lamar raps, “You’re in the mood for empathy, there’s blood in my pen.” This is how we might consider the essay — blood in the pen of the essayist, inking the personal to bring about an empathetic response. When it comes to the personal essay, we want so much and there is something cannibalistic about our desire. We want essayists to splay themselves bare. We want to see how much they are willing to bleed for us. This desire introduces an interesting tension for essay writers. How much should they bleed, and how much blood should they save for themselves? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14GAY/14gay-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14GAY/14gay-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14GAY/14gay-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14GAY/14gay-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14GAY/14GAY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14GAY/14GAY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14GAY/14GAY-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Unspeakable,’ by Meghan Daum', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Discomfort Zone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gay, Roxane', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daum, Meghan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T15:51:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roxane Gay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roxane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba9f1c9e-346a-50b7-a45f-f3360e7d4d9d', 'word_count': 1067, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba9f1c9e-346a-50b7-a45f-f3360e7d4d9d'}, {'abstract': 'Alice Munro’s later stories seem driven by a pure curiosity about other people’s journeys through life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/family-furnishings-selected-stories-by-alice-munro.html', 'snippet': 'Alice Munro’s later stories seem driven by a pure curiosity about other people’s journeys through life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the protagonists of Alice Munro’s stories — mostly women, usually Canadian, never quite content — it can be tough to get from one place to another, from where they are to where they’d rather be. There are no direct flights, no express buses or trains in the world her characters move through: They always have to change somewhere. The narrator of “Home,” one of the two dozen stories culled here from collections published in the last two decades, has to take three buses to visit her aging father and annoying stepmother and to learn a small thing about herself. Others in “Family Furnishings” have longer, harder journeys, with more ambiguous rewards: a new country, an uncomfortable truth, a difficult love. Some of Munro’s pilgrims, like the unhappy wife in “Runaway,” go part of the way and then turn back. But most just keep going: looking at the passing world, drifting in and out of the strange kind of contemplation travel can induce, until they’ve reached their destination.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RAFFERTY/14rafferty-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 613, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RAFFERTY/14rafferty-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 613}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RAFFERTY/14rafferty-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1003, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RAFFERTY/14rafferty-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2006, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RAFFERTY/14RAFFERTY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RAFFERTY/14RAFFERTY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RAFFERTY/14RAFFERTY-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Family Furnishings,’ Selected Stories by Alice Munro', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Passing World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Munro, Alice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T15:52:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Terrence Rafferty', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rafferty', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/04e734c6-ec44-539b-898d-f8e091f40c15', 'word_count': 1237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04e734c6-ec44-539b-898d-f8e091f40c15'}, {'abstract': 'Religion has long been a scapegoat for violence and conflict, Karen Armstrong argues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/fields-of-blood-by-karen-armstrong.html', 'snippet': 'Religion has long been a scapegoat for violence and conflict, Karen Armstrong argues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just after finishing Karen Armstrong’s new book, I happened to hear a discussion on television about the latest outbreak of violence in the Middle East. “We have to hope that this disagreement stays on the political level, rather than becoming a religious dispute,” one of the experts said. “Political differences can be resolved. Religious ones cannot.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14FALLOWS/1214-bks-FALLOWS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14FALLOWS/1214-bks-FALLOWS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 649}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14FALLOWS/1214-bks-FALLOWS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 948, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14FALLOWS/1214-bks-FALLOWS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1895, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14FALLOWS/1214-bks-FALLOWS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14FALLOWS/1214-bks-FALLOWS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14FALLOWS/1214-bks-FALLOWS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Fields of Blood,’ by Karen Armstrong', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unholy Wars', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fallows, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARMSTRONG, KAREN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T15:59:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Fallows', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fallows', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/76a469b9-b5c7-5fde-82f4-b736d1ddff51', 'word_count': 1194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76a469b9-b5c7-5fde-82f4-b736d1ddff51'}, {'abstract': 'The woman behind Finland’s Oscar submission used an unorthodox approach to auditions to find her young teenage star: she played a Shostakovich sympony and watched the reaction.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/10/shhh-dont-tell-other-directors-working-with-kids-is-easy/', 'snippet': 'The woman behind Finland’s Oscar submission used an unorthodox approach to auditions to find her young teenage star: she played a Shostakovich sympony and watched the reaction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a sense, the director Pirjo Honkasalo has been living with “Concrete Night,” Finland’s submission for the best foreign-language film Oscar, for a long time. Her movie is based on a 1981 novel of the same title written by Pirkko Saisio, who is both her domestic and screenwriting partner.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shhh, Don’t Tell Other Directors: Working With Kids Is Easy', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Finland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T16:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ec409f6-c97e-549f-aa48-f0ac1d73fdaa', 'word_count': 1042, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ec409f6-c97e-549f-aa48-f0ac1d73fdaa'}, {'abstract': 'The East Village has long been associated with the counterculture and cheap housing, but much of that history has disappeared as home prices and retail rents have escalated.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/realestate/the-east-village-clings-to-a-colorful-past.html', 'snippet': 'The East Village has long been associated with the counterculture and cheap housing, but much of that history has disappeared as home prices and retail rents have escalated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the decades, living in the East Village has been about finding cheap housing. But there was much more to the equation. Historically a first stop for new immigrants, the neighborhood became associated with the counterculture, art and the punk movement in the 1970s, and living there served as a sort of rite of passage for people of a certain age and inclination.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The East Village Clings to a Colorful Past', 'kicker': 'Living In', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Clinging to a Colorful Past', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name':