Selling', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T16:01:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Farhad Manjoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Farhad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Manjoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d38091b-8250-5093-9a8a-892f99d69d1f', 'word_count': 1220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d38091b-8250-5093-9a8a-892f99d69d1f'}, {'abstract': 'Robert A. Caro reflects on writing his classic book about Robert Moses’s influence on New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/the-power-broker-40-years-later.html', 'snippet': 'Robert A. Caro reflects on writing his classic book about Robert Moses’s influence on New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Working on “The Power Broker” — researching it, writing it — took me seven years, and when I finished I was sure I never wanted to see it again. For a long time after it was published (in 1974, 40 years ago), for a couple of decades probably, I never opened the book. When, more recently, I occasionally did so, it was only to read a chapter or two to prepare myself for a lecture or an interview I was giving, certainly never to read it all the way through to the last line. This year, however, because PEN asked me to annotate the book, I did read it all the way through, and I was glad I did, because when I got to that final sentence, I suddenly remembered something I had long since forgotten: The moment when I thought of that sentence — and how much that moment meant to me.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '45', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Power Broker,’ 40 Years Later', 'kicker': 'Author’s Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Suddenly I Saw It Whole’ ‘The Power Broker,’ 40 years later.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Caro, Robert A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moses, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T16:02:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert A. Caro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Caro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cf5677c-67d1-5112-9f45-e13f2b3fa283', 'word_count': 1094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cf5677c-67d1-5112-9f45-e13f2b3fa283'}, {'abstract': 'Nationwide, many students are borrowing more than they understand. At elite colleges, though, the opposite may be true.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/11/upshot/what-students-dont-know-about-their-loans.html', 'snippet': 'Nationwide, many students are borrowing more than they understand. At elite colleges, though, the opposite may be true.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Student debt is a vexing issue, because it’s a legitimate problem, but not the problem that many people assume.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/11/upshot/11up-debt1-1418226996204/11up-debt1-1418226996204-articleLarge.png', 'height': 513, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/11/upshot/11up-debt1-1418226996204/11up-debt1-1418226996204-articleLarge.pn