esk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Elaine Constantine for The New York Times', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/9d9288dc-3dbc-5d3b-86b9-12856b5bd883', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/9d9288dc-3dbc-5d3b-86b9-12856b5bd883'}, {'abstract': 'With a boost from the 2010 Winter Olympics, Whistler-Blackcomb has grown up, drawing an international crowd and giving them plenty of options.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/travel/things-to-do-in-36-hours-in-whistler-british-columbia.html', 'snippet': 'With a boost from the 2010 Winter Olympics, Whistler-Blackcomb has grown up, drawing an international crowd and giving them plenty of options.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With apologies to Aspen, Whistler-Blackcomb in British Columbia really is North America’s most cosmopolitan ski resort. Walk down the Village Stroll. Hear the voices heading toward the gondola: Chinese, Russian, Japanese. Lean in, though, and you’ll also hear a Babel of young ski bums who give the place its real vibrancy: Poles. Aussies. Kiwis. Brits. South Africans. For a long time Whistler felt like just another cookie-cutter “insta-resort” (just add snow!), if an enormous one. But with time, and the 2010 Vancouver Winter Olympics, this place finally feels grown-up. At heart it’s the people — not terrain, or snowfall — that make a ski town great. And with those people have come more interesting things to do, and places to eat.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Whistler, British Columbia', 'kicker': 'Weekend Itinerary', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Solomon, Christopher', 'rank': 1, 'majo