, 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb5edc8b-237a-5697-87a2-6be2eb1422df', 'word_count': 964, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb5edc8b-237a-5697-87a2-6be2eb1422df'}, {'abstract': 'In this lesson we offer three different teaching ideas to engage students in the debate over immigration policy in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/10/border-politics-debating-immigration-policy/', 'snippet': 'In this lesson we offer three different teaching ideas to engage students in the debate over immigration policy in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While America wrestles with how to fix its “broken” immigration system, an estimated 11 million undocumented immigrants live, work and raise families in the United States. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Border Politics: Debating Immigration Policy', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T19:15:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Marshall and Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marshall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9cf83959-dcea-526b-86e1-e6c7d8cc7cec', 'word_count': 2068, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9cf83959-dcea-526b-86e1-e6c7d8cc7cec'}, {'abstract': 'Never forget.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/10/american-evil-2/', 'snippet': 'Never forget.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the Bush administration fades away in the rearview mirror, my sense is that many people — even liberals — are forgetting what it was really like. It becomes, in memory, just another administration whose policies you disapprove of, like the reign of Bush the elder.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'American Evil', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T19:15:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/394c52c5-0c85-5ffc-b44c-f38ea71d4911', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/394c52c5-0c85-5ffc-b44c-f38ea71d4911'}, {'abstract': 'The United States cannot afford to get caught up in another endless, all-consuming conflict.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/11/opinion/authorizing-military-force-must-have-limits.html', 'snippet': 'The United States cannot afford to get caught up in another endless, all-consuming conflict.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly five months and 1,100 airstrikes into the American-led war against the Islamic State, Congress has barely begun to fulfill its constitutional war-making responsibilities. The Senate Foreign Relations Committee on Tuesday belatedly opened debate on legislation that would authorize the use of force, but there’s no expectation that the work can be finished before the session ends on Thursday. That means it will be put off at least until January, when the new Congress convenes. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/11/opinion/11thu1/11thu1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/11/opinion/11thu1/11thu1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/11/opinion/11thu1/11thu1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/11/opinion/11thu1/11thu1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1338, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/11/opinion/11thu1/11thu1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/11/opinion/11thu1/11thu1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/11/opinion/11thu1/11thu1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rules of Engagement, Still Missing', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rules of Engagement, Still Missing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerry, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T19:15:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/9af0311a-2276-52ee-830e-a8506169df40', 'word_count': 982, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9af0311a-2276-52ee-830e-a8506169df40'}, {'abstract': '“Unspeakable” is a collection of Meghan Daum’s essays about herself and her family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/11/books/unspeakable-essays-by-meghan-daum.html', 'snippet': '“Unspeakable” is a collection of Meghan Daum’s essays about herself and her family.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Once upon a time, in the early 1990s,” Meghan Daum writes in “The Unspeakable,” her new essay collection, Generation X was “the cohort people were interested in.” The author, 44, is squarely a member of that generation, never more clearly than when she asks herself, “How did I get to be middle aged without really growing up?”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/10/books/10daum-cover/10daum-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 870, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/10/books/10daum-cover/10daum-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 870}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/10/books/10daum-cover/10daum-cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 707, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/10/books/10daum-cover/10daum-cover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1413, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail'