paragraph': 'Broadway got lost in the stars this year. It seemed that everywhere you looked — at least in that consortium of temples, er, theaters around Times Square dedicated to the adoration of the boldface — there was yet another celebrity, using his or her magnetism to draw gullible audiences and, as often as not, upset the balance of a perfectly good drama. It is fitting that the fall’s runaway hit, Terence McNally’s “It’s Only a Play,” was an overextended sketch in which famous people portrayed famous people talking about famous people.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14BRANTLEY1/14BRANTLEY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14BRANTLEY1/14BRANTLEY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 409}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14BRANTLEY1/14BRANTLEY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14BRANTLEY1/14BRANTLEY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1397, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14BRANTLEY1/14BRANTLEY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14BRANTLEY1/14BRANTLEY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14BRANTLEY1/14BRANTLEY1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ben Brantley’s Best Theater of 2014: ‘An Octoroon,’ ‘On the Town’ and More', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Star Turns, Yes; Celebrity, Not So Much', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T20:55:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/041483d8-c288-5fb7-bc16-31e0168fe985', 'word_count': 999, 'uri': 'nyt://article/041483d8-c288-5fb7-bc16-31e0168fe985'}, {'abstract': 'A change in Billboards’s album chart could shake up its rankings. But not this week.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/10/in-a-new-chart-regime-taylor-swift-still-reigns/', 'snippet': 'A change in Billboards’s album chart could shake up its rankings. But not this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Taylor Swift is No. 1 on Billboard’s album chart yet again, easily beating challenges from AC/DC and the a cappella group Pentatonix to notch a fifth week at the top.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In a New Chart Regime, Taylor Swift Still Reigns', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'However You Count, Swift Still Tops', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swift, Taylor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T20:56:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68b2a1b6-7e4f-581f-b5a8-9696ce7f2517', 'word_count': 431, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68b2a1b6-7e4f-581f-b5a8-9696ce7f2517'}, {'abstract': 'Charles Isherwood’s Top 10 in theater reflects a year noteworthy for the diversity of its subject matter and for the originality of its writing and staging.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/theater/charles-isherwoods-best-theater-of-2014-father-comes-home-from-the-wars-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Charles Isherwood’s Top 10 in theater reflects a year noteworthy for the diversity of its subject matter and for the originality of its writing and staging.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The older you get, the quicker Top 10 time seems to arrive. The past year in theater seemed more than usually breathless, but the rewards were many, sometimes seemingly constant. An exciting new play one month, a revitalized Broadway musical the next, and always what astonishes me most: the breadth and depth of the acting talent to be seen on New York stages. All my choices, and many shows that didn’t make the final cut, left me immensely gratified by the dedication and sometimes necessary fearlessness of New York’s theater actors.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14ISHERWOOD2/14ISHERWOOD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 356, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14ISHERWOOD2/14ISHERWOOD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 356}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14ISHERWOOD2/14ISHERWOOD2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 608, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14ISHERWOOD2/14ISHERWOOD2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1216, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14ISHERWOOD2/14ISHERWOOD2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14ISHERWOOD2/14ISHERWOOD2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14ISHERWOOD2/14ISHERWOOD2-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Charles Isherwood’s Best Theater of 2014: ‘Father Comes Home From the Wars’ and More', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dazzling Diversity in a Briskly Moving Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T21:02:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c17180f-0901-5b45-9d03-fce1eca4bc27', 'word_count': 949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c17180f-0901-5b45-9d03-fce1eca4bc27'}, {'abstract': 'The founder of Gawker Media said he would remain the company’s chief executive, but recent failures had shown he needed more support.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/11/business/nick-denton-reorganizes-gawker-to-share-managerial-responsibilities.html', 'snippet': 'The founder of Gawker Media said he would remain the company’s chief executive, but recent failures had shown he needed more support.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a candid memo that pointed a finger at some of his own mistakes, the founder of Gawker Media, Nick Denton, announced on Wednesday that he was stepping down as president and would be sharing managerial responsibilities with a board of six managing partners. The move was in part because of the company’s editorial failures in the last year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nick Denton Reorganizes Gawker to Share Managerial Responsibilities', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Denton, Nick', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gawker Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Joel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-10T21:02:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leslie Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leslie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3815c464-496a-5d44-93d4-2572d8a78869', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3815c464-496a-5d44-93d4-2572d8a78869'}, {'abstract': 'There’s a magic number when it comes to European copyright law. Hence the rush to release outtak