s power to discipline abusive officers is troublesome. Gov. Andrew Cuomo should veto it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/11/opinion/police-discipline-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'A bill that would undercut the police commissioner’s power to discipline abusive officers is troublesome. Gov. Andrew Cuomo should veto it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Union negotiators in New York have a well-known political trick: If they don’t succeed at the bargaining table, they try again in Albany. New York’s Legislature is notoriously easy for the big unions to manipulate, especially in the last frantic hours of any session. This year, for example, legislators passed a bill on police misconduct that would make it far more difficult for New York City’s police commissioner, William Bratton, to discipline officers under his command. Instead, any disciplinary matter would be subject to collective bargaining rules, which means that the final decision would be up to the police officers’ union, not the commissioner.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Police Discipline in New York', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Police Discipline in New York', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bratton, William J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collective Bargaining', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-11T01:57:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/887d6af4-7186-5724-92f7-76942871bcd3', 'word_count': 354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/887d6af4-7186-5724-92f7-76942871bcd3'}, {'abstract': 'An amendment in the spending bill would block the legalization of marijuana in the District of Columbia, rebuffing the will of its residents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/11/opinion/congresss-double-edged-marijuana-stance.html', 'snippet': 'An amendment in the spending bill would block the legalization of marijuana in the District of Columbia, rebuffing the will of its residents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A bold stride in the popular campaign to legalize marijuana — an amendment blocking federal interference with states that allow medical marijuana — has been written into the bipartisan budget spending bill that’s now being rushed through Congress. At the same time, this clear victory for the pro-marijuana movement nationally has been coupled with Congress’s outrageous rebuff of the will of District of Columbia residents, who voted overwhelmingly last month to join the growing state move to legalize small amounts of recreational marijuana. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Congress’s Double-Edged Marijuana Stance', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Congress’s Double-Edged Marijuana Stance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marijuana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Drug Enforcement Administration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medical Marijuana', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-11T01:58:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7281016-deed-515e-a623-aca1edc585f4', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7281016-deed-515e-a623-aca1edc585f4'}, {'abstract': 'Among the information spilled forth in a breach of Sony’s studio, perhaps nothing has riveted Hollywood more than a humiliating email exchange about Angelina Jolie.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/11/business/media/emails-from-hacking-reveal-sonys-dirty-laundry.html', 'snippet': 'Among the information spilled forth in a breach of Sony’s studio, perhaps nothing has riveted Hollywood more than a humiliating email exchange about Angelina Jolie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Salaries of its top executives. Unpublished scripts. Sensitive contracts. Aliases that stars use to check into hotels.\xa0', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/11/business/sony/sony-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 364, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/11/business/sony/sony-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 364}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/11/business/sony/sony-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 621, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': '