, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a830d01-c74d-5cda-bd82-f45ee3298a56', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a830d01-c74d-5cda-bd82-f45ee3298a56'}, {'abstract': 'Norman Mailer’s 1960 paean to then-presidential candidate John F. Kennedy is the first in the publisher’s series of special editions of iconic long-form nonfiction from the 1960s and ’70s.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/11/norman-mailer-new-journalism-kennedy/', 'snippet': 'Norman Mailer’s 1960 paean to then-presidential candidate John F. Kennedy is the first in the publisher’s series of special editions of iconic long-form nonfiction from the 1960s and ’70s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the 1960 presidential election, the writer Norman Mailer saw in John F. Kennedy the embodiment of the “hipster” values he believed could revitalize a stagnant America. Consequently, Mailer penned a lyrical 14,000-word paean to Kennedy entitled “Superman Comes to the Supermarket,” published in Esquire that same year. This week, Taschen has reissued Mailer’s iconic essay — one of the early masterpieces of so-called “New Journalism” — in a beautifully illustrated new edition\xa0($150).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/11/t-magazine/11jfk-mcauley-1/11jfk-mcauley-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/11/t-magazine/11jfk-mcauley-1/11jfk-mcauley-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/11/t-magazine/11jfk-mcauley-1/11jfk-mcauley-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/11/