ar. When I heard that his show at the Joyce Theater in June was to be titled “OM,” I got nervous. Though Mr. Glover is a great artist, his theatrical choices can be ill considered. Much about the show proved those worries to be justified: On the candle-bedecked stage, made into a shrine with photos of the Dalai Lama and departed tap masters, superfluous women did yoga.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14SEIBERT/14SEIBERT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14SEIBERT/14SEIBERT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14SEIBERT/14SEIBERT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14SEIBERT/14SEIBERT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1404, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14SEIBERT/14SEIBERT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14SEIBERT/14SEIBERT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14SEIBERT/14SEIBERT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bring In the Taps, Bring In the Claps', 'kicker': 'The Year in Culture', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bring In the Taps, Bring In the Claps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glover, Savion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Molina, Rocio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-11T21:35:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Seibert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seibert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/300186a7-9125-5179-9624-1d90d555b062', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/300186a7-9125-5179-9624-1d90d555b062'}, {'abstract': 'In 2014, female choreographers thrived, from Dana Michel to Gisèle Vienne.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/arts/dance/a-surprising-year-for-female-choreographers.html', 'snippet': 'In 2014, female choreographers thrived, from Dana Michel to Gisèle Vienne.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the ballet world, there is Twyla Tharp and then there are none — meaning female choreographers of note. At least that’s what it seems like. (Thank heavens for her “Bach Partita” at American Ballet Theater.) Fortunately, this says less about those who create dances than those who commission them. Ballet Theater and New York City Ballet can keep Liam Scarlett all to themselves: In 2014, female dance makers thrived, from the American premiere of the Canadian choreographer Dana Michel’s “Yellow Towel” to a revival by Pina Bausch, who died in 2009 but was kind enough to leave some gems behind.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'im