hwarted-counterfeiters-.html', 'snippet': 'A currency printing mold from Benjamin Franklin, and some mighty precious hardware.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Benjamin Franklin’s Philadelphia printing shop made plaster molds from pressed sage leaves to create metal stamps for marking foliage patterns on Colonial currency. The distinctive contours of leaf spines, stems and veins were meant to thwart counterfeiters, and Franklin’s workers managed to keep the casting technique a secret that has puzzled modern scholars, too.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/12/arts/12ANTIQUES3/ANTIQUES3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 793, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/12/arts/12ANTIQUES3/ANTIQUES3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 793}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/12/arts/12ANTIQUES3/ANTIQUES3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 775, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/12/arts/12ANTIQUES3/ANTIQUES3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1550, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/12/arts/12ANTIQUES3/ANTIQUES3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/12/arts/12ANTIQUES3/ANTIQUES3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/12/arts/12ANTIQUES3/ANTIQUES3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height