abstract': 'Children sing out for Mother Earth in “Saving My Tomorrow,” Monday night on HBO.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/arts/television/mother-earths-young-helpers.html', 'snippet': 'Children sing out for Mother Earth in “Saving My Tomorrow,” Monday night on HBO.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I used to think that no one would listen to you unless you were a grown-up, but then I realized, well, I really don’t have time to grow up,” Ta’Kaiya Blaney says with conviction. “And so I decided I’m going to try and fight for my future just as I am and just as the age that I am.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE9/14GUIDE9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE9/14GUIDE9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE9/14GUIDE9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE9/14GUIDE9-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE9/14GUIDE9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE9/14GUIDE9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE9/14GUIDE9-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mother Earth’s Young Helpers', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mother Earth’s Young Helpers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:27:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a10e4efa-d698-5e6b-a931-8e48b2be1806', 'word_count': 190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a10e4efa-d698-5e6b-a931-8e48b2be1806'}, {'abstract': 'This is your last chance to see American Ballet Theater’s “The Nutcracker,” choreographed by Alexei Ratmansky, in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/arts/dance/nutcracker-moves-to-milder-climes.html', 'snippet': 'This is your last chance to see American Ballet Theater’s “The Nutcracker,” choreographed by Alexei Ratmansky, in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the last scene of the first act of American Ballet Theater’s “The Nutcracker,” choreographed by Alexei Ratmansky, a gentle snow suddenly turns into a blizzard, a thrilling dark detour in this acclaimed production. But after last year’s harsh winter, Ballet Theater has had enough. Or perhaps after five years, it has been unable to find a real toehold in this city’s crowded Nutcracker marketplace. Either way, the company is off to Southern California: Beginning next year, Ballet Theater’s “The Nutcracker” will become an annual tradition at the Segerstrom Center for the Arts in Costa Mesa. Which means this is your last chance to see Mr. Ratmansky’s astute interpretation of the holiday classic in New York. All the ingredients are there: the opening party, the Christmas tree on steroids, a world tour of cultural stereotypes. But the great adventure at the heart of this version is the journey from adolescence to adulthood, dramatized with touching innocence and wonder. (Through Dec. 21, Brooklyn Academy of Music, 30 Lafayette Avenue, at Ashland Place, Fort Greene; 718-636-4100, bam.org.) ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 553, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 553}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 829, 'width': 899, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 829, 'width': 899, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Nutcracker’ Moves to Milder Climes', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Nutcracker’ Moves to Milder Climes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:27:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Schaefer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaefer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b84c3533-7d28-5502-9f3f-9ef67fea226e', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b84c3533-7d28-5502-9f3f-9ef67fea226e'}, {'abstract': 'Contemporary fiber art has a history of being set apart from — and by implication beneath — painting and sculpture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/arts/design/fiber-art-long-way-from-the-loom.html', 'snippet': 'Contemporary fiber art has a history of being set apart from — and by implication beneath — painting and sculpture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Contemporary fiber art has a history of being set apart from — and by implication beneath — painting and sculpture. It’s been decades since the last major museum show devoted to it, but even a quick tour of “Fiber: Sculpture 1960-Present” at the Institute of Contemporary Art, Boston, tells us what we’ve been missing. If you’re thinking braided rugs and samplers, think again. Much of the work in this survey of 33 artists is spectacularly space filling. A suspended three-dimensional sculpture of knotted sisal by the Croatian artist Jagoda Buic is as big as a ballroom chandelier. A new work by the great Sheila Hicks pours down, a Niagara of silk and linen cords, from the ceiling. Some of the quietest things are the most beautiful, like Lenore Tawney’s revolutionary open-weave “Dark River” from 1961. A gorgeous yarn piece by Sheila Pepe that rises and falls with the museum’s elevator, is moving in every sense. (Through Jan. 4, icaboston.org. The exhibition then travels to the Wexner Center for the Arts in Columbus, Ohio, and the Des Moines Art Center.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE7/14GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE7/14GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE7/14GUIDE7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE7/14GUIDE7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE7/14GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE7/14GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/arts/14GUIDE7/14GUIDE7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiber Art: Long Way From the Loom', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fiber Art: Long Way From the Loom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:27:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccefe93a-c575-54d1-bf4c-b670695e85d5', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccefe93a-c575-54d1-bf4c-b670695e85d5'}, {'abstract': 'The comedian and actress Joy Behar lives in a venerable building on the Upper West Side.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/realestate/joy-behars-big-mouth-and-her-apartment.html', 'snippet': 'The comedian and actress Joy Behar lives in a venerable building on the Upper West Side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Moving to a new apartment is an ideal opportunity to make a dream reality. With all those extra closets, the congenitally messy can finally impose order. Provided an airy new kitchen, the committed defroster can become an expert cook. And the sprawling living room lets the socially stifled get serious about entertaining.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/realestate/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/realestate/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/realestate/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/realestate/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/realestate/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/realestate/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/realestate/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6/20141214-LOVE-slide-Q9J6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Joy Behar’s Psychodrama and Her Apartment', 'kicker': 'What I Love', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Quiet Apartment in a Loud Neighborhood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Behar, Joy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper West Side (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:30:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3922d1a0-ccdc-5a02-8c1b-7ad0bba8e9df', 'word_count': 989, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3922d1a0-ccdc-5a02-8c1b-7ad0bba8e9df'}, {'abstract': 'Some think that the West is pushing too hard on Russia — and missing a complete picture of Putin’s motivations.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/12/does-the-u-s-need-a-new-approach-to-putin/', 'snippet': 'Some think that the West is pushing too hard on Russia — and missing a complete picture of Putin’s motivations.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Conventional wisdom — or conventional hope — among many of the people who would like to see the end of the Putin regime has long been that a turn for the worse in the Russian economy will make the moneyed elite turn on the Russian president,” Masha Gessen writes at The New York Times. “Journalists, pundits and Mr. Putin’s political opponents in Russia have predicted that Western sanctions and the economic disaster they hasten will result in a coup d’état staged by oligarchs. There is just one problem with that argument: There are no oligarchs anymore.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/12/opinion/nyto-putin/nyto-putin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/12/opinion/nyto-putin/nyto-putin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/12/opinion/nyto-putin/nyto-putin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'capti