bris. Scattered among the ruins of the vault, shrouded in clouds of dust and crumbled brick, were frescoes by Giotto and Cimabue that had stood since the late 13th century. Four people died, and so did any hope for a complete restoration.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BECKER1/07BECKER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 604, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BECKER1/07BECKER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 604}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BECKER1/07BECKER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1018, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BECKER1/07BECKER1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2012, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BECKER1/07BECKER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BECKER1/07BECKER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/books/review/07BECKER1/07BECKER1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Basilica of St. Francis in Assisi’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Divine Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malafarina, Gianfranco', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:37:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alida Becker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alida', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Becker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c328e43-2377-588a-b493-92f510e01d03', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c328e43-2377-588a-b493-92f510e01d03'}, {'abstract': 'A 19th-century scandal over dumping raw sewage into the Thames.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/dirty-old-london-by-lee-jackson.html', 'snippet': 'A 19th-century scandal over dumping raw sewage into the Thames.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1827, in a pamphlet called “The Dolphin,” a British journalist named John Wright described the source from which the Grand Junction Water Works Company was drawing its supply for the city of London. His revelation caused a scandal. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14JBAKER/14jbaker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 486, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14JBAKER/14jbaker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 486}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14JBAKER/14jbaker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 829, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14JBAKER/14jbaker-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1326, 'width': 1637, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14JBAKER/14jbaker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14JBAKER/14jbaker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14JBAKER/14jbaker-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Dirty Old London,’ by Lee Jackson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Old Filth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Lee (Author)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:37:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jo Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/16ccfdf9-12b0-5d08-82b9-da8fc77f164c', 'word_count': 925, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16ccfdf9-12b0-5d08-82b9-da8fc77f164c'}, {'abstract': '“The Beatles \xadLyrics,” No. 15 on the hardcover nonfiction list, includes handwritten drafts, including crossouts and false starts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': '“The Beatles \xadLyrics,” No. 15 on the hardcover nonfiction list, includes handwritten drafts, including crossouts and false starts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yeah Yeah Yeah: Shortly before it imploded earlier this month, the venerable old magazine — sorry, “vertically integrated digital media company” — The New Republic observed its centennial with a list of the 20th century’s leading thinkers across a range of disciplines: the top philosopher, the top economist, the top diplomat and so on. John Lennon and Paul McCartney did not win in the songwriting category; that honor went to Bob Dylan instead. But they were among the runners-up, and in its citation to explain why, the new New Republic made the kind of gaffe the old New Republic would probably have avoided: Two of the three Beatles songs it alluded to weren’t written by Lennon or McCartney. (“There was something in the way they came together,” the citation read, “the way they moved, the way they twisted and shouted.” But George Harrison wrote “Something,” while “Twist and Shout” isn’t a Beatles original at all; it was written by Phil Medley and Bert Berns. The one song the magazine got right, more or less, was “Come Together.”) The Beatles fare better in a new book by their longtime Boswell, Hunter Davies, whose collection “The Beatles \xadLyrics” enters the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 15. In gathering as many handwritten drafts as he could, including crossouts and false starts, Davies traces the evolution of some of rock ‘n’ roll’s most enduring songs. “The further we get from them,” he writes of the Beatles in his introduction, “the bigger they become.” Davies’s book has been greeted with almost religious devotion in some quarters. “When the lyrics, like those for ‘The Word’ and ‘Baby You’re a Rich Man,’ include multi\xadcolored doodlings by a presumably stoned author,” Andy Gill wrote in the British newspaper The Independent recently, “they’re like the primitivist equivalent of illuminated manuscripts.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14insidelist/14insidelist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 455, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14insidelist/14insidelist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 455, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14insidelist/14insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14insidelist/14insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14insidelist/14insidelist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:38:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b435abd9-5a30-5adf-a7b1-30fddafb8017', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b435abd9-5a30-5adf-a7b1-30fddafb8017'}, {'abstract': 'A. N. Wilson’s biography of the British monarch who reigned for more than six decades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/victoria-a-life-by-a-n-wilson.html', 'snippet': 'A. N. Wilson’s biography of the British monarch who reigned for more than six decades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1819, when the future Queen Victoria was born, few observers could have predicted that she would become the subject of several hundred biographies. A. N. Wilson’s addition to this library, “Victoria: A Life,” is a story of success against what looked at first like overwhelming odds. His Cinderella-like heroine grew up fatherless, a poor relation surrounded by warring court factions. Still a teenager when the death of successive relatives left her to inherit her uncle’s throne, Victoria was faced with a series of fragile coalition governments, labor unrest at home, a famine in Ireland, revolutions on the other side of the Channel, a spectacularly mismanaged war in the Crimea — and that’s just in the first two decades. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14PRICE/14price-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 788, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14PRICE/14price-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 788}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14PRICE/14price-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 780, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14PRICE/14price-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1559, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14PRICE/14PRICE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14PRICE/14PRICE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14PRICE/14PRICE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Victoria: A Life,’ by A. N. Wilson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Long Lived the Queen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, A N', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Victoria, Queen of Great Britain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:39:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leah Price', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Price', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/deb3ce6e-ec4e-5fe9-90de-d89923e58ea8', 'word_count': 1275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/deb3ce6e-ec4e-5fe9-90de-d89923e58ea8'}, {'abstract': 'In Frederick Barthelme’s novel, residents of a Texas condo development are meeting untimely ends at an alarming rate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/there-must-be-some-mistake-by-frederick-barthelme.html', 'snippet': 'In Frederick Barthelme’s novel, residents of a Texas condo development are meeting untimely ends at an alarming rate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re hoping to get to know your neighbors in Forgetful Bay, a condo development near the Texas Gulf Coast, you’d better act quickly, since they’re going belly up with remarkable frequency. Consider the fate of the nail-salon tycoon, Mr. Ng, who flipped his Mercedes, which promptly burst into flames, or the ex-Marine Duncan Parker, who was planning to lose his \xadAmazon-size wife but by the time the last bullet was fired ended up losing himself. Then, of course, there are the perfectly “normal” deaths brought about by bad hearts and other medical conditions, the kind that were responsible for the collapse of the neighbor found in his car “with the door open and one foot out on the concrete of the garage floor,” with “no signs of violence of any kind.” But it’s not easy distinguishing a normal death from an abnormal one in Forgetful Bay, where only one thing is ever certain — if you’re looking to move in, several of the nicer units should be available real soon.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘There Must Be Some Mistake,’ by Frederick Barthelme', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'There Goes the Neighborhood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barthelme, Frederick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'There Must Be Some Mistake (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:39:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Bradfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0c85bda-123b-59da-86e1-b3878b5e3eb2', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0c85bda-123b-59da-86e1-b3878b5e3eb2'}, {'abstract': 'Howard Jacobson’s love story is set in a dystopian world where history is suppressed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/j-by-howard-jacobson.html', 'snippet': 'Howard Jacobson’s love story is set in a dystopian world where history is suppressed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Howard Jacobson’s “J” opens with a parable, or, as the book terms it, an “argument.” A wolf and a tarantula are comparing modes of taking down prey, with the wolf’s rapacious efficiency pitted against the spider’s patience. As is the way with such tales, the wolf is undone by his own skill, polishing off all his quarry until he’s forced to eat his family and finally himself. “Moral: Always leave a little on your plate.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SPEKTOR/1214-bks-Spektor-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SPEKTOR/1214-bks-Spektor-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 511}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SPEKTOR/1214-bks-Spektor-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 871, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SPEKTOR/1214-bks-Spektor-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1295, 'width': 1522, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SPEKTOR/14SPEKTOR-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SPEKTOR/14SPEKTOR-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SPEKTOR/14SPEKTOR-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘J,’ by Howard Jacobson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Never Forget', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobson, Howard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'J (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:39:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Specktor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Specktor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/536a0423-a5b9-597f-91f3-7f34df68dfcc', 'word_count': 1415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/536a0423-a5b9-597f-91f3-7f34df68dfcc'}, {'abstract': 'Andrew Solomon reviews a new book by Jay Griffiths, who says contemporary society denies children the freedoms of space, time and the natural world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/a-country-called-childhood-by-jay-griffiths.html', 'snippet': 'Andrew Solomon reviews a new book by Jay Griffiths, who says contemporary society denies children the freedoms of space, time and the natural world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You know less than you think you do. The constant reinforcement of that sorry idea has become a drumbeat under parenting, as advice books of every kind pullulate like toadstools after a storm. Such literature sets out to refocus your daily life with your child, usually with proscriptive rebukes and optimistic exercises — with easy-sounding answers that are often impossible to enact. Anyone who has raised a child will know how assaultive the abundance of such parenting advice can feel, how dreary it is to be told constantly that if you only did (or, indeed, had done) something slightly different, your child’s problems would evanesce, and you would have, through the alchemy of nurture, a child who is happy / well behaved / nonviolent / good at math / successful / self-\xadmotivated / popular / thin. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SOLOMON/1214-bks-SOLOMON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SOLOMON/1214-bks-SOLOMON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 670}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SOLOMON/1214-bks-SOLOMON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 917, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SOLOMON/1214-bks-SOLOMON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1574, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SOLOMON/1214-bks-SOLOMON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SOLOMON/1214-bks-SOLOMON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14SOLOMON/1214-bks-SOLOMON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Country Called Childhood,’ by Jay Griffiths', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Go Play Outside', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Solomon, Andrew (1963- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Griffiths, Jay', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:40:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9f13b25-6e8d-54b0-b92d-f4ce38f0bd46', 'word_count': 1907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9f13b25-6e8d-54b0-b92d-f4ce38f0bd46'}, {'abstract': 'A study of how race and culture intertwine in our diversifying society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/who-we-be-by-jeff-chang.html', 'snippet': 'A study of how race and culture intertwine in our diversifying society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The dramatic changes spurred by the civil rights \xadmovement and other 1960s social upheavals are often chronicled as a time line of catalytic legal victories that ended anti-black segregation. Jeff Chang’s “Who We Be: The Colorization of America” claims that cultural changes were equally important in transforming American society, and that both the legal and cultural forms of desegregation faced a sustained hostile response that continues today. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14ROSE/14rose-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 807, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14ROSE/14rose-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 807}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14ROSE/14rose-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 762, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14ROSE/14rose-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1557, 'width': 1158, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14ROSE/14ROSE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14ROSE/14ROSE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14ROSE/14ROSE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Who We Be,’ by Jeff Chang', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Outside the White Space', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chang, Jeff', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:40:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tricia Rose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c26e634-fad6-56a3-837b-cd971fed5a8f', 'word_count': 827, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c26e634-fad6-56a3-837b-cd971fed5a8f'}, {'abstract': 'In Tod Goldberg’s novel, a synagogue becomes a front for organized crime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/gangsterland-by-tod-goldberg.html', 'snippet': 'In Tod Goldberg’s novel, a synagogue becomes a front for organized crime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For many of us, “The Godfather,” book or movie, ushered us into a second boyhood, teaching that the incorrigible vitality of a first-rate gangster story could temporarily inoculate us against adult sanity. For readers of a like mind, Tod Goldberg’s “Gangsterland” will arrive as a gloriously original Mafia novel: 100 percent unhinged about the professionally unhinged. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RUBIN/1214-bks-RUBIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 320, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RUBIN/1214-bks-RUBIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 320}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RUBIN/1214-bks-RUBIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 546, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RUBIN/1214-bks-RUBIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 1499, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RUBIN/1214-bks-RUBIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RUBIN/1214-bks-RUBIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14RUBIN/1214-bks-RUBIN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Gangsterland,’ by Tod Goldberg', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Rabbi Is a Hit Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldberg, Tod', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-12T16:41:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Rubin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb3541a1-d742-5826-a5c9-2bc7140a9072', 'word_count': 843, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb3541a1-d742-5826-a5c9-2bc7140a9072'}, {'abstract': 'The historian Jill Lepore examines the life and significance of Wonder Woman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/books/review/jill-lepores-secret-history-of-wonder-woman.html', 'snippet': 'The historian Jill Lepore examines the life and significance of Wonder Woman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Who could save America from fascism in the 1940s? Feminists, according to Wonder Woman’s creator, William Moulton Marston, in the form of Sapphists, coeds and Amazons. Marston created Wonder Woman to demonstrate how “feminine rule” would look and to cash in on the explosive popularity of comics, read by staggering numbers of people in the 1930s. “By 1939,” Jill Lepore writes, “almost every kid in the United States was reading comic books.” Marston estimated that upward of 40 million were hooked on the comics. Female superheroes were not especially popular, but Marston developed a patriotic (and erotic) formula that made Wonder Woman “the most popular female comic-book superhero of all time,” read by 10 million followers and rivaled only by Superman and Batman.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14KAPLAN/14kaplan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14KAPLAN/14kaplan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/books/review/14KAPLAN/14kaplan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201