here and then write a mass of brilliant clues that have no possibility of being wrong or offending anyone. Easy peasy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Well, Golly!', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-13T23:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/024ef3f3-ced5-5879-ab41-3e967be59238', 'word_count': 1031, 'uri': 'nyt://article/024ef3f3-ced5-5879-ab41-3e967be59238'}, {'abstract': 'Sergei I. Reuta, a Pentecostal minister, picked up a Kalashnikov rifle to volunteer with the Ukrainian Army, where he routinely held court at his post and cooked for his unit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/14/world/europe/a-pastors-turn-fighting-for-ukraine.html', 'snippet': 'Sergei I. Reuta, a Pentecostal minister, picked up a Kalashnikov rifle to volunteer with the Ukrainian Army, where he routinely held court at his post and cooked for his unit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PISKY, Ukraine — Sergei I. Reuta was in many ways typical of the 15,000 to 18,000 volunteers who, loosely organized in about 30 battalions, have emerged as the backbone of the Ukrainian military in the war in southeastern Ukraine.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/14/world/14volunteer/14volunteer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legac