document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bacb80e3-5f40-57cf-b822-89841c10931c', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bacb80e3-5f40-57cf-b822-89841c10931c'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/15/arts/whats-on-tv-monday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '7 P.M. (HBO) SAVING MY TOMORROW “I used to think that no one would listen to you unless you were a grown-up, but then I realized, well, I really don’t have time to grow up,” says Ta’Kaiya Blaney, a member of the Sliammon First Nation in British Columbia and an environmental activist since the age of 8. “And so I decided I’m going to try and fight for my future just as I am and just as the age that I am.” Ms. Blaney is one in a chorus of children, like those above, speaking and singing out for Mother Earth in this kaleidoscopic family special, created with the American Museum of Natural History, which smushes together science and animation, readings by Tina Fey, Liam Neeson and Neil deGrasse Tyson, and music by Pharrell Williams, Ziggy Marley and They Might Be Giants to address the perils of pollution, drilling and climate change. “We’re the ancestors of the many generations to come, and you know, we have a chance to do something and to change the world for the better,” says Ms. Blaney, now 13. “It can make a huge difference on the future.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Monday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s on TV Monday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-15T05:24:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television',