t of sight, at least as far as New York Times puzzle publishing is concerned.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Easy Chore', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-16T03:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57a37cb5-5b4f-5781-bfc2-8cc36571812c', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57a37cb5-5b4f-5781-bfc2-8cc36571812c'}, {'abstract': 'The BBC journalist’s latest work, “The South China Sea: The Struggle for Power in Asia,” addresses the history, politics and energy resources of the sea that has become central to China’s foreign policy.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/15/q-and-a-bill-hayton-on-growing-rivalries-in-the-south-china-sea/', 'snippet': 'The BBC journalist’s latest work, “The South China Sea: The Struggle for Power in Asia,” addresses the history, politics and energy resources of the sea that has become central to China’s foreign policy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bill Hayton, a longtime journalist with the BBC, has spent much of his career chronicling events in Southeast Asia and observing the growing rivalries in the region. His first book, “Vietnam: Rising Dragon” (Yale University Press, 2010), explored the authoritarian nature of the government and the human rights abuses that have accompanied the country’s economic rise.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/12