r': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-17T18:35:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6cad551-6893-512a-b6b1-da72878ed4b9', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6cad551-6893-512a-b6b1-da72878ed4b9'}, {'abstract': 'While the city shines brightest over the holidays, its allure endures with its canals, cobbled streets and hearty Alsatian cuisine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/travel/things-to-do-in-36-hours-in-strasbourg-france.html', 'snippet': 'While the city shines brightest over the holidays, its allure endures with its canals, cobbled streets and hearty Alsatian cuisine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Have you been naughty or nice? A self-proclaimed “Christmas Capital” — thanks to one of the oldest and largest Christmas markets in Europe — Strasbourg welcomes the virtuous and vice-loving alike to its December citywide extravaganza of gift bazaars, concerts, lights and mulled wine. But the city is hardly a holiday novelty. Nestled along France’s border with Germany, Strasbourg has been fawned over and fought over for centuries by the two nations, ping-ponging back and forth before returning to France at the end of World War II. The result is a fetching Franco-Teutonic core of cobbled traffic-free lanes, canals, half-timbered houses and spires where you can fill both your belly (with copious rustic Alsatian dishes) and your brain (with impressive art museums). Toss in the seat of the European Parliament, a lively bar scene and some winningly repurposed historical buildings — now elegant hotels and gastronomic havens — and you have an international city with year-round appeal.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Strasbourg, France', 'kicker': 'Weekend Itinerary', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '36 Hours in Strasbourg, France', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sherwood, Seth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Strasbourg (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-17T18:41:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Sherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4cb5ff2-96f4-59a3-8f18-2b01054462cb', 'word_count': 1620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4cb5ff2-96f4-59a3-8f18-2b01054462cb'}, {'abstract': 'The Times reporters Ashley Parker and Jonathan Martin write that President Obama’s shift on Cuba policy could fill in one of the most enduring fault lines in American politics and reshape the fight to win the vital battleground state of Florida.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2014/12/17/the-politics-of-the-shift-in-policy-on-cuba/', 'snippet': 'The Times reporters Ashley Parker and Jonathan Martin write that President Obama’s shift on Cuba policy could fill in one of the most enduring fault lines in American politics and reshape the fight to win the vital battleground state of Florida.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Change in Policy, Like Shift in Demographics, Could Alter Florida’s Political Map ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The P