{'original': 'By Michael Beschloss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beschloss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3db991b7-715f-581f-9825-04b4354a9124', 'word_count': 1014, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3db991b7-715f-581f-9825-04b4354a9124'}, {'abstract': 'As Mr. Colbert prepares for his last “Colbert Report” on Comedy Central, a summing-up of facts, numbers and spin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/18/arts/television/stephen-colbert-by-the-numbers.html', 'snippet': 'As Mr. Colbert prepares for his last “Colbert Report” on Comedy Central, a summing-up of facts, numbers and spin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A great man once said that he was no fan of reference books because they were “elitist, constantly telling us what is or isn’t true, or what did or didn’t happen.” On Thursday night, more than nine years and 1,440-plus episodes later, that comedic pundit, Stephen Colbert, and “The Colbert Report” on Comedy Central, will depart, along with Mr. Colbert’s dislike of facts and reality’s “well-known liberal bias.” And while some might say that a man with an identical name and face will become the host of CBS’s “Late Show” next year, it just won’t be the same Mr. Colbert. Until that time, we bid farewell to him with a look back at some of the facts and figures that made “The Colbert Report” memorable. Apologies for any elitism.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/18/arts/colbert1/colbert1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/18/arts/colbert1/colbert1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': Non