Homicides', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-18T15:29:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da3f1762-3e5a-5312-9f9b-fd75da704e3f', 'word_count': 1609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da3f1762-3e5a-5312-9f9b-fd75da704e3f'}, {'abstract': 'Why the fate of the “The Interview” promises to make Hollywood’s artistic problems worse.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/18/the-end-of-movies/', 'snippet': 'Why the fate of the “The Interview” promises to make Hollywood’s artistic problems worse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yesterday\xa0at Grantland, in a kind of harmonic convergence with Sony’s unprecedented cancellation of a major Christmas-season movie in response to pressure and threats from the North Korean-affiliated hackers who had already exposed various studio secrets, Mark Harris had an essay on the theme of Hollywood’s ongoing transformation from an industry invested in original storytelling and ad hoc experimentation, in which franchises and sequels exist to help “finance the rest of a studio’s lineup,” to a business in which “replication” and “reliability” are everything, and “a studio’s lineup is brands and franchises, and that’s it.” Harris has made versions of this argument before, as have others\xa0— it’s not exactly news that Hollywood is increasingly\xa0enslaved to the “pre-sold” property\xa0— but in this case he’s put together two grimly detailed documents, two release schedules\xa0showing the five-year plans that the major studios have built for themselves out of superhero\xa0movies (above all) and then also “Star Wars” sequels and “Harry Potter” arcana and reboots of everything from “Jurassic Park” to “Max Max” to “The Terminator” to “Beverly Hills Cop.” And to stare at these extraordinary lists of movies pre-planned entertainment properties, at “Aquaman” and “The Bourne Identity Five” and “Pirates of the Caribbean Whatever,” is to find oneself contemplating a kind of tentpole totalitarianism, a moviegoing future from which even the imagination will have no means of escape.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The End of Movies', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franco, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harris, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rogen, Seth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-18T15:35:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0ca57e6-cc98-53cb-9e68-7a107595a8b9', 'word_count': 635, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0ca57e6-cc98-53cb-9e68-7a107595a8b9'}, {'abstract': 'Some say the changes in United States-Cuba relations will be a boon to Internet access, but others worry decisions made in the United States will have little effect on Cubans’ Internet freedom.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/18/will-cuba-get-an-internet-boom/', 'snippet': 'Some say the changes in United States-Cuba relations will be a boon to Internet access, but others worry decisions made in the United States will have little effect on Cubans’ Internet freedom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the many changes to United\xa0States-Cuba relations announced Wednesday was a technological one: The United States government will now allow American telecommunications companies to build in and export to Cuba. To some, this\xa0means Cubans could see their Internet access improve radically. But others\xa0say decisions made in the United States may not have much effect on Cubans’ ability to get online — or on what happens when they do.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/18/opinion/nyto-internet/nyto-internet-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/18/opinion/nyto-internet/nyto-internet-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/18/opinion/nyto-internet/nyto-internet-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/18/opinion/nyto-internet/nyto-internet-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1976, 'width': 1995, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/18/opinion/nyto-internet/nyto-internet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/18/opinion/nyto-internet/nyto-internet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 