nt understanding what chronic-pain sufferers experience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every year brings celluloid surprises that few Oscar prognosticators see coming, usually in the form of vivid performances in small-scale independent films. This year’s batch includes “Still Alice” with Julianne Moore, who’s been garnering loads of awards buzz for the lead role, and “Cake,” starring Jennifer Aniston as Claire, an emotionally and physically shattered woman seeking respite in a sea of prescription painkillers. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/17/arts/aniston/aniston-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/17/arts/aniston/aniston-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 840}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/17/arts/aniston/aniston-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 732, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/17/arts/aniston/aniston-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1464, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/17/arts/aniston/aniston-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/17/arts/aniston/aniston-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/17/arts/aniston/aniston-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jennifer Aniston on ‘Cake’ and Empathy', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANISTON, JENNIFER', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-18T16:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f550c94f-d792-53db-a9f3-e9f71256ea0b', 'word_count': 834, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f550c94f-d792-53db-a9f3-e9f71256ea0b'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Two Soviet spies received long prison terms in the U.S. in 1964.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/18/1964-soviet-spies-get-30-years/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Two Soviet spies received long prison terms in the U.S. in 1964.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWARK, NJ. — An American engineer, and a Russian chauffeur convicted of conspiring to spy for the Soviet Union were sentenced to long prison terms today after protesting their innocence in court. They could have received death sentences. The engineer, John W. Butenko, 39, was sentenced to 30 years’ imprisonment on the espionage conspiracy charge and drew two five-year concurrent terms on other charges. A 20-year sentence was imposed on the Russian, Igor Ivanov, 34, on the espionage-conspiracy charge. He also was given five years, to be served concurrently, on a second count. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Dec. 19, 1964', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1964: Soviet Spies Get 30 Years', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-18T16:01:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/718c80b9-b8b7-5533-b496-50fa41146f3c', 'word_count': 103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/718c80b9-b8b7-5533-b496-50fa41146f3c'}, {'abstract': 'The provision suggested that a price-fixing investigation by the European Commission was gathering pace, years after the first raids were carried out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/19/business/daimler-eu-inquiry-into-truck-makers.html', 'snippet': 'The provision suggested that a price-fixing investigation by the European Commission was gathering pace, years after the first raids were carried out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRANKFURT — A European Union investigation into possible price fixing by Europe’s largest truck manufacturers appeared to be gathering momentum on Thursday after Daimler of Germany said it had set aside 600 million euros to cover possible costs resulting from the inquiry.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Daimler Sets Aside $748 Million Over E.U. Inquiry Into Truck Makers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Daimler Sets Aside Cash in Light of European Inquiry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keyword