tmas Day release of the action-comedy film “The Interview,” starring Seth Rogen and James Franco, which includes a scene depicting the leader of North Korea getting assassinated. The studio took the unusual step after hackers delivered vague but menacing threats against movie theaters. North Koreans have called the movie an “act of war.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/18/business/18sony-LN/18sony-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/18/business/18sony-LN/18sony-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/18/business/18sony-LN/18sony-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/18/business/18sony-LN/18sony-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1311, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/18/business/18sony-LN/18sony-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/18/business/18sony-LN/18sony-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/18/business/18sony-LN/18sony-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Was Sony Right to Cancel ‘The Interview’ After Receiving a Threat Against Theaters?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T10:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/832678ee-a7c1-561a-9fa0-bdd327f9270b', 'word_count': 500, 'uri': 'nyt://article/832678ee-a7c1-561a-9fa0-bdd327f9270b'}, {'abstract': 'Wanting to go beyond his usual Middle East haunts, Bryan Denton went looking for someplace exotic for his latest photo essay.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/19/striking-oil-in-a-dakota-boomtown/', 'snippet': 'Wanting to go beyond his usual Middle East haunts, Bryan Denton went looking for someplace exotic for his latest photo essay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bryan Denton considers himself “somewhat of an immigrant” in the Middle East, where he has spent pretty much his entire photographic career. He feels comfortable covering conflict — much of his work in the region looks at how violence changes cultures and societies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Striking Oil in a Dakota Boomtown', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Denton, Bryan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Press Photo Foundation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bakken Shale Field (ND)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Dakota', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date':