'Tanis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b2cc2f5-0067-50f3-b553-c37acc79f61d', 'word_count': 403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b2cc2f5-0067-50f3-b553-c37acc79f61d'}, {'abstract': 'To save your memories, sometimes it’s better just not to watch.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/19/how-to-survive-the-movie-apocalypse/', 'snippet': 'To save your memories, sometimes it’s better just not to watch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Consider this a supplement to yesterday’s piece on how the current fiasco surrounding “The Interview” will reinforce all of Hollywood’s worst contemporary instincts. (For once, George Clooney and I are on exactly the same page.) I’ve been reading reviews this week of “The Hobbit: The Battle of Five Armies,” the finale to a bloated trilogy that never should have been,\xa0and a number of them have\xa0done a good job discussing\xa0the differences between Peter Jackson’s choices in his “Lord of the Rings” adaptations and the choices he’s made while cashing in on and corrupting the real Tolkien trilogy’s slighter predecessor.\xa0(Sonny Bunch’s close-read of the final, blessedly CGI-free battle sequence in “The Fellowship of the Ring” is particularly good.) But I was struck by this depressing point in Chris Orr’s review\xa0of “Five Armies,” because it has relevance\xa0for a much wider range of contemporary movies:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How To Survive The Movie Apocalypse', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Peter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lucas, George', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, George (1945- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tolkien, J R R', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T15:12:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3e223f2-2efe-5531-a606-6268d93d3261', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3e223f2-2efe-5531-a606-6268d93d3261'}, {'abstract': 'A revamped version of “Gigi,” starring Vanessa Hudgens, will have its premiere at the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/19/sneak-peek-at-the-next-big-fashion-show-hint-vanessa-hudgens-is-in-it/', 'snippet': 'A revamped version of “Gigi,” starring Vanessa Hudgens, will have its premiere at the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'O.K., that title is a bit misleading, I admit it. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sneak Peek at the Next Big Fashion Show (Hint: Vanessa Hudgens Is in It)', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T15:21:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vanessa Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vanessa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42b111ba-eba4-5834-9d11-e6c280e32d14', 'word_count': 476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42b111ba-eba4-5834-9d11-e6c280e32d14'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/magazine/reply-all-the-12714-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRANCIUM KILLED MY GREAT-GREAT AUNT', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/07Cover_Type/07Cover_Type-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/07Cover_Type/07Cover_Type-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 731}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/07Cover_Type/07Cover_Type-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 842, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/07Cover_Type/07Cover_Type-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1683, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/07Cover_Type/07Cover_Type-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/07Cover_Type/07Cover_Type-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/07/magazine/07Cover_Type/07Cover_Type-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reply All: The 12.7.14 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 12.7.14 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T15:24:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23bc5f10-cbf2-564f-b682-1ae9c21e34da', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23bc5f10-cbf2-564f-b682-1ae9c21e34da'}, {'abstract': 'Applicant ambiguity; bidding peace; window undressing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/magazine/a-boy-named-dana.html', 'snippet': 'Applicant ambiguity; bidding peace; window undressing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have applied over email for internships with several high-tech companies in the Bay Area, and I suspect I have benefited from my potential employers’ incorrectly assuming that I am a woman. Many of these companies actively seek female applicants to balance out the field’s male dominance. Nowhere on my résumé do I state my gender, as that’s not typically done. Do I have an ethical obligation to mention my gender in the email? By refraining from doing so, am I taking advantage of a policy designed to counter social and institutional discrimination against women? Am I receiving some form of cosmic payback for having been given a woman’s name? DANA, CALIFORNIA', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/magazine/21ethicist/21ethicist-articleLarge-v3.png', 'height': 334, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/magazine/21ethicist/21ethicist-articleLarge-v3.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 334}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/magazine/21ethicist/21ethicist-jumbo-v3.png', 'height': 570, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': 