that rarely has an event lasting more than a night, has an eight-week installation at the New Museum of Contemporary Art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/arts/dance/a-museum-show-for-aunts-the-roving-dance-party.html', 'snippet': 'Aunts, a roving dance project that rarely has an event lasting more than a night, has an eight-week installation at the New Museum of Contemporary Art.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Are you going to Aunts?” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/arts/21AUNTS1/21AUNTS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/arts/21AUNTS1/21AUNTS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/arts/21AUNTS1/21AUNTS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/arts/21AUNTS1/21AUNTS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/arts/21AUNTS1/21AUNTS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/arts/21AUNTS1/21AUNTS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/arts/21AUNTS1/21AUNTS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Always Moving, Even in One Place ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Always Moving, Even in One Place', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Museum of Contemporary Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T17:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siobhan Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siobhan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea1af45d-14c0-5f43-8c7a-1aac964307fb', 'word_count': 1045, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea1af45d-14c0-5f43-8c7a-1aac964307fb'}, {'abstract': 'This novel’s 12-year-old heroine, growing up in 1940s New York, finds World War II a nuisance, until she meets a refugee.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/isabels-war-by-lila-perl.html', 'snippet': 'This novel’s 12-year-old heroine, growing up in 1940s New York, finds World War II a nuisance, until she meets a refugee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Any chronicle of World War II for the female middle schooler is bound to be measured against the gold standard of Anne Frank’s “The Diary of a Young Girl.” But I confess, when in my youth I first picked up my mother’s copy, a hallowed but yellowed Puffin edition brought over from her native England (her father, the composer Walter Leigh, was killed in the war when she was 5), I lacked the proper reverence. As if reading a Judy Blume novel, I fast-\xadforwarded, telltale pages crumbling, to Anne’s flirtation with Peter, to her menses. The naughty bits.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Isabel’s War,’ by Lila Perl', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At War With War', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perl, Lila', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T17:02:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2df9725-900e-5d48-aff6-e81f2d364e4a', 'word_count': 673, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2df9725-900e-5d48-aff6-e81f2d364e4a'}, {'abstract': 'I understand why the naughty list helps some parents. But for my anxious daughter, it’s the opposite of Christmas magic. Bless the mall Santa who understood that.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/19/why-we-dont-do-the-naughty-list/', 'snippet': 'I understand why the naughty list helps some parents. But for my anxious daughter, it’s the opposite of Christmas magic. Bless the mall Santa who understood that.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Right after we set up the Christmas tree, my 3-year-old daughter told me I was on the naughty list. I was on the naughty list because I told her to help me pick up her toys that were all over the floor. “Look, Mom,” she explained, “Santa doesn’t like you bossing me around. You won’t get presents if you talk to me like that.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/19/us/motherlode-no-elf/motherlode-no-elf-articleLarge.png', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/19/us/motherlode-no-elf/motherlode-no-elf-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/19/us/motherlode-no-elf/motherlode-no-elf-jumbo.png', 'height': 592, 'width': 896, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/19/us/motherlode-no-elf/motherlode-no-elf-superJumbo.png', 'height': 592, 'width': 896, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/19/us/motherlode-no-elf/motherlode-no-elf-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/19/us/motherlode-no-elf/motherlode-no-elf-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/19/us/motherlode-no-elf/motherlode-no-elf-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why We Don’t Do the Naughty List', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Santa Claus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T17:03:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lyz Lenz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lyz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lenz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb99d8ba-e719-5fc2-a0bb-d0a88b0725f2', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb99d8ba-e719-5fc2-a0bb-d0a88b0725f2'}, {'abstract': 'A high school senior grappling with her mother’s suicide is suddenly able to envision people’s pasts and futures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/glory-obriens-history-of-the-future-by-a-s-king.html', 'snippet': 'A high school senior grappling with her mother’s suicide is suddenly able to envision people’s pasts and futures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Near the midway point of her wickedly clever new novel, “Glory O’Brien’s History of the Future,” A. S. King puts this into the mouth of her 17-year-old narrator: “We form. We shine. We burn. Kapow.” The entire history of the universe, all 13 billion years of it, in seven little words. If only our high school history teachers had been this succinct.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Glory O’Brien’s History of the Future,’ by A. S. King', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seen It All', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, A S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T17:05:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Yancey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yancey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/befc4817-0371-56e8-a259-7a53f82156e8', 'word_count': 748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/befc4817-0371-56e8-a259-7a53f82156e8'}, {'abstract': 'Julianne Moore gives a wonderful performance in “Still Alice,” but