ular lifestyle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/living-the-secular-life-by-phil-zuckerman.html', 'snippet': 'As more Americans eschew religious affiliation, a sociologist examines the secular lifestyle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many years ago, when I was an innocent lamb making my first appearance on a right-wing radio talk show, the host asked, “If you don’t believe in God, what’s to stop you from committing murder?” I blurted out, “It’s never actually occurred to me to murder anyone.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21jacoby/21jacoby-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21jacoby/21jacoby-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21jacoby/21jacoby-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21jacoby/21jacoby-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21jacoby/21jacoby-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21jacoby/21jacoby-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21jacoby/21jacoby-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Living the Secular Life,’ by Phil Zuckerman', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Golden Rule', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacoby, Susan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerman, Phil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Living the Secular Life: New Answers to Old Questions (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Jacoby', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacoby', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fed99cc-5b2e-5e42-9738-236d1120ab3b', 'word_count': 929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fed99cc-5b2e-5e42-9738-236d1120ab3b'}, {'abstract': 'The second volume of Norton’s two-part anthology compiles texts and commentaries on Judaism, Christianity and Islam.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/the-norton-anthology-of-world-religions-volume-ii.html', 'snippet': 'The second volume of Norton’s two-part anthology compiles texts and commentaries on Judaism, Christianity and Islam.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At a time when religious faith is coming under intense scrutiny, “The Norton Anthology of World Religions” is presenting a documentary history of six major faiths with sufficient editorial explanation to make their major texts intelligible across the barriers of time and space. This second volume in the series is a textual overview of the three monotheisms — Judaism, Christianity and Islam — from the early scriptures to contemporary writings. It is presented as a journey of exploration, but any reader who hopes to emerge from this literary excursion with a clear-cut understanding of these religions will be disappointed — and that is the great strength of this book.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21armstrong-sub/21armstrong-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 138, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21armstrong-sub/21armstrong-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 138}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21armstrong-sub/21armstrong-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 235, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21armstrong-sub/21armstrong-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 1742, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21armstrong-sub/21armstrong-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21armstrong-sub/21armstrong-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21armstrong-sub/21armstrong-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Norton Anthology of World Religions: Volume II’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Articles of Faith', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miles, Jack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biale, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McAuliffe, Jane Dammen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cunningham, Lawrence S', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Norton Anthology of World Religions (Book)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Armstrong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Armstrong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/43cab428-0e6b-5bdd-b28f-3fccaa0558e1', 'word_count': 1157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43cab428-0e6b-5bdd-b28f-3fccaa0558e1'}, {'abstract': 'Merging neuroscience and philosophy, Evan Thompson casts new light on the relationship between the self and the brain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/evan-thompsons-waking-dreaming-being.html', 'snippet': 'Merging neuroscience and philosophy, Evan Thompson casts new light on the relationship between the self and the brain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the endless public wars between science and religion, Buddhism has mostly been given a pass. The genesis of this cultural tolerance began with the idea, popular in the 1970s, that Buddhism was somehow in harmony with the frontiers of quantum physics. While the silliness of “quantum spirituality” is apparent enough these days, the possibility that Eastern traditions might have something to say to science did not disappear. Instead, a more natural locus for that encounter was found in the study of the mind. Spurred by the Dalai Lama’s remarkable engagement with scientists, interest in Buddhist attitudes toward the study of the mind has grown steadily. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-FRANK/1221-bks-FRANK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 477, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-FRANK/1221-bks-FRANK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 477}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-FRANK/1221-bks-FRANK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 815, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-FRANK/1221-bks-FRANK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1630, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-FRANK/1221-bks-FRANK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-FRANK/1221-bks-FRANK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-FRANK/1221-bks-FRANK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Evan Thompson’s ‘Waking, Dreaming, Being’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Awakenings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frank, Adam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Evan (1962- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Frank', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frank', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7213979d-0bbe-5c60-887d-e070f3c387d0', 'word_count': 1192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7213979d-0bbe-5c60-887d-e070f3c387d0'}, {'abstract': 'Anthony Horowitz’s “Moriarty” is a Sherlock \xadHolmes pastiche that opens five days after the great detective and his nemesis plunged to their doom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/anthony-horowitzs-moriarty-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Anthony Horowitz’s “Moriarty” is a Sherlock \xadHolmes pastiche that opens five days after the great detective and his nemesis plunged to their doom.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Does anyone really believe what happened at the Reichenbach Falls?” Anthony Horowitz throws down the gauntlet in his infernally clever Sherlock \xadHolmes pastiche, MORIARTY (Harper, $26.99), which opens five days after the great detective and his nemesis, Prof. James Moriarty, plunged to their picturesque doom near the pretty Swiss village of Meiringen. The skeptical narrative voice belongs to Frederick Chase, a brash Pinkerton agent from New York who proceeds to deconstruct the “Final Problem,” challenging point by point Dr. Watson’s account of Holmes’s last adventure.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21CRIME/21CRIME-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 785, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21CRIME/21CRIME-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 785}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21CRIME/21CRIME-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 783, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21CRIME/21CRIME-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1565, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21CRIME/1221-bks-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21CRIME/1221-bks-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21CRIME/1221-bks-Crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Smoke and Mirrors', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Smoke and Mirrors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Horowitz, Anthony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Estleman, Loren D', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shoham, Liad', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKenzie, CB', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7efad5a3-76f7-5da7-99d1-8294e7225a08', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7efad5a3-76f7-5da7-99d1-8294e7225a08'}, {'abstract': 'Gerard Russell chronicles the long survival of religious minorities in the Middle East, and the extreme peril they face today..', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/heirs-to-forgotten-kingdoms-by-gerard-russell.html', 'snippet': 'Gerard Russell chronicles the long survival of religious minorities in the Middle East, and the extreme peril they face today..', 'lead_paragraph': 'In early August, fighters from the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria advanced on the northern Iraqi city of Erbil, routing the local Kurdish forces, storming the surrounding villages and seizing control of the Mosul Dam, the largest in the country. The militants drove thousands of Iraqi Christians and Yazidis out of their homes, forcing them to take refuge in the barren heights of nearby Mount Sinjar in the intense summer heat.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21bazzi/21bazzi-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21bazzi/21bazzi-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21bazzi/21bazzi-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21bazzi/21bazzi-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1369, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21bazzi/21bazzi-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21bazzi/21bazzi-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21bazzi/21bazzi-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Heirs to Forgotten Kingdoms,’ by Gerard Russell', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Last Adherents', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bazzi, Mohamad', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Russell, Gerard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mohamad Bazzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mohamad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bazzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e66c61d4-8e6c-51ab-bae4-4e2fae8b755d', 'word_count': 1214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e66c61d4-8e6c-51ab-bae4-4e2fae8b755d'}, {'abstract': 'The first volume of Norton’s two-part anthology includes texts of Hinduism, Buddhism and Daoism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/the-norton-anthology-of-world-religions-volume-i.html', 'snippet': 'The first volume of Norton’s two-part anthology includes texts of Hinduism, Buddhism and Daoism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By the end of the 19th century, almost all the major surviving classics of Greek and Roman literature had been translated into English. Since then a few new gems have turned up, mainly from papyri found in the rubbish dumps of the Egyptian town Oxyrhynchus. Here was found, for example, a tattered verse by Sappho and Pindar’s paeans to Apollo. It is unlikely, however, that many more great works of classical European literature will surface; the canon of ancient European literature we have now is likely to remain largely unchanged a century hence. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/sub-21dalrymple/sub-21dalrymple-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xl