'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robinson, James Jr (1939- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Norton Anthology of World Religions (Book)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Dalrymple', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dalrymple', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3d1c5b1-0163-54a3-b44e-22e19ae8063d', 'word_count': 1167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3d1c5b1-0163-54a3-b44e-22e19ae8063d'}, {'abstract': 'Roberto Calasso delves into the enigmatic web of ritual and myth that defined the ancient Vedic world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/ardor-by-roberto-calasso.html', 'snippet': 'Roberto Calasso delves into the enigmatic web of ritual and myth that defined the ancient Vedic world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The philosophers of India, T. S. Eliot once wrote, “make most of the great European philosophers look like schoolboys.” Roberto Calasso seems to concur. India, he writes in “Ardor,” his latest meditation on ancient Indian philosophy, “begins and ends with something that was to become central in the West only at the beginning of the 20th century.” Classical Indians not only “wanted to think,” Calasso argues; above all, they “wanted to be aware of thinking.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21mishra/21mishra-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21mishra/21mishra-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 768}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21mishra/21mishra-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21mishra/21mishra-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1662, 'width': 1298, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21mishra/21mishra-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21mishra/21mishra-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21mishra/21mishra-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Ardor,’ by Roberto Calasso', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quest for Enlightenment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mishra, Pankaj', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Calasso, Roberto', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pankaj Mishra', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pankaj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mishra', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/87917349-8e39-519a-9434-edd17328576a', 'word_count': 1226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87917349-8e39-519a-9434-edd17328576a'}, {'abstract': 'Five alphabet books feature rhyme and alliteration, exuberant animals and found photos of children from the past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/alphabetabum-take-away-the-a-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Five alphabet books feature rhyme and alliteration, exuberant animals and found photos of children from the past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A good alphabet book is like a raucous playroom for language, persuading children to internalize the ABCs by turning the letters into toys. Rhymes and rhythm, metaphor and simile, alliteration, assonance and consonance — the ABC book’s verbal gymnastics match the alphabet’s inherently visual nature, making the genre not just a feast for young readers but a rewarding medium for illustrators and designers too.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-Crain-2/1221-bks-Crain-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 471, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-Crain-2/1221-bks-Crain-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 471}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-Crain-2/1221-bks-Crain-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 805, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-Crain-2/1221-bks-Crain-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1225, 'width': 1559, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-Crain-2/1221-bks-Crain-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-Crain-2/1221-bks-Crain-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-Crain-2/1221-bks-Crain-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Alphabetabum,’ ‘Take Away the A’ and More', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let’s Start at the Beginning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Escoffier, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeffers, Oliver', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Di Giacomo, Kris', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schoonmaker, Elizabeth', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryski, Dawid', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Concepcion, Patrick', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Concepcion, Traci', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Raschka, Chris', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Radunsky, Vladimir', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Crain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c41f84c1-808a-5622-8098-406a0c817193', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c41f84c1-808a-5622-8098-406a0c817193'}, {'abstract': 'In these middle-grade novels, a young detective solves a lunar murder, and twins reunite to save their threatened worlds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/space-case-and-the-twin-powers.html', 'snippet': 'In these middle-grade novels, a young detective solves a lunar murder, and twins reunite to save their threatened worlds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m thinking every murder mystery should be set on the moon. A lunar colony is so much more menacing than a snowed-in mansion — there’s no escape, after all, when stepping outside means simultaneously suffocating and freezing.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Space Case’ and ‘The Twin Powers’', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mysteries in the Cosmos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lipsyte, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gibbs, Stuart', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eliot Schrefer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eliot', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schrefer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef232866-0cea-5493-804f-94d1cc099e13', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef232866-0cea-5493-804f-94d1cc099e13'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of Jorge Mario Bergoglio traces his Argentine origins and his path to the Vatican.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/the-great-reformer-a-biography-of-pope-francis.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of Jorge Mario Bergoglio traces his Argentine origins and his path to the Vatican.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An avalanche of insta-biographies follows every papal conclave. Catholics and outsiders alike are eager to understand the man who is, in effect, the only monarch with meaningful power remaining in the Western world. But almost two years after Pope Francis’ election, many are keen for deeper analysis of this “pope for the poor” known for holding freewheeling news conferences and driving his own 30-year-old Renault.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21worthen/21worthen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21worthen/21worthen-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21worthen/21worthen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 684, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21worthen/21worthen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1367, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21worthen/21worthen-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21worthen/21worthen-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21worthen/21worthen-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Great Reformer,’ a Biography of Pope Francis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Franciscan Age', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Worthen, Molly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ivereigh, Austen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Great Reformer: Francis and the Making of a Radical Pope (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Worthen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Worthen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/05f5ab61-9760-5480-b4a9-92ad20c2ba6d', 'word_count': 1317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05f5ab61-9760-5480-b4a9-92ad20c2ba6d'}, {'abstract': 'Cynthia Ozick and others recommend novels with religious themes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/religious-reading.html', 'snippet': 'Cynthia Ozick and others recommend novels with religious themes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Book Review turns its attention to spiritual matters this week with a special issue on world religion. We took the opportunity to ask a few writers to recommend novels with religious themes, preferably lesser known. (If you don’t already know you should read Marilynne Robinson’s “Gilead,” well, you should.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21openbook/21openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 265, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21openbook/21openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 265}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21openbook/21openbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21openbook/21openbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21openbook/21openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21openbook/21openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21openbook/21openbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Religious Reading', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Religious Reading', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a30b099e-7be1-5b39-b8ee-1e01d9813483', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a30b099e-7be1-5b39-b8ee-1e01d9813483'}, {'abstract': 'Kathryn Harrison’s biography interweaves Joan of Arc’s story with both fictional and historical interpretations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/joan-of-arc-by-kathryn-harrison.html', 'snippet': 'Kathryn Harrison’s biography interweaves Joan of Arc’s story with both fictional and historical interpretations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It remains, after nearly 600 years, a story to break your heart. In the 1420s, in a village in northeastern France, an illiterate teenage peasant girl has a series of visions telling her that God wants her to lead a French army to lift the English siege of Orléans and help crown the dauphin, Charles, king in the cathedral at Reims. Putting on men’s clothing and winning over everyone by force of character and belief, she gets her army and triumphantly achieves all that the voices have foretold. Pious, outspoken, stubborn and recklessly courageous, she breaks every rule of gender, class and organized religion, and the people flock to her. Then comes her fall. Sidelined at court, she waits too long to launch an unsuccessful attack on English-occupied Paris, during which she is wounded, and then in a skirmish at Compiègne she is captured. Abandoned by the king, she is cruelly imprisoned, tried as a heretic and witch, then burned at the stake. The light of revelation ends in the agony of fire, and the legend of Joan of Arc is born.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21dunant/21dunant-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 824, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21dunant/21dunant-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 824}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21dunant/21dunant-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 746, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21dunant/21dunant-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1491, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21dunant/21dunant-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21dunant/21dunant-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21dunant/21dunant-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Joan of Arc,’ by Kathryn Harrison', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Death and the Maiden', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harrison, Kathryn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Joan of Arc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Joan of Arc: A Life Transfigured (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Dunant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dunant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd11a070-d977-5f88-b3e4-ced477956421', 'word_count': 1278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd11a070-d977-5f88-b3e4-ced477956421'}, {'abstract': 'A study of Billy Graham’s ministry examines how American culture shaped him — and he shaped it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/americas-pastor-about-billy-graham-by-grant-wacker.html', 'snippet': 'A study of Billy Graham’s ministry examines how American culture shaped him — and he shaped it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I grew up in West Virginia as a Catholic in a Protestant culture, the kind we would today describe as evangelical. We Catholics had the pope — but he was a distant and, to be blunt, foreign figure. Our Protestant neighbors had Billy Graham, the friend of presidents, business magnates and celebrities, who through the magic of television was a frequent, familiar guest in the homes of ordinary people; and he was as American as apple pie.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21george/21george-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21george/21george-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 574}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21george/21george-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21george/21george-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1934, 'width': 2020, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21george/21george-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21george/21george-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/21george/21george-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘America’s Pastor,’ About Billy Graham, by Grant Wacker', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Evangelist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'George, Robert P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wacker, Grant', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Graham, Billy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "America's Pastor: Bill Graham and the Shaping of a Nation (Book)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T18:39:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert P. George', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'George', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6705c277-a926-5152-9c5a-b368a53affc7', 'word_count': 1243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6705c277-a926-5152-9c5a-b368a53affc7'}, {'abstract': 'Charting the many ways we walk, and what they say about us.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/books/review/a-philosophy-of-walking-by-frdric-gros.html', 'snippet': 'Charting the many ways we walk, and what they say about us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The act the French philosopher Frédéric Gros describes in his athletic new book, “A Philosophy of Walking,” has more in common with what Americans call hiking and the French call la randonnée than with what they are likely to think of as simply “walking.” But for Gros this is the only kind that matters: City dwellers can only ever be “strollers,” stretching their legs in fragmented moments between street-crossings. Gros’s true walker leaves the pavement far behind. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-ELKIN-sub/1221-bks-ELKIN-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 328, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-ELKIN-sub/1221-bks-ELKIN-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 328}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/21/books/review/1221-bks-ELKIN-sub/1221-bks-ELKIN-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 560, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'i