ey', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Segal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Segal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a5fc2d7-9069-5c21-ada0-aa42ce6e4040', 'word_count': 984, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a5fc2d7-9069-5c21-ada0-aa42ce6e4040'}, {'abstract': 'Seeing the city from the sidewalk, not the freeway, opens a visitor to quiet wonders that would be lost at 60 miles per hour.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/travel/los-angeles-as-a-pedestrian.html', 'snippet': 'Seeing the city from the sidewalk, not the freeway, opens a visitor to quiet wonders that would be lost at 60 miles per hour.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Had I been driving I would not have stopped here. But I was lured from the sidewalk by an open gate and the mysterious buildings beyond. There was a Moorish structure with a minaret, another was Italian with a loggia, a third had a fleur-de-lis on a chimney. It was as if a snow globe village had been dropped onto Sunset Boulevard. At the back of the hushed lot, a stone statue, naked to her hips, stood sentry.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Los Angeles, as a Pedestrian', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Los Angeles, as a Pedestrian', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenbloom, Stephanie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Walking', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-19T19:34:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Rosenbloom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steph