sing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a moot point, but someone should have told Kim Jong-un that “The Interview” isn’t really about blowing him up. Sure, its narrative climax features his character being vaporized by a rocket-propelled grenade. But its comic and emotional high points come earlier, when he bonds over hoops and babes with a dimwitted American television host. Also, when the somewhat less dimwitted American played by Seth Rogen has to hide the business end of a missile in his rectum.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/20/world/INTERVIEW-1/INTERVIEW-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/20/world/INTERVIEW-1/INTERVIEW-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/20/world/INTERVIEW-1/INTERVIEW-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/20/world/INTERVIEW-1/INTERVIEW-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/20/world/INTERVIEW-1/INTERVIEW-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/20/world/INTERVIEW-1/INTERVIEW-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/20/world/INTERVIEW-1/INTERVIEW-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'heig