ation': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4482886d-a1d0-5988-bd87-cfd35dc64802', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4482886d-a1d0-5988-bd87-cfd35dc64802'}, {'abstract': 'What happens when you strike a match. A lot, it turns out. A video reveals swirling streams of hot gas and flame.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/a-quick-video-of-a-slow-burn/', 'snippet': 'What happens when you strike a match. A lot, it turns out. A video reveals swirling streams of hot gas and flame.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three research scientists from Stanford University have made an entrancing slow-motion video of what happens when a match is lit. In “The Hidden Complexities of the Simple Match,” Victor Miller, Matthew Tilghman and Ronald Hanson employed Schlieren imaging to capture the fleeting, normally unseen patterns in the flame.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Quick Video of a Slow Burn', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gorman, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hanson, Ronald K', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Victor A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tilghman, Matthew Seidel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Milton (NY)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-21T14:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8a78f35-d86f-5ed4-8c69-81c8a5de0d85', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8a78f35-d86f-5ed4-8c69-81c8a5de0d85'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/21/arts/television/whats-on-tv-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': "3:15 P.M. (Showtime 2) LEE DANIELS’ THE BUTLER (2013) Inspired by the real story of Eugene Allen, Mr. Daniels follows a White House butler, Cecil Gaines (Forest Whitaker), and his wife, Gloria (Oprah Winfrey), as history — particularly the civil rights movement — unfolds over seven presidential administrations. A. O. Scott, writing in The New York Times, called the film “a brilliantly truthful movie on a subject that is usually shrouded in wishful thinking, mythmongering and outright denial.” Robin Williams portrays Dwight D. Eisenhower; Liev Schreiber is Lyndon B. Johnson; and Alan Rickman is Ronald Reagan, with Jane Fonda as his wife, Nancy; Cuba Gooding Jr. and Lenny Kravitz are the fellow butlers who become Cecil’s close friends; and David Oyelowo is the older son whose politics Cecil eschews. “In part, their story is a chronicle of the generational misunderstanding that has occurred in upwardly mobile families of every background, especially in the baby boom era, when educated and restless young people turned against the parents who had sacrificed so much on their behalf,” Mr. Scott wrote. “ 'The Butler’ has the historical insight and the generosity of spirit to honor the father and the son equally, and to look with skepticism at each’s point of view.” He added: “We remember those who marched and who stood up for themselves in the face of injustice. It is good to remember that, to paraphrase Milton, they also stand who only wait and serve.”", 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-21T15:28:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0063755-9001-5a75-9d66-bf7d8a90b15a', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0063755-9001-5a75-9d66-bf7d8a90b15a'}, {'abstract': 'The killings of two officers in Brooklyn on Saturday set off a bunker mentality across the department, as sentries were posted outside station houses and officers were barred from patrolling alone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/22/nyregion/a-divided-city-mourns-the-deaths-of-two-officers.html', 'snippet': 'The killings of two officers in Brooklyn on Saturday set off a bunker mentality across the department, as sentries were posted outside station houses and officers were barred from patrolling alone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Faced with the first targeted killings of New York City police officers in years, police leaders ratcheted up precautions for their vast patrol force on Sunday as officials described the predatory final movements of a gunman bent on killing officers.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/22/nyregion/SUB-COPS/SUB-COPS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/22/nyregion/SUB-COPS/SUB-COPS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/22/nyregion/SUB-COPS/SUB-COPS-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo