uclear power plants.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Climate-Friendly Nuclear Energy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-22T17:38:12+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/28190be2-7592-534d-b287-949982acc948', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/28190be2-7592-534d-b287-949982acc948'}, {'abstract': 'Natural gas seems to be better for the environment than coal because of its reduced carbon emissions, but environmentalists say it may not be better after all.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2014/12/22/science/scitimes-podcast-natgas.html', 'snippet': 'Natural gas seems to be better for the environment than coal because of its reduced carbon emissions, but environmentalists say it may not be better after all.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Natural gas seems to be better for the environment than coal because of its reduced carbon emissions, but environmentalists say it may not be better after all.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Natural Gas Better?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-22T17:38:32+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/8f209840-2f6f-5872-bb5d-6ac06a8ac2a5', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/8f209840-2f6f-5872-bb5d-6ac06a8ac2a5'}, {'abstract': 'A final word, in alphabetical order, to bid farewell to the Science Times podcast.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2014/12/22/science/scitimes-podcast-farewell.html', 'snippet': 'A final word, in alphabetical order, to bid farewell to the Science Times podcast.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A final word, in alphabetical order, to bid farewell to the Science Times podcast.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Farewell', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-22T17:39:57+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/c0cde164-ed43-50c8-81cd-815f041a3e46', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/c0cde164-ed43-50c8-81cd-815f041a3e46'}, {'abstract': 'Books by Louise Glück, Fanny Howe, Christian Wiman and others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/22/books/review/david-orrs-10-favorite-poetry-books-of-2014.html', 'snippet': 'Books by Louise Glück, Fanny Howe, Christian Wiman and others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it comes to annual best-books lists, poetry is the cat that wandered into the dog show. No matter how much one tries, as The Times does, to include poets among the various biographers, essayists, novelists and so forth, it’s hard not to feel that poetry is fundamentally different from prose of any stripe. (Prose, said Coleridge, is “words in their best order,” whereas poetry is “the best words in their best order” — but then, Coleridge was a poet.) So here are 10 American poetry collections published in 2014 that I would recommend not only to poetry veterans, but to readers coming to the form with limited experience. In keeping with Times policy, this list includes no friends, lovers, babysitters or college roommates. (If I were allowed to include friends, my list would certainly include Maureen McLane’s “This Blue,” which was a finalist for the National Book Award.) In the interests of spreading the wealth, I’ve also generally favored work by poets I haven’t written about already.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/22/books/review/22poetry/22poetry-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 271, 'width': 574, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/22/books/review/22poetry/22poetry-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 271, 'width': 574, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/22/books/review/22poetry/22poetry-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/22/books/review/22poetry/22poetry-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/22/books/review/22poetry/22poetry-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'David Orr’s 10 Favorite Poetry Books of 2014', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-22T17:53:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Orr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b896d757-da90-53f4-aef4-5e398e37ea76', 'word_count': 593, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b896d757-da90-53f4-aef4-5e398e37ea76'}, {'abstract': 'The Treehouse Theater said the live reading, with comedians from the Upright Citizens Brigade Theater, is “an opportunity for people to come together in the name of free speech.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/22/new-york-theater-plans-live-reading-of-the-interview-script/', 'snippet': 'The Treehouse Theater said the live reading, with comedians from the Upright Citizens Brigade Theater, is “an opportunity for people to come together in the name of free speech.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'While Sony is deciding whether you’ll be able to see “The Interview” on movie screens, through video-on-demand services or perhaps not at all, an independent New York theater says that it will present a live reading of the script for that controversial Seth Rogen-James Franco comedy this weekend. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Theater Plans Live Reading of ‘The Interview’ Script', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘The Interview’ Reading Set for New York Theater', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-22T18:07:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd340272-8d13-59c2-8bf7-eefe905c9848', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd340272-8d13-59c2-8bf7-eefe905c9848'}, {'abstract': 'Toasting holidays past and present with a cocktail of amaretto, Curaçao, bourbon and clementines.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/dining/a-christmas-day-cocktail-the-clementine-sour.html', 'snippet': 'Toasting holidays past and present with a cocktail of amaretto, Curaçao, bourbon and clementines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chestnuts roasting on an open fire are all fine and well, but to me, the most satisfying smells of Christmas are those of clementine peels and brown sugar, cinnamon and clove — along with the sharp sting of strong whiskey and a sweet waft of amaretto liqueur. (I love the fatty fragrance of a slowly cooking goose, too, but that’s hard to work into a drink, even though I imagine an inventive bartender in, say, Seattle, would happily give it a shot.)', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/dining/24TOAST/24TOAST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/24/dining/24TOAST/24TOAST-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/dining/24TOAST/24TOAST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/dining/24TOAST/24TOAST-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': '