r a massacre at a school, a court granted bail to a militant accused of orchestrating the 2008 Mumbai terrorist attacks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/23/opinion/pakistans-baffling-response-to-extremism.html', 'snippet': 'Two days after a massacre at a school, a court granted bail to a militant accused of orchestrating the 2008 Mumbai terrorist attacks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite the grief and rage that followed the massacre of 148 students and their teachers by Taliban militants at an army-run school in Peshawar last week, Pakistan persists in its duplicitous and self-defeating response to the extremism that is threatening the country. Immediately after the attack, Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif promised that Pakistan would no longer distinguish between the “bad” Pakistani Taliban, which is seeking to bring down the Pakistani state, and other “good” Taliban groups that for years have been supported or exploited by Pakistan’s Army and intelligence service to attack India and wield influence in Afghanistan. What his words should mean is that Pakistan will no longer tolerate any extremists. But initial indications are not promising.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistan’s Baffling Response to Extremism', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistan&#8217;s Baffling Response to Extremism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Peshawar (Pakistan)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharif, Nawaz', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lashkar-e-Taiba', 'rank': 7, 'major': 