: {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a8be27a-f98e-54d4-8c79-c477f31c65d9', 'word_count': 202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a8be27a-f98e-54d4-8c79-c477f31c65d9'}, {'abstract': 'The best present of all, of course, is the presence of other souls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/my-christmas-visitors.html', 'snippet': 'The best present of all, of course, is the presence of other souls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BELGRADE LAKES, Me. — Of all the unexpected visitors my family received over the holidays, there was probably no soul more unlikely than Vietnam Santa. Forty years ago, my parents and sister had gone out one night just before Christmas, leaving me alone. I was 16, and I’d managed to make some unfortunate decisions, winding up goofy on a cocktail I’d invented that contained equal measures of Virginia Gentleman bourbon and orange Hi-C. Also, I had invited over a girl I barely knew, who’d arrived intending to make out with me but who had instead passed out in a guest room on the third floor.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edboylanart/edboylanart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edboylanart/edboylanart-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edboylanart/edboylanart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edboylanart/edboylanart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1402, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edboylanart/edboylanart-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edboylanart/edboylanart-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edboylanart/edboylanart-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Christmas Visitors', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Christmas Visitors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boylan, Jennifer Finney', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-23T10:33:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Finney Boylan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Finney', 'lastname': 'Boylan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bbb1e89-acd7-50e0-9f2c-94b5d7dd8175', 'word_count': 972, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bbb1e89-acd7-50e0-9f2c-94b5d7dd8175'}, {'abstract': 'There are more than 1,300 species of mistletoe, some tropical.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/the-hemiparasite-season.html', 'snippet': 'There are more than 1,300 species of mistletoe, some tropical.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — Mistletoe. The word conjures winter holidays, office parties, stolen kisses, romance. But appreciation of the plant is no modern thing. Kissing under the mistletoe has been happening for at least 200 years. And some 2,000 years ago, the druids in what is now Britain venerated the plant when it grew on an oak. When they found it, they dressed in white, harvested it with a golden sickle, and sacrificed two white bulls. Or so says that great Roman, Pliny the Elder, in his “Natural History.” The druids — Pliny continues — believed that mistletoe could make barren animals fecund, and that it was an antidote to all poisons. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edjudsonart/edjudsonart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 960, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edjudsonart/edjudsonart-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 960}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edjudsonart/edjudsonart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edjudsonart/edjudsonart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1280, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edjudsonart/edjudsonart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/23/opinion/edjudsonart/edjudsonart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thum