nt_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Attacks on Police', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConnell, Mitch', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vitter, David', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Pictures Entertainment', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-23T12:04:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f5ff554-77eb-5cf7-97cb-2cd74aa5aa8b', 'word_count': 1636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f5ff554-77eb-5cf7-97cb-2cd74aa5aa8b'}, {'abstract': 'Previous studies have found a link between high unemployment and improved health indicators, like weight loss. But that may be changing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/upshot/how-losing-a-job-can-be-bad-for-your-health.html', 'snippet': 'Previous studies have found a link between high unemployment and improved health indicators, like weight loss. But that may be changing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Being out of work can be advantageous for people’s physical health. Unemployed people have more time to exercise and cook at home, and less money to buy cigarettes or junk food. Studies in the United States and Europe over the years have found that when unemployment is high, people lose weight and become healthier and overall mortality rates drop.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/upshot/24UP-Weight/24UP-Weight-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/24/upshot/24UP-Weight/24UP-Weight-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/upshot/24UP-Weight/24UP-Weight-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/upshot/24UP-Weight/24UP-Weight-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/upshot/24UP-Weight/24UP-Weight-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/24/upshot/24UP-Weight/24UP-Weight-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/upshot/24UP-Weight/24UP-Weight-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Losing a Job Can Be Bad for Your Health', 'kicker': 'Nonemployed', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pain-Relieving Drugs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-23T12:06:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c8f0eb5-b1ed-5654-9bf4-dfc1619597c6', 'word_count': 773, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c8f0eb5-b1ed-5654-9bf4-dfc1619597c6'}, {'abstract': 'Good morning. Here is Daily Clip Report, a collection of articles about The Times and the media sent by email each morning to senior executives and newsroom editors. The email is produced by the Corporate Communications Department at The Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2014/12/23/daily-clip-report-88/', 'snippet': 'Good morning. Here is Daily Clip Report, a collection of articles about The Times and the media sent by email each morning to senior executives and newsroom editors. The email is produced by the Corporate Communications Department at The Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Good morning. Here is Daily Clip Report, a collection of articles about The Times and the media sent by email each morning to senior executives and newsroom editors. The email is produced by the Corporate Communications Department at The Times.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Clip Report', 'kicker': 'Times Insider', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-23T12:29:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jordan Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jordan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9275c8d1-90a1-5b95-ad64-681914ee1ccb', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9275c8d1-90a1-5b95-ad64-681914ee1ccb'}, {'abstract': 'The computer breach at JPMorgan Chase might have been prevented with a simple security fix. | Financial start-ups are trying to reach an anti-finance crowd. | After a settlement, Ocwen will remain on a tight regulatory leash. | F-Squared Investments was fined over accusations it defrauded investors.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/12/23/morning-agenda-how-hackers-got-into-jpmorgan/', 'snippet': 'The computer breach at JPMorgan Chase might have been prevented with a simple security fix. | Financial start-ups are trying to reach an anti-finance crowd. | After a settlement, Ocwen will remain on a tight regulatory leash. | F-Squared Investmen...', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOW HACKERS GOT INTO JPMORGAN | The giant computer breach at JPMorgan Chase this summer might have been prevented if the bank had installed a simple security fix to an overlooked server in its network, Matthew Goldstein, Nicole Perlroth and Michael Corkery report in DealBook. While JPMorgan spends $250 million a year on computer security, the weak spot in this case was very basic, according to people who have been briefed on internal and outside investigations into the attack.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: How Hackers Got Into JPMorgan', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-23T12:52:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d54c2c2-1640-539a-95e7-a7f797d460ac', 'word_count': 1909, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d54c2c2-1640-539a-95e7-a7f797d460ac'}, {'abstract': 'The soprano Danielle de Niese sings the Bach-Gounod setting of “Ave Maria,” which she will perform on Christmas Eve at St. Patrick’s Cathedral.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/23/in-performance-danielle-de-niese-sings-ave-maria/', 'snippet': 'The soprano Danielle de Niese sings the Bach-Gounod setting of “Ave Maria,” which she will perform on Christmas Eve at St. Patrick’s Cathedral.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Danielle de Niese, a soprano who weds diva glamor to pop star chic, just finished a run as Susanna in Mozart’s “Le Nozze di Figaro” at the Metropolitan Opera and is preparing to sing at another New York institution: she is scheduled to appear Christmas Eve at midnight mass at St. Patrick’s Cathedral. Perhaps with that in mind, she chose to sing some sacred music here: the Bach-Gounod setting of “Ave Maria.” But Ms. de Niese, who has an operatic family — her husband, Gus Christie, is chairman of the Glyndebourne Festival in Britain — will be back on secular opera stages soon. The Met’s new production of Lehar’s “The Merry Widow” opens New Year’s Eve, and Ms. de Niese will join its cast in April singing the role of Valencienne.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Performance: Danielle de Niese Sings ‘Ave Maria’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'na