 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5781799d-c3f0-541f-b634-f7ea7e1a5363', 'word_count': 235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5781799d-c3f0-541f-b634-f7ea7e1a5363'}, {'abstract': 'An overhaul of the Freedom of Information Act did not survive the legislative deal-making that produced the spending plan. William D. Cohan laments that loss in the Street Scene column.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/12/23/wall-street-blocks-the-light/', 'snippet': 'An overhaul of the Freedom of Information Act did not survive the legislative deal-making that produced the spending plan. William D. Cohan laments that loss in the Street Scene column.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lost in all the recent hoopla about how Wall Street used its substantial political muscle this month to water down the laws and regulations intended to keep us safe from its bad behavior as part of the spending plan known as Cromnibus is the sad story of how the Street somehow was also able to kill another bill it did not like: the revised version of what was generally known as the FOIA Improvement Act of 2014.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/business/dbpix-leahy/dbpix-leahy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/24/business/dbpix-leahy/dbpix-leahy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/business/dbpix-leahy/dbpix-leahy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_