.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/23/multimedia/opinions-cuba-boggers/opinions-cuba-boggers-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/23/multimedia/opinions-cuba-boggers/opinions-cuba-boggers-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuban Bloggers Find Their Voice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Havana (Cuba)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanchez, Yoani', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-24T01:09:38+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'Alexandra Garcia, Ernesto Londoño and Andrew Blackwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garcia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ernesto', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Londoño', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Blackwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/943adb4c-75dd-5356-9897-73d1ab36b2c1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/943adb4c-75dd-5356-9897-73d1ab36b2c1'}, {'abstract': 'La creciente y diversa comunidad de blogueros puede sacar provecho del acercamiento entre Washington y La Habana.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/prometedoras-nuevas-voces-digitales-de-cuba.html', 'snippet': 'La creciente y diversa comunidad de blogueros puede sacar provecho del acercamiento entre Washington y La Habana.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A veces, hacer clic para publicar un texto genera la sensación de un juego de ruleta rusa. Pero, en el caso de Carlos Alberto Pérez, un bloguero en La Habana, su situación está lejos de ser la de un disidente. Trabaja para el Ministerio de Comunicaciones, lo cual le permite tener acceso a Internet en la oficina y su hogar. Su habitación está cubierta de fotos en blanco y negro del Ché Guevara, el legendario líder guerrillero argentino que ayudó a llevar a Fidel Castro al poder en 1959. Y aún así, leer su blog, La Chiringa de Cuba, es sumergirse en un sinnúmero de ejemplos de todo aquello que marcha mal en esta nación de 11 millones de habitantes, aún gobernada, después de 50 años, bajo el mantra “Socialismo o muerte”.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/opinion/24WEDS1/24WEDS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/24/opinion/24WEDS1/24WEDS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/opinion/24WEDS1/24WEDS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/opinion/24WEDS1/24WEDS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/opinion/24WEDS1/24WEDS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/24/opinion/24WEDS1/24WEDS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/opinion/24WEDS1/24WEDS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prometedoras nuevas voces digitales de Cuba', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-24T01:13:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'Por El Comité Editorial', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Por', 'middlename': 'El', 'lastname': 'Comité', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4deaac5-516f-5890-8f07-24ddb4feddbe', 'word_count': 1151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4deaac5-516f-5890-8f07-24ddb4feddbe'}, {'abstract': 'A diverse and growing community of bloggers in Cuba stand to benefit from their government’s improved relations with the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/cubas-promising-new-online-voices.html', 'snippet': 'A diverse and growing community of bloggers in Cuba stand to benefit from their government’s improved relations with the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At times, hitting the publish button feels like playing Russian roulette. Carlos Alberto Pérez, a blogger in Havana, is far from being a dissident. He works for the Ministry of Communication and has government-provided access to the Internet at work and at home. Mr. Pérez’s bedroom walls are covered with black-and-white photos of Che Guevara, the legendary Argentine guerrilla leader who helped catapult Fidel Castro to power in 1959. Yet to read his blog, La Chiringa de Cuba, is to get a gritty view of how much has gone awry in Cuba, a nation of 11 million, still governed, after five decades, under the mantra “socialism or death.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuba’s Promising New Online Voices ', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cuba’s Promising New Online Voices', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castro, Raul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diaz-Canel Bermudez, Miguel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Series', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanchez, Yoani', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-24T01:13:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c12b188-38ff-511f-a73a-7077b77d06a9', 'word_count': 977, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c12b188-38ff-511f-a73a-7077b77d06a9'}, {'abstract': 'We haven’t seen the ramifications of the drop in global oil prices yet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/thomas-friedman-is-vacation-over.html', 'snippet': 'We haven’t seen the ramifications of the drop in global oil prices yet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than we may realize, the world has been riding a lucky streak since the global financial meltdown in 2008. How so? The years between 2008 and late 2013 were — relatively speaking — a rather benign period of big power politics and geopolitics. This allowed the major economic powers — the United States, the European Union, China, India, Russia, Brazil and Japan — to focus almost exclusively on economic rehabilitation. But now there are strong indications that our vacation from geo-instability is over. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/friedman-circular/friedman-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/friedman-circular/friedman-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/friedman-circular/friedman-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/friedman-circular/friedman-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/friedman-circular/friedman-circular-thumbLarge.png', 'heigh