oined what had become more a campaign to defend free speech against foreign intimidation than a business initiative.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/25/business/sony-the-interview-online-streaming.html', 'snippet': 'In getting behind “The Interview,” Google and Microsoft joined what had become more a campaign to defend free speech against foreign intimidation than a business initiative.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — With the help of the technology giants Google and Microsoft, Sony Pictures began distributing “The Interview” online Wednesday after a terror threat led the major theater chains to cancel the film’s release.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sony Streams ‘The Interview’ on YouTube, Google Play and Xbox', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sony Finds Allies in Google and Microsoft to Stream Movie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Pictures Entertainment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberwarfare', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong-un', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rogen, Seth', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franco, James', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Interview (Movie)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-24T17:40:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply and Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bd43dda-593f-5a87-8d42-5038447ebcf9', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bd43dda-593f-5a87-8d42-5038447ebcf9'}, {'abstract': 'A surge of native Californians and Northeasterners who travel home from the South highlights its new role as a place to pursue the American dream.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/25/upshot/going-home-from-the-south-a-new-holiday-exodus.html', 'snippet': 'A surge of native Californians and Northeasterners who travel home from the South highlights its new role as a place to pursue the American dream.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The adopted Southerners have landed this week in San Francisco and Los Angeles, in New York, Chicago and Denver. It is the new pattern in holiday travel: Thousands of people raised in California, the Midwest or the Northeast will return, albeit temporarily, from their homes in Atlanta, Memphis or Raleigh.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/31/business/25UP-Migration/25UP-Migration-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/31/business/25UP-Migration/25UP-Migration-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2