hat do people like when they can choose?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/25/upshot/pandoras-top-christmas-songs-from-mariah-carey-to-eartha-kitt.html', 'snippet': 'If you’ve been in public in the past month, you’ve been forced to hear Christmas music. But what do people like when they can choose?', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s almost over: That ever-present, ever-looping Christmas song season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/31/upshot/25UP-Music/25UP-Music-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 487, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/31/upshot/25UP-Music/25UP-Music-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 487}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/31/upshot/25UP-Music/25UP-Music-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 832, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/31/upshot/25UP-Music/25UP-Music-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1647, 'width': 2028, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/31/upshot/25UP-Music/25UP-Music-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/31/upshot/25UP-Music/25UP-Music-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/31/upshot/25UP-Music/25UP-Music-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pandora’s Top Christmas Songs: From Mariah Carey to Eartha Kitt', 'kicker': 'Holiday Playlist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pandora Media Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-24T19:38:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damon Darlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Darlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c557112-87b2-5301-9588-0d536115071c', 'word_count': 578, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c557112-87b2-5301-9588-0d536115071c'}, {'abstract': 'Morocco’s cosmopolitan port beckons with its convivial cafes, colorful markets, waterfront nightclubs and a palm-filled park for a quiet stroll.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/28/travel/what-to-do-in-36-hours-in-casablanca.html', 'snippet': 'Morocco’s cosmopolitan port beckons with its convivial cafes, colorful markets, waterfront nightclubs and a palm-filled park for a quiet stroll.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Marrakesh and Fez bewitch travelers with their ancient customs, and Tangier and Essaouira with their counter-culture exotica, but Casablanca, Morocco’s largest city, is a cosmopolitan Atlantic port, an alluring blend of tradition and progressiveness. With Roman roots, French city planning and a Portuguese name (later changed to Spanish) that dates to the 16th century, the city retains a midcentury elegance with modern touches, including a sleek tram system across its center. In the oldest part of town, wanderers can buy strawberries and eggs off wheeled carts, eat sandwiches fried on the spot outside the mosque, and engage with locals, who always seem willing to share their pathways through the city’s history and geography. A short taxi ride away, visitors can indulge in a luxury shopping spree at Galeries Lafayette, watch a 3-D movie or peer into a three-story aquarium. Here’s looking at you, kid.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Casablanca', 'kicker': '36 Hours', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Casablanca (Morocco)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-24T19:39:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e07bf4a-bc6b-54c5-aa07-10b5099d0a8c', 'word_count': 1613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e07bf4a-bc6b-54c5-aa07-10b5099d0a8c'}, {'abstract': 'Defying predictions of their demise, personal computers are making a comeback, enticing a niche audience of professional users with power and beauty, and an Apple machine stands out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/25/technology/personaltech/retina-5k-imac-powerful-proof-of-the-pc-renaissance.html', 'snippet': 'Defying predictions of their demise, personal computers are making a comeback, enticing a niche audience of professional users with power and beauty, and an Apple machine stands out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About a year before he died, Steve Jobs  was asked at a conference to predict the future of the market for personal computers. Back in the late 1970s, as the chief executive and a co-founder of Apple, Mr. Jobs had presided over the birth of the PC industry, but then, after blockbuster sales of the iPhone and the iPad, he had taken to describing the tech business as entering the “post-PC” era. Did he really believe that desktop and laptop computers were going extinct?', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/25/technology/personaltech/25state-illo/25state-illo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/25/technology/personaltech/25state-illo/25state-illo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/25/technology/personaltech/25state-illo/25state-illo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 615, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/25/technology/personaltech/25state-illo/25state-illo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 615, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/25/technology/personaltech/25state-illo/25state-illo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/25/technology/personaltech/25state-illo/25state-illo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/25/technology/personaltech/25state-illo/25state-illo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Retina 5K iMac: Powerful Proof of the PC Renaissance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Renaissance in PCs Underscored by an iMac', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manjoo, Farhad', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Luxury Goods and Services', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Desktop Computers', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Laptop Computers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-24T19:40:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Farhad Manjoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Farhad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Manjoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34246008-fb71-5946-9a79-40317f98a877', 'word_count': 1203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34246008-fb71-5946-9a79-40317f98a877'}, {'abstract': 'Toronto, the hub of hockey, moonlights as the continent’s unlikeliest incubator of basketball prodigies, pumping out an extraordinary number of professional players.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/25/sports/basketball/in-toronto-a-basketball-boom-north-of-the-border.html', 'snippet': 'Toronto, the hub of hockey, moonlights as the continent’s unlikeliest incubator of basketball prodigies, pumping out an extraordinary number of professional players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TORONTO — There was a time, not long ago, when New York and Los Angeles seemed to monopolize much of the world’s basketball talent. They produced the best players, who went to the best college programs before graduating to the N.B.A. It was the American way.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/sports/TORONTO1/TORONTO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/24/sports/TORONTO1/TORONTO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/sports/TORONTO1/TORONTO1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/sports/TORONTO1/TORONTO1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/sports/TORONTO1/TORONTO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/24/sports/TORONTO1/TORONTO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/sports/TORONTO1/TORONTO1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stardom Without Skates: Toronto Becomes a Hub of Basketball Talent', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stardom Without Skates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Toronto (Ontario)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toronto Raptors', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiggins, Andrew', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Vince', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, Jamal (1997- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-24T19:43:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Cacciola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cacciola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47693400-f92d-51ec-9cc4-bc1338f1ba2f', 'word_count': 2090, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47693400-f92d-51ec-9cc4-bc1338f1ba2f'}, {'abstract': 'Two new translations of Tolstoy’s “Anna Karenina.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/28/books/review/new-translations-of-tolstoys-anna-karenina.html', 'snippet': 'Two new translations of Tolstoy’s “Anna Karenina.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'What difference is there between being repelled, being repulsed, being disgusted and being offended? Not much, perhaps, but consider the scene: Anna Karenina has taken a sip of coffee and raised her eyes to look at Vronsky, her lover, who is watching her. After hundreds of pages of love, lust, passion, fear, exhilaration, disappointment, exhaustion, aggression and, probably most important, jealousy, they are having their final fight. Leo Tolstoy is describing Anna ascribing an emotion to a man whose love she needs so desperately that she is convinced he has stopped loving her. Consider also this: When she lifted her coffee cup, she extended her pinkie away from it — a precious gesture that signals just how far this domesticated, miserable Anna has come from the glamorous young woman she was at the beginning of the novel; she made a sound with her lips — and she realized this when she lifted her gaze and saw Vronsky looking at her. She saw the most painful thing a woman can see: a lover who is turned off by her physical being.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28COVER/1228-bks-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28COVER/1228-bks-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28COVER/1228-bks-cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28COVER/1228-bks-cover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1358, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28COVER/28COVER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28COVER/28COVER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28COVER/28COVER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Translations of Tolstoy’s ‘Anna Karenina’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Found in Translation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gessen, Masha', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tolstoy, Leo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwartz, Marian', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bartlett, Rosamund', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Translation and Interpreters', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Anna Karenina (Book)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-24T19:48:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Masha Gessen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Masha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gessen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fc44013-c140-5367-ad45-7da44c4449d2', 'word_count': 2806, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fc44013-c140-5367-ad45-7da44c4449d2'}, {'abstract': 'A goal against the Capitals originally credited to Martin St. Louis was changed to Nash, giving him 23 total, which ranked second in the league.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/25/sports/hockey/rangers-nash-gets-a-hat-trick-for-christmas.html', 'snippet': 'A goal against the Capitals originally credited to Martin St. Louis was changed to Nash, giving him 23 total, which ranked second in the league.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Tuesday, Rick Nash recorded his first Rangers hat trick. He just did not know it until a day later.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page':