kend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicolas Rapold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicolas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rapold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/46537aa2-cef9-5831-a377-57d5ad5e70da', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46537aa2-cef9-5831-a377-57d5ad5e70da'}, {'abstract': 'A pair of exhibitions is a must for students of 20th-century design.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/26/arts/design/the-memphis-group.html', 'snippet': 'A pair of exhibitions is a must for students of 20th-century design.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Postmodernism may mean different things to different people, but certainly one of its most memorable manifestations was that of the Memphis Group, the wildly influential, much-debated Milan-based designer collective started by Ettore Sottsass in 1981. Featuring playfully eccentric bookcases, tables, chairs, vases, lamps and other domestic items mostly by Mr. Sottsass and including works by six other group members, this exhilarating pair of exhibitions is a must for students of 20th-century design.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Memphis Group’', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘The Memphis Group’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Memphis Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-25T21:08:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8a0eccc-9506-57c3-923e-ad9f93f7c640', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8a0eccc-9506-57c3-923e-ad9f93f7c640'}, {'abstract': 'Polymorphous perversity seems to be the thematic principle behind “Disturbing Innocence,” a show with many contributors at Flag Art Foundation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/26/arts/design/disturbing-innocence.html', 'snippet': 'Polymorphous perversity seems to be the thematic principle behind “Disturbing Innocence,” a show with many contributors at Flag Art Foundation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the start of “Disturbing Innocence,” an entertaining and provocative exhibition organized by the painter Eric Fischl, you encounter ominous images of suburban homes in photographs by James Casebere and Gregory Crewdson, paintings by Peter Drake and a sculpture by Roy Lichtenstein. Moving into the main part of the exhibition, you discover what goes on behind closed doors: a riot of polymorphous perversity in the form of paintings, photographs and sculptures, by more than 50 artists, representing children, dolls, mannequins, robots and toys.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26INNOCENCE/26INNOCENCE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 510, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26INNOCENCE/26INNOCENCE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 510}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26INNOCENCE/26INNOCENCE-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 870, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26INNOCENCE/26INNOCENCE-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1700, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26INNOCENCE/INNOCENCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26INNOCENCE/INNOCENCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26INNOCENCE/INNOCENCE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Disturbing Innocence’', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Disturbing Innocence’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Flag Art Foundation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-25T21:17:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbebcf08-ce26-5fd1-a843-c8c83dac51d9', 'word_count': 274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbebcf08-ce26-5fd1-a843-c8c83dac51d9'}, {'abstract': 'This year, Egyptian antiquities, correspondence with Susan B. Anthony and even mastodon bones gained notice because of auctions, and are headed for public display.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/26/arts/design/long-unseen-treasures-have-a-new-spotlight.html', 'snippet': 'This year, Egyptian antiquities, correspondence with Susan B. Anthony and even mastodon bones gained notice because of auctions, and are headed for public display.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Several of the year’s most intriguing auction lots emerged out of the blue, little known to experts until the sales were announced and then in some cases canceled. The objects — which include Egyptian cosmetic pots, a Navajo code talker’s uniform, a suffragist’s letters and a teenage Civil War soldier’s memorabilia — are headed for public display by the institutions that snapped them up before they could be scattered. Here are some highlights:', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26ANTIQUES3/26ANTIQUES3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 799, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26ANTIQUES3/26ANTIQUES3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 799}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26ANTIQUES3/26ANTIQUES3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 769, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26ANTIQUES3/26ANTIQUES3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1538, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26ANTIQUES3/26ANTIQUES3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/26/arts/26ANTIQUES3/26ANTIQUES3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image