icers Wenjian Liu and Rafael Ramos were killed last week, law enforcement officials, clergy members and others gathered to pay respects to them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They stood, in tears, or crouched in silence, or stared solemnly on a Brooklyn street corner before a pile of bouquets, Christmas wreaths, teddy bears and candles snuffed out by the December wind. One after another — police officers, detectives, sergeants and lieutenants — arrived to pay respects to two of their own who were ambushed and killed last week at that spot, now a shrine to the fallen men.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/nyregion/VIGIL-web/VIGIL-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/26/nyregion/VIGIL-web/VIGIL-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/nyregion/VIGIL-web/VIGIL-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/nyregion/VIGIL-web/VIGIL-web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/nyregion/VIGIL-web/VIGIL-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/26/nyregion/VIGIL-web/VIGIL-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/nyregion/VIGIL-web/VIGIL-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prayers and Tears at Vigil for Slain Officers ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At a Vigil for Two Slain Officers, Paying Respects and Praying for the Police', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liu, Wenjian (1982-2014)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nir, Sarah Maslin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ramos, Rafael (1974-2014)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Attacks on Police', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bedford-Stuyvesant (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T00:19:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Maslin Nir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7a5ebb4-e68c-5402-9bc7-f24d4e9428a6', 'word_count': 604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7a5ebb4-e68c-5402-9bc7-f24d4e9428a6'}, {'abstract': 'When most of the cases accepted by the Supreme Court are argued by elite litigators, who work mainly for corporate clients, all Americans suffer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/26/opinion/the-best-lawyers-money-can-buy.html', 'snippet': 'When most of the cases accepted by the Supreme Court are argued by elite litigators, who work mainly for corporate clients, all Americans suffer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States Supreme Court decides cases involving the nation’s most pressing legal issues, affecting the daily lives of hundreds of millions of Americans — and yet so much about its functioning is shrouded in mystique and exclusivity. The court’s front doors are locked and its vast “public” plaza is off-limits to protesters. Alone among the branches of government, it refuses to televise its proceedings, even though its gallery can seat only 250 members of the public. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/opinion/26FRIDAY2/26FRIDAY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/26/opinion/26FRIDAY2/26FRIDAY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/opinion/26FRIDAY2/26FRIDAY2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/opinion/26FRIDAY2/26FRIDAY2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/opinion/26FRIDAY2/26FRIDAY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/26/opinion/26FRIDAY2/26FRIDAY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/26/opinion/26FRIDAY2/26FRIDAY2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Best Lawyers Money Can Buy', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Best Lawyers Money Can Buy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Reuters Group PLC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legal Profession', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T00:40:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/88c95282-987a-5513-ba3a-314ee26de775', 'word_count': 678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88c95282-987a-5513-ba3a-314ee26de775'}, {'abstract': 'Social media companies are unlikely to uphold liberal values abroad if doing so would harm their lucrative business opportunities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/26/opinion/facebook-is-not-the-public-square.html', 'snippet': 'Social media companies are unlikely to uphold liberal values abroad if doing so would harm their lucrative business opportunities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dissidents in Russia and elsewhere are upset that Facebook has blocked an event page for a demonstration to support a leading opposition figure at the behest of the government of President Vladimir Putin. While their outrage is understand