y', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holidays and Special Occasions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T05:08:20+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/6edaa8b5-652a-5220-8cce-111f5348a1e3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/6edaa8b5-652a-5220-8cce-111f5348a1e3'}, {'abstract': 'A 51-year-old woman waiting for a bus on Boston Road was punched and raped by a man who approached her and tried to talk to her, the authorities said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/26/nyregion/police-seek-rapist-in-bronx-attack-on-christmas-morning-.html', 'snippet': 'A 51-year-old woman waiting for a bus on Boston Road was punched and raped by a man who approached her and tried to talk to her, the authorities said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A man punched and raped a 51-year-old woman in the Bronx early on Christmas morning, the police said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Police Seek Rapist in Bronx Attack on Christmas Morning   ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T05:33:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e895fc2-1cb9-5008-9b03-f2e4e097c77d', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e895fc2-1cb9-5008-9b03-f2e4e097c77d'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/26/arts/television/whats-on-tv-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '1 P.M. (TMC) FRUITVALE STATION (2013) In the small hours of New Year’s Day, 2009, an unarmed young black man named Oscar Grant III, handcuffed and lying on his belly, was shot and killed by a white police officer in the Fruitvale subway station in Oakland, Calif. The director Ryan Coogler’s first feature film, which won a Grand Jury Prize at Sundance, carefully reconstructs the last 24 hours of Mr. Grant’s life. Michael B. Jordan (“Friday Night Lights,” “The Wire”) gives Mr. Grant “a fully human presence,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times, “acknowledging his complexities and contradictions.” The film is steady, subtle and seldom moralistic, Mr. Scott wrote, which is what gives it its power. “The climactic encounter with BART police officers erupts in a mood of vertiginous uncertainty, defusing facile or inflammatory judgments and bending the audience’s reflexive emotional horror and moral outrage toward a necessary and difficult ethical inquiry. How could this have happened? How did we — meaning any one of us who might see faces like our own depicted on that screen — allow it?”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s on TV Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T06:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brandon K. Thorp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brandon', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Thorp', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf187ec2-30b5-50d6-938b-569def0e3b2a', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf187ec2-30b5-50d6-938b-569def0e3b2a'}, {'abstract': 'Ken Schles, who photographed a Manhattan neighborhood in the mid-80s, edited the work into two books, one in 1988 and one this year, whose tone is strikingly different.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/28/nyregion/the-east-village-in-the-1980s-and-looking-back.html', 'snippet': 'Ken Schles, who photographed a Manhattan neighborhood in the mid-80s, edited the work into two books, one in 1988 and one this year, whose tone is strikingly different.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A byword for New Year’s Eve parties: How much fun you have often depends on where you’re standing. Ken Schles, 54, spent the mid-1980s living and taking photographs in the East Village, and twice he edited his work into books — the first time when the photos were taken, and the second time more recently. The differences in mood are striking. The first book, “Invisible City” (1988), which Mr. Schles created while living on a drug-ravaged block on Avenue B, reflects the turmoil and death in his life at the time. “My friends were dying of AIDS, and I was living in an abandoned building,” he said. A quarter of a century later, when he went back to the work, it was as someone who had survived the era, and the images revealed themselves to him in a different light. The resulting book, “Night Walk” (2014), is the retrospective glance of a father of two living in Fort Greene, in Brooklyn. It is “much more about the people and the vitality,” he said. “There’s an excitement about going out. In ‘Invisible City,’ there’s a darkness to the book.”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The East Village, in the 1980s and Looking Back', 'kicker': 'Album', 'content_kicker': N