[{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc3a1902-4080-5f72-836e-34bfcabd6730', 'word_count': 390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc3a1902-4080-5f72-836e-34bfcabd6730'}, {'abstract': 'From the time our boys were babies, we were so desperate for a few hours of respite that we turned our house into a library with a nasty librarian. Now they can’t sleep without silence — and I know how well that ends.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/26/training-children-in-insomnia-and-embracing-the-enemy/', 'snippet': 'From the time our boys were babies, we were so desperate for a few hours of respite that we turned our house into a library with a nasty librarian. Now they can’t sleep without silence — and I know how well that ends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A parent’s regrets are many, and the insomniac plains of the night a fertile ground for their sprouting, but no remorse is more apposite to the long hours before dawn than that of having passed sleep problems on to one’s children. I generally have no problem falling asleep; the 6:30 alarm on school mornings takes care of that. But lately, sometime between 3:30 a.m. and 4:30 a.m. every night, I find myself conscious. I know the alarm is poised to cut off any relief at the other end, so I must get back to sleep, yet I can’t. My wife has been experiencing lacunas in her slumber as well, and often one’s will exacerbate the other’s. I didn’t think she had my sleep problem, but I guess she does now. I don’t know if she thinks I gave it to her; I think she does. I may have. I know she thinks I gave it to the kids. And so another roadblock on my path back to senselessness.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/19/us/motherlode-slee