pg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 443, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28PAUL/28PAUL-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 886, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28PAUL/28PAUL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28PAUL/28PAUL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28PAUL/28PAUL-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Me, Myself, and Us,’ by Brian R. Little', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Do You Think You Are?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Annie Murphy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Little, Brian R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T17:51:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Murphy Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': 'Murphy', 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f240071-af6b-5ac4-ba6b-8c3bde6f25db', 'word_count': 879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f240071-af6b-5ac4-ba6b-8c3bde6f25db'}, {'abstract': 'From dawn to dusk, a social historian sees for herself the intricacies of life in Victorian England.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/28/books/review/how-to-be-a-victorian-by-ruth-goodman.html', 'snippet': 'From dawn to dusk, a social historian sees for herself the intricacies of life in Victorian England.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s the sexiest era? The Roaring Twenties? Hardly. The late-20th-century free-love movement? Not even close. The Victorian era is, unquestionably, the sexiest. What’s hotter than anxiety and repression? Or more titillating than the chasm between public and private behavior, between the pedestal-placing of the wife/mother and the enormous rise in prostitution? It’s no coincidence that one of the most erotic characters in literature, Bram Stoker’s Dracula, appeared in 1897, toward the end of Queen Victoria’s reign. (Remember: He had to leave Transylvania and come to England to feast upon pure blood, and open all those untouched . . . necks.) Even the thought of the armorlike clothing of the times coupled with the frilly nothings underneath makes me swoon. There’s a reason the most ubiquitous lingerie shop in America is called Victoria’s Secret and not, say, Richard Nixon’s Secret.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28NEWMAN/28newman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28NEWMAN/28newman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 574}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28NEWMAN/28newman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 578, 'width': 604, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28NEWMAN/28newman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 578, 'width': 604, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28NEWMAN/28NEWMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28NEWMAN/28NEWMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28NEWMAN/28NEWMAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘How to Be a Victorian,’ by Ruth Goodman', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Mile in Her Corset', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Newman, Judith', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodman, Ruth (1963- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'How to Be a Victorian (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T17:51:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f480f33f-913d-5d09-999f-09a9ec2d1be4', 'word_count': 1237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f480f33f-913d-5d09-999f-09a9ec2d1be4'}, {'abstract': 'Steven Johnson explores the ramifications, good and bad, of six technologies that have changed the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/28/books/review/how-we-got-to-now-by-steven-johnson.html', 'snippet': 'Steven Johnson explores the ramifications, good and bad, of six technologies that have changed the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By the time you reach the end of Steven Johnson’s latest book on innovation, you’re apt to find yourself exhilarated and a little bit fatigued. It’s a case of literary jet lag. You’ve been with Johnson to secret chambers within the pyramids of Giza and foul trenches in the sewers of old Chicago; you’ve looked through telescopes on Mauna Kea and peered into microscopes with 19th-century bacteriologists. You’ve had to move around space and time to do this, but more crucially, you’ve had to wander back and forth through passageways that connect technological history with politics, economics and culture. Have you ever wondered why flash photography led to antipoverty programs at the turn of the 20th century? Or how the invention of the laser contributed to the decline of mom-and-pop stores? Of course you haven’t, because you didn’t really stop to think — wait, check that; I didn’t really stop to think — how the invention of flash photography finally allowed Jacob Riis to capture the images of dismal tenement life on New York’s Lower East Side that he had already been writing about, with little impact, for years. Or how the laser begat the bar code that, in turn, gave an efficiency advantage to stores like Target and Walmart. “How We Got to Now” is full of nifty connections like these — stories that illustrate obscure chains of causality that shaped the modern world. We’ve gone from making cave paintings to using slender slabs of powerful technology like the iPhone 6. But it’s hardly been a linear path. Johnson shows the meandering process by which we’ve made this journey, and what sort of cultural waves ripple outward as we move ahead. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28GERTNERSUB1/1228-bks-GERTNER-sub-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28GERTNERSUB1/1228-bks-GERTNER-sub-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 512}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28GERTNERSUB1/1228-bks-GERTNER-sub-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 874, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28GERTNERSUB1/1228-bks-GERTNER-sub-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1337, 'width': 1567, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28GERTNERSUB1/28GERTNERSUB1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28GERTNERSUB1/28GERTNERSUB1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28GERTNERSUB1/28GERTNERSUB1-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘How We Got to Now,’ by Steven Johnson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unforeseeable Consequences', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gertner, Jon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Steven Berlin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T17:51:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Gertner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gertner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd80db2e-1d65-55dc-b80a-6c1ff2c4404f', 'word_count': 1323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd80db2e-1d65-55dc-b80a-6c1ff2c4404f'}, {'abstract': 'A playwright turned statesman, Vaclav Havel came to embody the soul of post-Communist Czechoslovakia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/28/books/review/havel-a-life-by-michael-zantovsky.html', 'snippet': 'A playwright turned statesman, Vaclav Havel came to embody the soul of post-Communist Czechoslovakia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In February 1990, Vaclav Havel, recently elected the first president of post-\xadCommunist Czechoslovakia, came to Washington. “Consciousness precedes being, and not the other way around, as Marxists claim,” he told a joint meeting of Congress. Michael Zantovsky, then Havel’s press secretary (and now the Czech ambassador to Britain), recalls that after Havel’s speech many of the senators and representatives wanted to know what he meant. But there was no time to explain. Rock ‘n’ roll was calling, and Havel was headed to New York, to a club in the East Village.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SHORE/28shore-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SHORE/28shore-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SHORE/28shore-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SHORE/28shore-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1403, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SHORE/28shore-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SHORE/28shore-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SHORE/28shore-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Havel: A Life,’ by Michael Zantovsky', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disturbing the Peace', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shore, Marci', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zantovsky, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Havel, Vaclav', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T17:51:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marci Shore', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marci', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d256bb18-4e98-5bcb-82aa-df3be4c6e8cd', 'word_count': 1274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d256bb18-4e98-5bcb-82aa-df3be4c6e8cd'}, {'abstract': 'For the voyager Adam Nicolson, Homer is impossible to understand while sitting behind a desk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/28/books/review/why-homer-matters-by-adam-nicolson.html', 'snippet': 'For the voyager Adam Nicolson, Homer is impossible to understand while sitting behind a desk.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Homer has become a kind of scripture for me, an ancient book, full of urgent imperatives and ancient meanings, most of them half discerned, to be puzzled over. It is a source of wisdom.” So begins the third chapter of Adam Nicolson’s highly accessible new book, “Why Homer Matters,” in which he compares his relationship with epic poetry to a form of possession, a “colonization of the mind by an imaginative presence from the past.” The world needs more Adam Nicolsons, unabashedly passionate evangelists for the power of ancient poetry to connect us with our collective past, illuminate our personal struggles and interrogate our understanding of human history.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28DOERRIES/28DOERRIES-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 617, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28DOERRIES/28DOERRIES-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 617}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28DOERRIES/28DOERRIES-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 997, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28DOERRIES/28DOERRIES-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1993, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28DOERRIES/DOERRIES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28DOERRIES/DOERRIES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28DOERRIES/DOERRIES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Why Homer Matters,’ by Adam Nicolson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Songs of the Sirens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doerries, Bryan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nicolson, Adam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Homer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T17:51:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bryan Doerries', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bryan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doerries', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6efc703d-0d76-5f99-a8b7-0fe02913a01f', 'word_count': 1210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6efc703d-0d76-5f99-a8b7-0fe02913a01f'}, {'abstract': 'In the 1960s, a middle-class urbanite leaves Calcutta to join the Maoist rebellion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/28/books/review/the-lives-of-others-by-neel-mukherjee.html', 'snippet': 'In the 1960s, a middle-class urbanite leaves Calcutta to join the Maoist rebellion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Technological innovation and economic development have brought great wealth to a few parts of India, but they have further marginalized the country’s adivasis, or tribals, the aboriginal communities of forest dwellers and farmers who live outside of mainstream society. These groups were systematically victimized under colonial rule, when the British Raj passed legislation that classified them as criminals. The British-Indian government subjected adivasis to police brutality, herded them into reformatory settlements and forced them to perform hard labor.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SAWHNEY/28sawhney-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SAWHNEY/28sawhney-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SAWHNEY/28sawhney-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1023, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SAWHNEY/28sawhney-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1869, 'width': 1867, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/books/review/28SAWHNEY/28SAWHNEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 't