ciating gifts (no matter what they are), impolite party guests, thank-you notes and an automobile mishap.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am a 15-year-old girl with a problem that’s going to make me sound super-spoiled. I get excited for the holidays, especially (and selfishly) about the gifts I’m going to get. But every year, I end up bummed out. Like: “Really, Mom? A bracelet for a 10-year-old?” It happened again this year. I know the holidays aren’t about gifts, but I can’t help feeling as if the people who get me presents don’t even know me. Any advice?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Count on the Thought', 'kicker': 'Social Q’s', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Count on the Thought', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gifts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T20:21:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e14874ef-c368-52f0-bee0-f840345b2c8d', 'word_count': 864, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e14874ef-c368-52f0-bee0-f840345b2c8d'}, {'abstract': 'Lifestyle modification prevents Type 2 diabetes even more effectively in the elderly than in middle-aged patients.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/26/diabetes-prevention-that-works/', 'snippet': 'Lifestyle modification prevents Type 2 diabetes even more effectively in the elderly than in middle-aged patients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What happens in Room 6 at the Y.M.C.A. in Wayne, N.J., on Wednesday mornings doesn’t look terribly dramatic. About a dozen people trickle in, step on a digital scale, show the program leader Elizabeth Youngstein their logs — on iPads or smartphones or in paper notebooks — recording what they ate since last week and how much they exercised. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Diabetes Prevention That Works', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diabetes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Preventive Medicine', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Young Men's Christian Assn (YMCA)", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T20:22:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25aaf387-71d5-555d-818d-7b3424572c24', 'word_count': 998, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25aaf387-71d5-555d-818d-7b3424572c24'}, {'abstract': 'A fashion editor and a principal find their destinies are intertwined.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/28/fashion/weddings/a-relationship-with-style-and-substance.html', 'snippet': 'A fashion editor and a principal find their destinies are intertwined.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brandon Cardet-Hernandez, a Cuban-American who grew up in Orlando, Fla., didn’t know the Yiddish term for soul mate (bashert) when he bumped into Ariel Foxman on the uptown A train in March 2012. But it’s clear now that that one subway trip was an express to destiny.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Relationship With Style and Substance', 'kicker': 'Brandon Cardet-Hernandez and Ariel Foxman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Relationship With Style and Substance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vows (Times Column)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foxman, Ariel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cardet-Hernandez, Brandon', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T20:22:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bob Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c6a2d40-c6de-5656-8f0f-f942983258e9', 'word_count': 1558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c6a2d40-c6de-5656-8f0f-f942983258e9'}, {'abstract': 'I do not think that word means etc.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/26/structural-confusion/', 'snippet': 'I do not think that word means etc.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Economists use a lot of jargon, and rightly so. When an economist refers to comparative advantage, or total factor productivity, or the neutrality of money, etc., she or he is using that phrase to refer to a concept developed over decades of discussion and debate; trying to spell that out in plain English every time you invoke the concept would be a huge waste of time, and would introduce much potential for confusion too.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Structural Confusion', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-26T20:23:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a624af1-e6e9-5b6b-8dc7-97901b82dd8c', 'word_count': 401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a624af1-e6e9-5b6b-8dc7-97901b82dd8c'}, {'abstract': 'One particular change in relationship status remains a difficult subject to raise online.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/28/fashion/facebook-last-taboo-the-unhappy-marriage.html', 'snippet': 'One particular change in relationship status remains a difficult subject to raise online.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the end of July, Michael Ellsberg posted something truly subversive on Facebook. It wasn’t a scantily clad photograph of himself. Instead, to his 25,000 or so followers he wrote: “Jena and I are no longer married. This has been a heart-wrenching process for both of us, over the past year, and we are thankful for the support of our friends, family and community in helping us through this. We are on very good terms.” (His former wife, Jena la Flamme, posted the same message on her wall.)', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/fashion/28JPFACEBOOKSDIVORCESUB01/28JPFACEBOOKSDIVORCESub01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 532, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/28/fashion/28JPFACEBOOKSDIVORCESUB01/28JPFACEBOOKSDIVORCESub01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 532}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/28/fashion/28JPFACEBOOKSDIVORCESUB01/28JPFACEBOOKSDIVORCESub01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 908, 'width': 1024, 's