lture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ecccaff-31ef-59ed-b394-589046ea2174', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ecccaff-31ef-59ed-b394-589046ea2174'}, {'abstract': 'In his solo show, the comedian Brad Zimmerman draws on his long climb to the stage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/29/theater/my-son-the-waiter-a-jewish-tragedy-brad-zimmermans-show.html', 'snippet': 'In his solo show, the comedian Brad Zimmerman draws on his long climb to the stage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brad Zimmerman may well have brought many of us a salad or a mac and cheese once. Now, in “My Son the Waiter: A Jewish Tragedy,” he brings us the story of his failure, and it’s pretty delicious. As Mr. Zimmerman admits, a typical proud mom might brag about her son vacationing in the Caribbean or buying a big, new house; his mother has to tell her friends, “If all goes well, Brad is buying a bookcase.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/29/arts/MYSON/MYSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/29/arts/MYSON/MYSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/29/arts/MYSON/MYSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/29/arts/MYSON/MYSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1381, 'width':