: 'Publicly funded museums that seek to sell important work will face tougher sanctions from the Museums Association in Britain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Publicly funded museums that seek to sell important work will face tougher sanctions from the Museums Association in Britain, The Independent reported. The move comes after the Northampton Borough Council decided to sell an ancient Egyptian statue owned by the Northampton Museum and Art Gallery. After that sale, through Christie’s in July, the association barred the Northampton council from its membership, and Arts Council England revoked the accreditations of the Northampton museum as well as the Abington Park Museum, also run by the borough council, for five years. The Museums Association is further tightening its ethics code to help stem sell-offs of valuable antiquities from collections run by financially struggling institutions, and it is in talks with Arts Council England, the Heritage Lottery Fund and the Art Fund to establish a joint response to such sales. The Museums Association’s director, Sharon Heal, said the association was not opposed to sales “if museums act in an ethical way in respect to the public, the collection and the museum.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'British Group Toughens Its Code for Art Sales', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'British Group Toughens Its Code for Art Sales', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museums Assn (Great Britain)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-29T22:27:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fa8692c-e1d2-5882-9545-59a40dc1ce45', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fa8692c-e1d2-5882-9545-59a40dc1ce45'}, {'abstract': 'Jean-Pierre and Luc Dardenne explain the genesis of “Two Days, One Night,” which did not make the Academy’s shortlist of foreign films.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/30/movies/the-dardenne-brothers-discuss-two-days-one-night.html', 'snippet': 'Jean-Pierre and Luc Dardenne explain the genesis of “Two Days, One Night,” which did not make the Academy’s shortlist of foreign films.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Another Oscar season, another snub for the Dardenne brothers. Their “Two Days, One Night,” Belgium’s submission for the Academy Award for best foreign-language film, won various festival and critics’ awards, as well as a European Film Award this month for Marion Cotillard’s taut performance as a factory worker whose job is in jeopardy. But the drama did not make the cut for the Oscar shortlist — the fourth time that the Dardennes, two of the most acclaimed European filmmakers, have been passed over by Hollywood.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/jpdardenne/jpdardenne-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/jpdardenne/jpdardenne-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/jpdardenne/jpdardenne-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/jpdardenne/jpdardenne-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/jpdardenne/jpdardenne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/jpdardenne/jpdardenne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/jpdardenne/jpdardenne-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Respect and Awards, but Still No Oscar', 'kicker': 'A Word With: Jean-pierre and Luc Dardenne', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Respect and Awards, but Still No Oscar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dardenne, Jean-Pierre', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dardenne, Luc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotillard, Marion', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Two Days, One Night (Movie)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-29T22:39:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/3343354d-8346-5287-9806-f28d871a0762', 'word_count': 1027, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3343354d-8346-5287-9806-f28d871a0762'}, {'abstract': '“Burning Bluebeard,” a play set around a 1903 theater fire, has become a tradition in Chicago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/30/theater/burning-bluebeard-becomes-a-holiday-tradition-in-chicago.html', 'snippet': '“Burning Bluebeard,” a play set around a 1903 theater fire, has become a tradition in Chicago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO — As holiday traditions go, the play “Burning Bluebeard” doesn’t exactly seem like a natural: no Cratchits, no elves, no Grinches, no sugarplum fairies. Its place in the season is all about timing. It’s based on the real-life story of a fatal fire that happened in the week between Christmas and New Year’s Day — and it’s told by clowns.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/BLUEBEARD/BLUEBEARD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 714, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/BLUEBEARD/BLUEBEARD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 714}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/BLUEBEARD/BLUEBEARD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 622, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/BLUEBEARD/BLUEBEARD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 622, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/30/arts/BLUEBEARD/BLUEBEARD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2