or': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-30T04:54:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8e302ea-4ab2-5a71-9e56-697a3952e6dc', 'word_count': 534, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8e302ea-4ab2-5a71-9e56-697a3952e6dc'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/30/arts/television/whats-on-tv-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '3 P.M. (FXM) SUPER 8 (2011) Steven Spielberg co-produced this film, but that’s not the only service he rendered. He also seems to be its inspiration: Since its release, “Super 8” has drawn usually unfavorable comparisons to “Close Encounters of the Third Kind,” and especially to “E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial.” “Still,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times, " ‘not as good as E.T.’ is not so bad.” The film, directed by the Spielberg admirer J. J. Abrams, is about a group of young teenagers (including Gabriel Basso, Ryan Lee, Joel Courtney and Riley Griffiths) in 1979 who are filming a low-budget zombie movie when they witness a violent train derailment. They can’t see much in the chaos of the crash, but it’s possible that their camera saw more. While they wait for the film to be developed, strange things begin happening in their town. Dogs and people disappear, electronics go missing, and the streets fill with soldiers. Mr. Abrams, Mr. Scott wrote, captures his protagonists and their era “with a sense of period detail that sits right on the line between uncanny and neurotic.” He added: “His 1979 is more like 1979 than the real 1979, which hardly seemed like a time of innocence and eager wonder. But no time ever does, except in retrospect.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Tuesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Tuesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-12-30T05:31:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brandon K. Thorp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brandon', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Thorp', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b4c85ce-b04a-5965-927b-9a08b529e55e', 'word_count': 731, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b4c85ce-b04a-5965-927b-9a08b529e55e'}, {'abstract': 'In states like North Carolina and Georgia, the black share of registered voters remains low among those who reached voting age before 1965.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/30/upshot/evidence-that-the-jim-crow-era-endures-for-older-black-voters-in-the-south-.html', 'snippet': 'In states like North Carolina and Georgia, the black share of registered voters remains low among those who reached voting age before 1965.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Democrats have lamented low black turnout for decades, but black turnout today rivals that of white voters. That’s probably in part because of President Obama, but also because of generational replacement.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/24/upshot/24UP-Jim-Crow/24UP-Jim-Crow-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/24/upshot/24UP-Jim-Crow/24UP-Jim-Crow-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xl