den and Carol Ann Duffy are among the Britons who may be celebrating the arrival of 2015 a bit early, having been named to Queen Elizabeth II’s annual new year honors list, the British government has announced. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Honors From the Queen for Joan Collins and John Hurt', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Honors From the Queen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Asher, Peter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Joan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Corden, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duffy, Carol Ann', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Elizabeth II, Queen of Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hurt, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott Thomas, Kristin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watson, Emily', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-31T16:16:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba87cc05-0d5e-53d5-8fe9-113749d8ba98', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba87cc05-0d5e-53d5-8fe9-113749d8ba98'}, {'abstract': 'A Harvard historian shows how every stage of the industrialization of cotton rested on violence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/empire-of-cotton-by-sven-beckert.html', 'snippet': 'A Harvard historian shows how every stage of the industrialization of cotton rested on violence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The history of an era often seems defined by a particular commodity. The 18th century certainly belonged to sugar. The race to cultivate it in the West Indies was, in the words of the French Enlightenment writer Guillaume-Thomas de Raynal, “the principal cause of the rapid movement which stirs the Universe.” In the 20th century and beyond, the commodity has been oil: determining events from the Allied partitioning of the Middle East after World War I to Hitler’s drive for Balkan and Caspian wells to the forging of our own fateful ties to the regimes of the Persian Gulf. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HOCHSCHILD/04hochschild-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 319, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HOCHSCHILD/04hochschild-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 319}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HOCHSCHILD/04hochschild-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 545, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HOCHSCHILD/04hochschild-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1090, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HOCHSCHILD/04HOCHSCHILD-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HOCHSCHILD/04HOCHSCHILD-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HOCHSCHILD/04HOCHSCHILD-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Empire of Cotton,’ by Sven Beckert', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dyed in Blood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hochschild, Adam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beckert, Sven', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cotton', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-31T16:16:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Hochschild', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hochschild', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cd66fbc-7f63-538e-b286-b7806635d580', 'word_count': 1171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cd66fbc-7f63-538e-b286-b7806635d580'}, {'abstract': 'Carrie Gibson makes a case for the centrality of the Caribbean through 500 years of exploitative history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/empires-crossroads-by-carrie-gibson.html', 'snippet': 'Carrie Gibson makes a case for the centrality of the Caribbean through 500 years of exploitative history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To get from the airport on the former British West Indian colony of Dominica to the capital, Roseau, the birthplace of the novelist Jean Rhys, one has to travel through narrow winding roads with a sheer drop to the sea on one side and impenetrable forest on the other. Halfway along this road one is suddenly flung backward in time, seeing faces that have a startling resemblance to the indigenous people of the pre-Columbian era. They are the Kalinago people, or Caribs, as the Europeans called them. Carrie Gibson’s readable book, “Empire’s Crossroads: A History of the Caribbean From Columbus to the Present Day,” tells of the Europeans’ first encounter with these and other Amerindians, and she explores the lingering impact of colonization on the island territories today. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04NUNEZ/04NUNEZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 463, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04NUNEZ/04NUNEZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 463}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04NUNEZ/04NUNEZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04NUNEZ/04NUNEZ-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1580, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04NUNEZ/04NUNEZ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04NUNEZ/04NUNEZ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04NUNEZ/04NUNEZ-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'hei