ifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f6defd0-1195-57b4-8cce-62cbf7649df3', 'word_count': 1024, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f6defd0-1195-57b4-8cce-62cbf7649df3'}, {'abstract': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/12/31/classical-playlist-tchaikovsky-faur-bach-and-more/', 'snippet': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TCHAIKOVSKY: ‘The Nutcracker’Valery Gergiev, conductor, Kirov Orchestra(Philips 289 462 114-2; one CD)Tchaikovsky’s score for “The Nutcracker” is probably taken for granted. I was recently reminded of how astonishing this music is — full of nuance and intelligence as well as charm and color — when I went to the New York City Ballet’s production for the first time in many years. Afterward, I could hardly stop listening to the outstanding 1998 recording of the complete score Valery Gergiev made for Philips with what was then still being called the Kirov Orchestra (now the Mariinsky). This conductor and these players can rightly claim a birthright to “The Nutcracker,” first performed at the Mariinsky Theater in St. Petersburg in 1892. (Anthony Tommasini)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/01/arts/artsbeat-classical/artsbeat-classical-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/01/arts/artsbeat-classical/artsbeat-classical-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/01/arts/artsbeat-classical/artsbeat-classical-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'lega