ame': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32dd618d-3098-5f7a-b982-297f1c5d6489', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32dd618d-3098-5f7a-b982-297f1c5d6489'}, {'abstract': 'Benedict Cumberbatch, the unlikely heartthrob who plays Alan Turing in “The Imitation Game,” has worked his way to stardom playing quirky characters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/movies/awardsseason/benedict-cumberbatch-acts-clever-again-in-the-imitation-game.html', 'snippet': 'Benedict Cumberbatch, the unlikely heartthrob who plays Alan Turing in “The Imitation Game,” has worked his way to stardom playing quirky characters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With his wide forehead and high cheekbones, Benedict Cumberbatch has been compared, and not unreasonably (especially if you’ve seen him in “August: Osage County”), both to an otter and to Sid the Sloth from the animated movie “Ice Age.” He also has one of those rumbling, mellifluous British voices that are capable of revving up and whipping out dialogue, perfectly articulated, at breakneck speed. He can talk faster than most people think. Lately, all these gifts — the big brainpan, the quick tongue, the upper-class diction — have allowed him to make a reputation playing characters who are very bright but not quite normal.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/arts/04MCGRATH1/04MCGRATH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 693, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/arts/04MCGRATH1/04MCGRATH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 693}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'ima