': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Recycling Electronic Waste Responsibly: Excuses Dwindle', 'kicker': 'Machine Learning', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Recycling Tech Waste Responsibly: Excuses Dwindle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Best BUY Company Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dell Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gazelle Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Environment Program', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'eBay Inc', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco Bay Area (Calif)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'GreenCitizen', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-12-31T23:48:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Wood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc9554c0-2d23-5f85-acd9-ad3c130bd9fc', 'word_count': 1083, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc9554c0-2d23-5f85-acd9-ad3c130bd9fc'}, {'abstract': '“Collected,” by Fritz Karch and Rebecca Robertson, breaks down the many varieties of acquisitive types.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/01/garden/an-assortment-of-hoarders.html', 'snippet': '“Collected,” by Fritz Karch and Rebecca Robertson, breaks down the many varieties of acquisitive types.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Used tea bags, peach-pit monkeys, vintage folding travel hangers, candy-colored knitting needles. Oh, and blown-glass food domes, paper clips and sailor’s valentines, Bakelite, vintage Pyrex and matte black Wedgwood basalt teapots. These are just a few of the delectable multiples collected in “Collected: Living With the Things You Love,” by Fritz Karch and Rebecca Robertson, out last November from Abrams ($40). Mr. Karch and Ms. Robertson, former editors at Martha Stewart Living, have formulated a taxonomy of the collector’s mind and extrapolated 15 types, from the Modest-ist (bottlecaps, twine and so forth) to the Containerist (aluminum suitcases, vintage tea canisters), the Zoologist (pom-pom creatures made by Steif, majolica crustaceans) to the Fabulist (swizzle sticks, vintage Lily Pulitzer blazers). Which are you? Information: 212-206-7715 or abramsbooks.com. \xad', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/01/garden/01CURR_BOOKS/01CURR_BOOKS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 774, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/01/garden/01CURR_BOOKS/01CURR_BOOKS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 774}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/01/garden/01CURR_BOOKS/01CURR_BOOKS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 794, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/01/garden/01CURR_BOOKS/01CURR_BOOKS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1588, 'subType': 'su